IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `family-domain`

§ `Guardianship of Infants Act 1934`

-- ===========================================================================
-- GUARDIANSHIP OF INFANTS ACT 1934 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition.
--
-- SOURCE. source/GIA1934.txt in this directory, a `pdftotext -layout`
-- extraction of the official SSO PDF. Its own front matter says:
--   "2020 REVISED EDITION"
--   "This revised edition incorporates all amendments up to and including
--    1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021."
-- and every page carries the watermark line
--   "Informal Consolidation – version in force from 2/1/2025".
-- Every quotation below is copied mechanically out of that file, with the
-- running headers, page numbers and watermark lines discarded and
-- line-wrapping normalised to single spaces. The source uses curly quotes;
-- they are reproduced as they appear.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS. The determinate part of the Act, and a register of
-- the rest. This is the smallest-determinate-fraction Act of the four. It
-- has 23 sections (1 to 21, plus 5A and 11A), and they divide four ways.
--
-- FIVE are wholly discretionary and are wholly unencoded -- ss 5, 12, 13, 14
-- and 19 -- qualified variously by "as it may think fit" (s 5), "as the court
-- thinks fit" (s 13), "the court may direct" (s 12) and "in his sole and
-- uncontrolled discretion" (s 19(1)); s 14 says "the court may" with no
-- qualifier at all.
--
-- A SIXTH, s 11A, is wholly unencoded for a different reason: its content is
-- weight ("have regard to the advice of a person"), its duty is conditioned
-- on a judgement ("whenever it is practicable"), and its closing words
-- withhold a duty rather than imposing one ("shall not be bound to follow
-- such advice").
--
-- FIVE are encoded entire, nothing being left out of them: ss 1, 2, 4, 17
-- and 21.
--
-- The remaining TWELVE -- ss 3, 5A, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 15, 16, 18 and 20 --
-- are part rule and part something this module does not encode, and each is
-- encoded as far as the rule goes and no further. A discretion has no
-- determinate output and a boolean standing in for one is a lie, so what was
-- left out of each is NAMED with its reason rather than silently dropped --
-- see `Sections not encoded, and why` at the foot of this file, and the
-- per-section notes in place.
--
-- (An earlier version of this paragraph said the whole operative content of
-- ss 5, 8, 9(1), 10, 12, 13, 14 and 19 was a discretion. That was wrong of
-- three of the eight: s 8's gate on the application, s 10's effect, and
-- s 9(1)'s gate and its two "shall be" consequences are all encoded below.
-- The error mattered most for s 9(1), because a reader taking the header at
-- its word would never have gone looking for what that subsection contains --
-- and for a while nobody did.)
--
-- WHAT IS DETERMINATE, and is encoded here:
--   s 1       the short title;
--   s 2       the definition of "court";
--   s 3       the applicability gate, and the neutrality rule between the
--             father and the mother (the paramountcy direction itself is
--             not decidable and is named as such);
--   s 4       the mother's parity of standing to apply;
--   s 5A      the s 5A(1) gate, the s 5A(3)(e) ceilings, the s 5A(4)
--             ceiling, the s 5A(5)(b) no-fresh-application rule, the
--             s 5A(6) exclusive election, and the s 5A(8) exception;
--   s 6       who is guardian on the death of a parent, and the gate on the
--             court's power to appoint a further one;
--   s 7       the composition of testamentary, parental and court-appointed
--             guardianship -- the heart of the Act;
--   s 8       the gate on an application by joint guardians;
--   s 9(1)    the gate on the court's power to attach a pension or income,
--             and the two things the attachment order "shall be" once made
--             (the size of the attachment and the payees are discretionary);
--   s 10      the EFFECT of a removal (the power itself is discretionary);
--   s 11      WHICH matters the court must consider (not what weight);
--   s 15(1)   the duty to give security, and its dispensation;
--   s 16      the prohibition on dealing, its permission exception, its
--             one-year threshold, and the s 16(3) constraint on permitting;
--   s 17      the want of power to give a good discharge;
--   s 18      the $100-per-month cap, and the gate on resort to capital;
--   s 20      who may apply for the court's opinion;
--   s 21      the District Court exception.
--
-- AND TWO SECTIONS THAT BELONG TO NO ONE PROVISION:
--   `The Act as a whole` -- the infant every section above presupposes and
--     none of them re-tests, gated once, with the reason it is gated once
--     rather than threaded through; and an integrity check on a case file's
--     record of who is guardian of whom.
--   `Where this Act joins the other three` -- the four places this Act meets
--     wills-act.l4, intestate-succession-act.l4 and
--     probate-administration-act.l4, and which side of each owns what.
--
-- HOUSE STYLE. Inert: nothing is @export'ed. `GIVEN`, never `ASSUME`.
-- `BRANCH ... IF ... OTHERWISE` for multi-way choices. One decision per
-- provision, named in the provision's own words. Fixture constructors and
-- fixture values that build a type declared in `family-domain.l4` carry the
-- tag `GIA` immediately after their determiner -- `a GIA person`,
-- `a GIA infant`, `no GIA provision` -- so that the four Act modules can be
-- imported together; the statutory decisions are NOT tagged, because a reader
-- holding the Act must find the rule by the Act's own words.
--
-- THE ONTOLOGY. `family-domain.l4` supplies every noun this module shares
-- with the other three Acts: `Person`, `Vital status`, `Infant`, `Guardian`,
-- `How the guardian was appointed`, `Kind of guardianship`,
-- `Alone or jointly`, `Court`, `Money`, `A periodical sum`. Nothing there is
-- redeclared. What this module declares for itself is (a) the forum type,
-- because the GIA names two forums that are NOT "court" within s 2 and the
-- shared `Court` type deliberately excludes them, and (b) the shapes that
-- exist only inside one section of this Act: the s 3 question, the s 5A
-- breach, sentence and election, the s 7(4) order, the s 11 matters, the
-- s 18(1) cap verdict and the s 21 money.
--
-- "INFANT" HAS NO AGE IN THIS ACT. s 2 defines "court" and nothing else.
-- The word "infant" is used in every operative section and is defined
-- nowhere, and the Act nowhere fixes an age of majority: a search of
-- source/GIA1934.txt for the strings   years of age / majority / minor /
-- attain / 21 years / 18 years   returns nothing, and the only
-- QUANTIFIED durations anywhere in the Act are s 5A(3)(e)'s "12 months" of
-- imprisonment, s 16(1)(b)'s "one year" of lease and the "per month" of
-- s 18(1)'s cap. The Act does name other periods, but names none of them
-- with a number: "such weekly or other periodical sum" (s 7(4), s 8(b)) and
-- accounts passed "at such periods as may be ordered" (s 15(2)). An earlier
-- draft of this comment said the three quantified durations were the only
-- periods of time in the Act at all, which was wrong, and the module's own
-- s 18(1) arithmetic depends on the weekly one. None of them supplies an
-- age.
--
-- BE EXACT ABOUT THE SEARCH, because two earlier versions of this paragraph
-- and of ambiguity register entry 1 were not. (i) The Act DOES name an age,
-- exactly once, and it is not the age of majority: s 11(b), "where the infant
-- is of an age to express an independent opinion". That is a capacity and the
-- Act leaves it at large; it is carried as `Infant`'s
-- `of an age to express an independent opinion` and read by s 11. (ii) The
-- string "age of" DOES occur in source/GIA1934.txt, once, inside "the sale or
-- mortgage of any part of the infant’s property" in s 18(2) -- which is why
-- it has been struck from the list of strings above rather than left there to
-- be falsified by anyone who runs the grep. Neither correction touches the
-- conclusion: no age of MAJORITY is stated anywhere in the Act. So the age
-- comes from
-- outside the Act and is NOT guessed here: it is a PARAMETER. See
-- `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix` below.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 -- "infant", used in ss 3-21 and defined nowhere in the Act
-- Two routes to the same question, and both take the age from OUTSIDE.
--
-- The first reads `family-domain`'s already-construed input, `the date on
-- which the person attains the age of majority`, and asks whether the date
-- in question falls before it. NOTHING out means the majority date is not in
-- evidence -- NOT that the person is an infant.
--
-- The second computes that date from a date of birth and an age supplied by
-- the caller, so that a reader can see exactly where the number enters and
-- that this Act did not supply it. The year is incremented rather than days
-- added, which is leap-safe.
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `the date in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is an infant on the date in question` IS
    CONSIDER person's `the date on which the person attains the age of majority`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the date of majority` THEN
        JUST (Day `the date in question` LESS THAN Day `the date of majority`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 -- the Act fixes no age; the age is an input
GIVEN person IS A Person
      `the age of majority, in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `the date on which the person attains the age of majority, computed from the date of birth` IS
    CONSIDER person's `date of birth`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the date of birth` THEN
        JUST (Date (DATE_DAY   `the date of birth`)
                   (DATE_MONTH `the date of birth`)
                   (DATE_YEAR  `the date of birth` PLUS `the age of majority, in years`))

-- WHAT `NOTHING` MEANS HERE, AND WHAT THE NEIGHBOURING ACTS DO WITH THE SAME
-- SILENCE. Three of the four modules in this cleanroom ask "is this person
-- under age?", and all three resolve the not-in-evidence case differently.
-- Each has an argument; a reader who sees only one of them will take it for
-- the corpus's answer, so all three are set down here.
--
--   THIS ACT KEEPS IT AS A THIRD ANSWER -- `NOTHING`, distinct from
--     `JUST FALSE` -- and does not fold it, for two reasons that are this
--     Act's own. First, the Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 supplies no age
--     at all (ambiguity register entry 1), so there is no test here to fail:
--     folding to FALSE would report "not an infant" on the strength of a
--     number this Act does not contain. Second, this Act has no evidential
--     provision -- nothing in it answers to Probate and Administration Act
--     1934 s 6(3) -- so it names no forum to which the silence could be sent.
--     WHAT THE `NOTHING` BECOMES DOWNSTREAM is therefore neither of the two
--     folds but a named third outcome: see
--     `Whether this Act has an infant to govern` in
--     `The Act as a whole` below, whose third member says in words that the
--     question could not be determined. A consumer that needs a boolean must
--     choose its own fold, and this module refuses to choose one for it.
--   THE PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT folds `NOTHING` to FALSE, so its
--     s 21(1) bar does not bite on an unproved age. Its stated reason is
--     s 6(3) -- "The court in granting letters of administration may act on
--     such prima facie evidence, furnished by the applicant or any other
--     person, as to whether or not there is a minority or life interest, as
--     may be prescribed by rules made under any written law for the time
--     being in force relating to the courts." That Act routes the EVIDENTIAL
--     question to a forum; this one has no such provision.
--   THE WILLS ACT gives the silence a verdict of its own, on the ground that
--     formal validity must be affirmatively established, and computes the age
--     from `date of birth` rather than reading the shared field at all,
--     because its s 4 states a number -- "No will made by any person under 21
--     years of age shall be valid." -- that this Act does not.
--
-- (The Probate and Administration Act and Wills Act sentences above are
-- quoted from source/PAA1934.txt and source/WA1838.txt, not from this Act,
-- which is the only source this module encodes from.)
--
-- WHERE THIS ACT AND THE PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT DO AGREE: wherever
-- both give an answer they give the same one, boundary included, because
-- both compare the SAME shared field -- `family-domain`'s
-- `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` -- and both
-- compare it strictly. This module writes that comparison `LESS THAN`;
-- probate-administration-act.l4's `is an infant on` writes it `is before`.
-- On a majority date of 19 July 2033 both make him an infant on 18 July and
-- not an infant on the 19th. The boundary is asserted on both sides of it
-- below -- in this module; probate-administration-act.l4 pins its own.
-- They part only in the silent case, which is what this note is about.


§§ `Helpers -- facts the Act reads, in the shape the ontology stores them`

-- The Act's "if surviving", "is or are dead" and "so long as the mother or
-- father remains alive" are all answered from one absolute fact, the
-- person's `vital status`. See the note on `Vital status` in
-- `family-domain.l4` for why survivorship is not a flag.
GIVEN person IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is dead` IF
    CONSIDER person's `vital status`
    WHEN `living` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN TRUE

GIVEN person IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person remains alive` IF NOT `the person is dead` person

-- A parent of the infant is a `MAYBE Person`, and `family-domain` is
-- emphatic that NOTHING means NOT IN EVIDENCE and must never be allowed to
-- read as a dead parent -- otherwise a missing record satisfies s 6(3)'s
-- jurisdictional gate. These three keep the three states apart.
GIVEN parent IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent is in evidence and has died` IF
    CONSIDER parent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST p  THEN `the person is dead` p

GIVEN parent IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` IF
    CONSIDER parent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST p  THEN `the person remains alive` p

GIVEN parent IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent is not in evidence` IF isNothing parent

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `the reference for the guardian` IS
    guardian's `the guardian`'s `the reference for this person`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2) -- "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` IF
        `the person is dead` (guardian's `the guardian`)
    OR  guardian's `refuses to act`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 10 -- "The court may remove from his guardianship any guardian"
-- s 10's POWER is a discretion and is not encoded; s 10's EFFECT is
-- determinate and is read everywhere a guardian is counted, because a
-- removed guardian is no longer a guardian.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` IF
    guardian's `removed from his guardianship by the court`

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian is able and willing to act and has not been removed` IF
        NOT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` guardian
    AND NOT `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` guardian

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 ss 16(1), 17, 18(1) -- "A guardian of the property of an infant"; s 15(1) -- "the guardian of an infant’s property"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a guardian of the property of the infant` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `of the person or of the property`
    WHEN `guardian of the person of the infant`              THEN FALSE
    WHEN `guardian of the property of the infant`            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `guardian of the person and property of the infant` THEN TRUE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(3) -- "no guardian of the person"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a guardian of the person of the infant` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `of the person or of the property`
    WHEN `guardian of the person of the infant`              THEN TRUE
    WHEN `guardian of the property of the infant`            THEN FALSE
    WHEN `guardian of the person and property of the infant` THEN TRUE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1) -- "The father of an infant may by deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant after his death."
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `appointed by the father by deed or will under section 7(1)` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by deed` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `appointed by the father by will` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(2) -- "The mother of an infant may by deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant after her death."
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `appointed by the mother by deed or will under section 7(2)` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by deed` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by will` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `appointed by a parent by deed or will under section 7(1) or (2)` IF
        `appointed by the father by deed or will under section 7(1)` guardian
    OR  `appointed by the mother by deed or will under section 7(2)` guardian

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 15(1) -- "a person appointed by the court"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a person appointed by the court` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`           THEN TRUE
    WHEN `appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian`           THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE


§§ `1. Short title`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 1 -- "This Act is the Guardianship of Infants Act 1934."
`the short title of this Act` MEANS "Guardianship of Infants Act 1934"



§§ `2. Interpretation`

-- s 2 in full:
--   "In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires, “court” means the
--    General Division of the High Court or a Family Court."   [27/2014; 40/2019]
--
-- THE DEFINED TERM IS ALREADY IN THE SHARED ONTOLOGY. `family-domain`'s
-- `Court` type is exactly this two-member definition, shared with PAA s 2
-- and WA s 28(7), which are in the same words. This module does not
-- redeclare it.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE MUST DECLARE is the WIDER SLOT, because the GIA names two
-- forums that are NOT "court" within s 2 and then does operative work with
-- them: s 5A(7)(a) ("an application for a summons is made to a District
-- Court or Magistrate’s Court under the Criminal Procedure Code 2010") and
-- s 21 ("any money paid into a District Court to the credit of an infant").
-- s 2 cannot be exercised at all against a type whose only members already
-- satisfy it, so the test below needs something that can fail.
--
-- AND THERE IS A THIRD PLACE, which an earlier draft of this note missed: s 3
-- opens "Where in any proceedings before any court", and "any court" is a
-- context that "otherwise requires" within s 2's own opening words. That is
-- the one wider-forum case this module actually encodes -- s 5A(7) and s 21
-- are both declined for other reasons -- and it is why `Forum` is a
-- parameter of `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` below and
-- not merely a type declared for the self-test that follows.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 2; s 5A(7)(a); s 21
DECLARE Forum IS ONE OF
    `the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court`  HAS `which of them` IS A Court
    `a District Court`
    `a Magistrate's Court`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 2 -- “court” means the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court
GIVEN forum IS A Forum
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the forum is a court within the meaning of section 2` IF
    CONSIDER forum
    WHEN `the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` c THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a District Court`                                           THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court`                                       THEN FALSE



§§ `3. Welfare of infant to be paramount consideration`

-- s 3 in full:
--   "Where in any proceedings before any court the custody or upbringing of
--    an infant or the administration of any property belonging to or held in
--    trust for an infant or the application of the income thereof is in
--    question, the court, in deciding that question, shall regard the welfare
--    of the infant as the first and paramount consideration and, save insofar
--    as such welfare otherwise requires, the father of an infant shall not be
--    deemed to have any right superior to that of the mother in respect of
--    such custody, administration or application nor shall the mother be
--    deemed to have any claim superior to that of the father."
--
-- THREE PARTS, AND ONLY TWO OF THEM ARE DECIDABLE.
--
--   (i)  THE GATE -- "Where in any proceedings before any court the custody
--        or upbringing ... is in question". A four-limbed disjunction over
--        the kind of question, and determinate. Encoded.
--
--   (ii) THE PARAMOUNTCY DIRECTION -- "shall regard the welfare of the
--        infant as the first and paramount consideration". NOT ENCODED, and
--        deliberately: it is an instruction about the WEIGHT a court gives a
--        consideration, and weight is not an output. A predicate
--        `the welfare of the infant was regarded as paramount` would be a
--        boolean nobody can compute and everybody would read as decided.
--        What is encoded instead is that the direction APPLIES -- (i) above.
--
--   (iii) THE NEUTRALITY RULE -- "the father of an infant shall not be
--        deemed to have any right superior to that of the mother ... nor
--        shall the mother be deemed to have any claim superior to that of
--        the father". This one IS determinate, because it is a NEGATIVE: it
--        forbids a deeming, and the answer is the same in every case except
--        the one the section itself carves out, "save insofar as such
--        welfare otherwise requires". So it is encoded as a two-valued
--        result driven by that carve-out, which arrives as a construed fact
--        because whether the welfare "otherwise requires" is the very
--        judgement s 3 leaves to the court.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3 -- "the custody or upbringing of an infant or the administration of any property belonging to or held in trust for an infant or the application of the income thereof is in question"
DECLARE `A question in proceedings before a court` IS ONE OF
    `the custody of an infant`
    `the upbringing of an infant`
    `the administration of any property belonging to or held in trust for an infant`
    `the application of the income thereof`
    `a question of some other kind`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3 -- "the custody or upbringing of an infant ... is in question"
GIVEN question IS A `A question in proceedings before a court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the question is one of those section 3 names` IF
    CONSIDER question
    WHEN `a question of some other kind` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3 -- "Where in any proceedings before any court"; s 2 -- "In this Act, unless the context otherwise requires"
-- s 3 SAYS "ANY COURT", AND THAT DISPLACES THE s 2 DEFINITION. s 2 defines
-- "court" as "the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court", but
-- it does so only "unless the context otherwise requires", and s 3's own
-- words are "in any proceedings before any court" -- not "the court", which
-- is the form every other section uses when it means s 2's court. Reading
-- s 2 into s 3 would leave a District Court free to decide an infant's
-- custody without regarding his welfare as paramount, which the words "any
-- court" plainly do not intend. So the answer below is the same for every
-- forum, and it is written out member by member rather than as a constant so
-- that a reader can see that it is exhaustive and deliberate.
GIVEN forum IS A Forum
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 reaches proceedings before the forum` IF
    CONSIDER forum
    WHEN `the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` c THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a District Court`                                           THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a Magistrate's Court`                                       THEN TRUE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3
GIVEN forum    IS A Forum
      question IS A `A question in proceedings before a court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` IF
        "Where in any proceedings before any court"
    ... `section 3 reaches proceedings before the forum` forum
    ... "the custody or upbringing of an infant or the administration of any property belonging to or held in trust for an infant or the application of the income thereof is in question,"
    AND `the question is one of those section 3 names` question

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3 -- "save insofar as such welfare otherwise requires"
DECLARE `The standing of the parents under section 3` IS ONE OF
    `neither the father nor the mother is deemed to have any right or claim superior to that of the other`
    `the welfare of the infant otherwise requires, and to that extent the deeming rule in section 3 does not apply`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 3
-- NOTHING out means s 3 does not govern the question at all, so the section
-- says nothing about the standing of the parents in deciding it. That is not
-- one of s 3's own outcomes, which is why it is a MAYBE and not a third
-- member of the enum above.
GIVEN forum    IS A Forum
      question IS A `A question in proceedings before a court`
      `such welfare otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `The standing of the parents under section 3`
DECIDE `the standing of the father and the mother in deciding the question` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` forum question
           THEN NOTHING
           IF `such welfare otherwise requires`
           THEN JUST `the welfare of the infant otherwise requires, and to that extent the deeming rule in section 3 does not apply`
           OTHERWISE JUST `neither the father nor the mother is deemed to have any right or claim superior to that of the other`



§§ `4. Equal right of mother to apply to court`

-- s 4 in full:
--   "The mother of an infant shall have the like powers of applying to the
--    court in respect of any matter affecting the infant as are possessed by
--    the father."
--
-- A PARITY RULE, and therefore determinate without being self-standing: s 4
-- confers on the mother whatever power the father has, so its output is a
-- function of the father's power and of nothing else. Both inputs are
-- GIVENs rather than facts on a record, because neither is a fact about the
-- infant: the first is about the matter, the second about the general law.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 4
GIVEN `the matter affects the infant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the father possesses the power of applying to the court in respect of the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mother has the like powers of applying to the court in respect of the matter` IF
        `the matter affects the infant`
    AND `the father possesses the power of applying to the court in respect of the matter`



§§ `5. Power of court to make, discharge or amend orders for custody and maintenance of infants`

-- NOT ENCODED. s 5 is a pure discretion:
--   "The court may, upon the application of either parent or of any guardian
--    appointed under this Act, make orders as it may think fit regarding the
--    custody of such infant, the right of access thereto and the payment of
--    any sum towards the maintenance of the infant and may alter, vary or
--    discharge such order on the application of either parent or of any
--    guardian appointed under this Act."
-- "make orders as it may think fit" has no determinate output: nothing in
-- the section lets you compute, from facts, what order follows. What IS
-- determinate is WHO may apply -- "either parent or ... any guardian
-- appointed under this Act" -- and that is not encoded separately either,
-- because standing to apply produces no outcome of its own; the s 5 access
-- order is then taken as a given fact by s 5A(1)(a) below.


§§ `5A. Enforcement of child access order`

-- MOSTLY DISCRETIONARY, AND THE DETERMINATE PARTS ARE CEILINGS AND AN
-- ELECTION. s 5A(3) opens "Without limiting any other power of the court,
-- the court may do all or any of the following in an application under
-- subsection (2)" and then lists five things it MAY do. Nothing there is
-- computable. But four things in the section are:
--
--   (1)    the gate -- both limbs are facts, and their conjunction is the
--          section's own "This section applies where";
--   (3)(e) two hard numeric ceilings, "$20,000" and "12 months";
--   (4)    a hard ceiling on the s 5A(3)(a) order, expressed as a
--          comparison against an existing entitlement;
--   (5)(b) that no fresh application is required;
--   (6)    an EXCLUSIVE CHOICE -- "either, but not both";
--   (8)    an exception with no discretion in it -- "a Family Court has no
--          power to vary an order of the General Division of the High
--          Court".
--
-- NOTE THE ACT'S OWN VOCABULARY SHIFT. s 5A speaks of "a child" throughout,
-- and so does s 11A -- "any question relating to the custody of any child",
-- and "child welfare". Those two are the only sections that use the word.
-- ss 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 11 and 12 to 21 use the word "infant" instead, and
-- ss 1, 2, 9 and 10 use neither word. The word and not the phrase: most of
-- those sections do say "an infant", but three do not use that form at all --
-- s 5 has "the custody of such infant" and "the maintenance of the infant",
-- s 11 has "the welfare of the infant" and "where the infant has a parent",
-- and s 20 has "the infant’s property". (An earlier draft of this
-- comment said s 5A was the only "child" section and that every other
-- operative section said "infant"; both halves were wrong. s 11A is the
-- second, and it is the one that bears on whether the Act treats the two
-- words as interchangeable, since s 11A sits among the "infant" sections and
-- governs the same custody question they do.) The Act does
-- not say whether the two words denote the same class, and nothing in s 2
-- helps. This module does not decide that question -- s 5A is encoded over
-- its own record, `A breach of an access order`, and never over `Infant`.
-- See the ambiguity register at the foot of this file.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(1)(a)-(b)
-- s 5A(1): "This section applies where —
--            (a) the court makes an order under section 5 (called in this
--                section the access order) giving a person (X) access to a
--                child; and
--            (b) the order is breached by the person (Y) who is required by
--                the order to give X access to the child."
-- X and Y are the section's own labels and are carried as references, not
-- as embedded people, because s 5A never asks anything about them except
-- who they are. See the identity note in `family-domain.l4`.
DECLARE `A breach of an access order` HAS
    `the reference for X, the person given access to the child by the access order` IS A STRING
    `the reference for Y, the person required by the order to give X access to the child` IS A STRING
    `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the order is breached by Y`                                          IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(1)
GIVEN breach IS A `A breach of an access order`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 5A applies` IF
        "(a) the court makes an order under section 5 (called in this section the access order) giving a person (X) access to a child; and"
    ... breach's `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child`
    ... "(b) the order is breached by the person (Y) who is required by the order to give X access to the child."
    AND breach's `the order is breached by Y`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(2) -- "X may apply to the court to enforce the access order against Y."
-- The permission is determinate as to WHO and WHEN: it is X's, and it arises
-- once the section applies. What the court then does with the application is
-- s 5A(3), which is not encoded.
GIVEN breach IS A `A breach of an access order`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `X may apply to the court to enforce the access order against Y` IF
    `section 5A applies` breach

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(3)(e) -- "for every breach of the access order by Y, sentence Y to a fine not exceeding $20,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding 12 months or to both"
-- TWO MAYBEs, NOT TWO NUMBERS, because (3)(e) offers three sentences and not
-- one: a fine, imprisonment, or both. A record of two present numbers could
-- not say that only a fine was imposed, and a sentence with neither is not a
-- sentence under (3)(e) at all.
DECLARE `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` HAS
    `the fine imposed, in dollars`                IS A MAYBE Money
    `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(3)(e)
-- "not exceeding" is AT MOST, not BELOW: a fine of exactly $20,000 and a
-- term of exactly 12 months are both within the section. And the ceilings
-- are PER BREACH -- "for every breach of the access order by Y" -- so this
-- decision is about one breach and says nothing about an aggregate.
GIVEN sentence IS A `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` IF
        `a fine or imprisonment or both was imposed`
    AND `the fine does not exceed $20,000`
    AND `the term of imprisonment does not exceed 12 months`
    WHERE
        `a fine or imprisonment or both was imposed` MEANS
                isJust (sentence's `the fine imposed, in dollars`)
            OR  isJust (sentence's `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months`)
        `the fine does not exceed $20,000` MEANS
            CONSIDER sentence's `the fine imposed, in dollars`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
            WHEN JUST f  THEN f AT MOST 20_000
        `the term of imprisonment does not exceed 12 months` MEANS
            CONSIDER sentence's `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE
            WHEN JUST t  THEN t AT MOST 12

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(4) -- "To avoid doubt, an order under subsection (3)(a) must not give X more access than what X is entitled to under the access order."
-- The subsection supplies no UNIT for access. Both quantities are therefore
-- in whatever unit the access order itself measures access by, and the only
-- thing this decision does is compare them -- which is all s 5A(4) does.
GIVEN `the additional access ordered under section 5A(3)(a)`   IS A NUMBER
      `the access to which X is entitled under the access order` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the order under section 5A(3)(a) is within section 5A(4)` IF
    `the additional access ordered under section 5A(3)(a)` AT MOST `the access to which X is entitled under the access order`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(5) -- "The court may, in making any order under subsection (3)(a), (b), (c) or (d) in respect of a breach of the access order — (a) reserve the question whether Y should also be sentenced under subsection (3)(e) for that breach; and (b) return to the question subsequently (without X having to make a fresh application)."
-- s 5A(5)(a) is a discretion -- the court "may ... reserve the question
-- whether Y should also be sentenced under subsection (3)(e) for that
-- breach". s 5A(5)(b) is not: where the question HAS been reserved, the
-- court returns to it WITHOUT a fresh application, and that is a determinate
-- rule about what X need not do.
--
-- IT IS A GATED RULE, NOT A CONSTANT, and an earlier draft had it as a bare
-- `MEANS FALSE`. The whole of (5) hangs off a chapeau -- the court may do
-- these things "in making any order under subsection (3)(a), (b), (c) or
-- (d) in respect of a breach of the access order" -- so the power to
-- reserve, and with it the dispensation from a fresh application, arise only
-- there. Where no question was reserved, s 5A(5)(b) says nothing at all
-- about what X must do, and the constant asserted that it did. The gate
-- arrives as one GIVEN carrying the chapeau's words, because whether the
-- court reserved the question is a fact about what the court did, not
-- something computable from the breach.
GIVEN `the court, in making an order under subsection (3)(a), (b), (c) or (d) in respect of the breach, has reserved the question whether Y should also be sentenced under subsection (3)(e)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may return to the question subsequently without X having to make a fresh application` IF
    `the court, in making an order under subsection (3)(a), (b), (c) or (d) in respect of the breach, has reserved the question whether Y should also be sentenced under subsection (3)(e)`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(6)(a)-(b) -- "In respect of a breach of an access order, X may do either, but not both, of the following: (a) make an application under subsection (2); (b) bring proceedings to punish Y for contempt of court in respect of that breach."
-- THE ONE GENUINE EXCLUSIVE CHOICE IN THE ACT. Four states, not two
-- booleans: "either, but not both" forbids exactly one of the four, and a
-- pair of independent flags would let a reader think the section merely
-- prefers one route.
DECLARE `What X has done in respect of the breach` IS ONE OF
    `neither`
    `made an application under subsection (2)`
    `brought proceedings to punish Y for contempt of court in respect of that breach`
    `both made an application under subsection (2) and brought proceedings for contempt`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(6)
GIVEN `what X has done` IS A `What X has done in respect of the breach`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `X has kept within the election permitted by section 5A(6)` IF
    CONSIDER `what X has done`
    WHEN `both made an application under subsection (2) and brought proceedings for contempt` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(8) -- "except that a Family Court has no power to vary an order of the General Division of the High Court"
-- s 5A(8) confers a power and then withholds one, and the withholding is
-- absolute: the exception has no "unless", no "may" and no discretion. The
-- conferral is likewise determinate as to WHICH orders it reaches -- "an
-- access order made by the General Division of the High Court (whether
-- before, on or after 1 October 2014)", i.e. all of them, the parenthesis
-- removing any commencement question.
DECLARE `A step taken by a Family Court in respect of an access order made by the General Division of the High Court` IS ONE OF
    `enforcement in accordance with section 5A`
    `variation of the order`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 5A(8)
GIVEN step IS A `A step taken by a Family Court in respect of an access order made by the General Division of the High Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Family Court has power to take the step` IF
    CONSIDER step
    WHEN `enforcement in accordance with section 5A` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `variation of the order`                    THEN FALSE

-- NOT ENCODED, s 5A(3)(a)-(d): the menu of orders. "the court may do all or
-- any of the following" -- five discretionary powers, of which only (e)
-- carries a computable limit. (3)(c) further defers to a list of programmes
-- and to "a family support programme as defined in section 139A of the
-- Women’s Charter 1961", which is outside this Act. (3)(d)'s bond is "with
-- or without sureties or security, as the court may determine".
-- NOT ENCODED, s 5A(7): pure procedure, and expressly displaceable --
-- "Unless the Family Justice Rules provide otherwise" -- by rules this
-- module does not have. Its content is the manner of making an application
-- and the provisions of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 that apply, both
-- of which are outside the Act.


§§ `6. Rights of surviving parent as to guardianship`

-- THE FIRST OF THE TWO COMPOSITION RULES. s 6(1) and s 6(2) are written out
-- twice in the Act, once for each parent, and they are written out twice
-- here for the same reason: a reader holding s 6(2) should find s 6(2), not
-- a mirror flag on s 6(1).
--
-- s 6(1): "On the death of the father of an infant, the mother, if
--          surviving, shall, subject to the provisions of this Act, be
--          guardian of the infant, either alone or jointly with any guardian
--          appointed by the father. When no guardian has been appointed by
--          the father or if the guardian or guardians appointed by the
--          father is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act, the court may
--          if it thinks fit appoint a guardian to act jointly with the
--          mother."
--
-- TWO SENTENCES, TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF RULE. The first is self-executing --
-- "shall ... be guardian" -- and computes an answer from who died and who
-- was appointed. The second confers a DISCRETION -- "the court may if it
-- thinks fit appoint" -- so only its ANTECEDENT is encoded; whether the
-- court appoints anyone is not computable and is not pretended to be.
--
-- The words "subject to the provisions of this Act" are carried, not
-- dropped: they are why
-- the composition below reads s 7(3)'s objection limb and s 10's removal.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1) -- "On the death of the father of an infant, the mother, if surviving, shall ... be guardian of the infant"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` IF
        "On the death of the father of an infant,"
    ... `the parent is in evidence and has died` (infant's `the father of the infant`)
    ... "the mother, if surviving,"
    AND `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (infant's `the mother of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(2) -- "On the death of the mother of an infant, the father, if surviving, shall ... be guardian of the infant"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` IF
        "On the death of the mother of an infant,"
    ... `the parent is in evidence and has died` (infant's `the mother of the infant`)
    ... "the father, if surviving,"
    AND `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (infant's `the father of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1) -- "any guardian appointed by the father"; s 10
-- A guardian removed by the court under s 10 is not counted, because s 10
-- ends his guardianship; the Act says so of removal generally and s 6(1)
-- does not need to repeat it.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
DECIDE `the guardians appointed by the father` IS
    filter (GIVEN g YIELD     `appointed by the father by deed or will under section 7(1)` g
                          AND NOT `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` g)
           (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(2) -- "any guardian appointed by the mother"; s 10
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
DECIDE `the guardians appointed by the mother` IS
    filter (GIVEN g YIELD     `appointed by the mother by deed or will under section 7(2)` g
                          AND NOT `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` g)
           (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)

GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
DECIDE `the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` IS
    filter `the guardian is able and willing to act and has not been removed`
           (`the guardians appointed by the father` infant)

GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
DECIDE `the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` IS
    filter `the guardian is able and willing to act and has not been removed`
           (`the guardians appointed by the mother` infant)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2) -- "shall, subject to the provisions of this Act, be guardian of the infant"; s 10 -- "The court may remove from his guardianship any guardian"
-- s 6 MAKES THE SURVIVOR GUARDIAN AND s 10 CAN TAKE IT BACK. s 10 reaches
-- "any guardian", and s 6(1)-(2) make the survivor's guardianship "subject
-- to the provisions of this Act" in terms, so s 10 reaches the parent as
-- much as it reaches an appointee. An earlier draft read s 10 on every
-- appointee and on nobody else, so a mother removed by the court was still
-- returned by `the persons who are guardians of the infant` -- which
-- falsified this module's own claim, made twice, that a removal is read in
-- every count.
--
-- WHERE THE FLAG LIVES. A parent is guardian by force of the section and
-- needs no `Guardian` record to be one; but if the court has removed her
-- there will be a record, because `removed from his guardianship by the
-- court` is a field on `Guardian` and there is nowhere else to put it. Its
-- route is `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` -- the one
-- route the traversal at the foot of s 7 excludes, precisely so the parent
-- is not counted twice. Excluded from the count is not excluded from the
-- reading: the record is skipped there and read here.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
      parent IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` IF
    CONSIDER parent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST p THEN
        any (GIVEN g YIELD     `he holds by virtue of surviving the other parent` g
                           AND (g's `the guardian`'s `the reference for this person`)
                                 EQUALS (p's `the reference for this person`)
                           AND `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` g)
            (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1); s 10
-- s 6(1) FIRST SENTENCE, READ SUBJECT TO s 10. The decision above is s 6(1)
-- in its own words and is left that way, because a reader holding s 6(1)
-- should find s 6(1); this one is the composition, and it is what every
-- count below uses.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` IF
        `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` infant
    AND NOT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10`
              infant (infant's `the mother of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(2); s 10
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` IF
        `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` infant
    AND NOT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10`
              infant (infant's `the father of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1) -- "either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father"
-- NOTHING out means s 6(1) does not make the mother guardian at all -- the
-- father has not died, or the mother is not surviving, or one of them is not
-- in evidence, or the court has removed her under s 10. Those four are
-- separately answerable above; the section
-- itself names no such outcome, which is why this is a MAYBE rather than a
-- fourth member of `Alone or jointly`.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE `Alone or jointly`
DECIDE `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` infant
           THEN NOTHING
           IF count (`the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` infant) EQUALS 0
           THEN JUST `alone`
           OTHERWISE JUST (`jointly with`
                             (map `the reference for the guardian`
                                  (`the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` infant)))

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(2) -- "either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE `Alone or jointly`
DECIDE `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` infant
           THEN NOTHING
           IF count (`the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` infant) EQUALS 0
           THEN JUST `alone`
           OTHERWISE JUST (`jointly with`
                             (map `the reference for the guardian`
                                  (`the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` infant)))

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1) -- "When no guardian has been appointed by the father or if the guardian or guardians appointed by the father is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act"
-- THE ANTECEDENT ONLY. The consequent, "the court may if it thinks fit
-- appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother", is a discretion.
--
-- THE PLURAL IS READ AS "ALL", AND THAT IS A CHOICE. "the guardian or
-- guardians appointed by the father is or are dead or refuses or refuse to
-- act" pairs a singular and a plural verb with a singular and a plural
-- subject, so on its face it covers the one-appointee case and the
-- several-appointee case; in the several-appointee case the natural reading
-- of "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act" is that they all are.
-- Read that way the
-- limb is exactly the complement of the first sentence's "jointly with any
-- guardian appointed by the father": the court's power arises precisely
-- where the mother would otherwise be guardian ALONE. The competing reading
-- -- that the power arises as soon as ANY one appointee drops out -- is
-- recorded in the ambiguity register at the foot of this file.
--
-- AND ON THAT READING THE TWO LIMBS ARE NOT INDEPENDENT: `all` over an empty
-- list is TRUE, so wherever "no guardian has been appointed" holds, "the
-- guardian or guardians ... is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act" holds
-- with it, and the first limb decides nothing the second would not decide
-- alone. It is kept because the Act writes it, and isomorphism is the point
-- of this module; it is named here because no fixture can ever falsify it, so
-- a reader looking for the test that pins it will not find one and should
-- know why. On the ANY reading the two limbs WOULD be independent, which is
-- one more thing that turns on register entry 2.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` IF
        `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` infant
    AND (    "When no guardian has been appointed by the father"
         ..  count (`the guardians appointed by the father` infant) EQUALS 0
         ..  "or if the guardian or guardians appointed by the father is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act,"
         OR  all `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` (`the guardians appointed by the father` infant))

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(2) -- "When no guardian has been appointed by the mother or if the guardian or guardians appointed by the mother is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` IF
        `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` infant
    AND (    "When no guardian has been appointed by the mother"
         ..  count (`the guardians appointed by the mother` infant) EQUALS 0
         ..  "or if the guardian or guardians appointed by the mother is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act,"
         OR  all `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` (`the guardians appointed by the mother` infant))

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(3) -- "Where an infant has no parent, no guardian of the person and no other person having parental rights with respect to him"
-- THE THREE-LIMBED GATE, and each limb is answered from a different place.
--
--   "no parent"                -- BOTH parents in evidence AND both dead.
--                                 A parent who is NOT IN EVIDENCE does not
--                                 satisfy this limb: `family-domain` says in
--                                 terms that a missing record must not read
--                                 as a dead parent and let a jurisdictional
--                                 gate through, and this is the gate it
--                                 means.
--   "no guardian of the person"-- the guardians of the infant, filtered on
--                                 `of the person or of the property` and on
--                                 the same able-and-willing test ss 6 and 7
--                                 use everywhere else -- see below.
--   "no other person having parental rights with respect to him"
--                              -- a recorded fact; nothing else answers it.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the infant has no parent` IF
        `the parent is in evidence and has died` (infant's `the father of the infant`)
    AND `the parent is in evidence and has died` (infant's `the mother of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(3) -- "no guardian of the person"; s 6(1)-(2) -- "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act"; s 10
-- A DEAD, A REFUSING AND A REMOVED GUARDIAN ALL LEAVE THE INFANT WITH "NO
-- GUARDIAN OF THE PERSON". An earlier draft filtered on removal alone, and
-- the result was a module that answered "this infant has no guardians at
-- all" and "the s 6(3) gate is not satisfied" in the same breath, whenever
-- the one recorded guardian of the person was dead or refused to act.
--
-- Death and removal need no argument: a dead man is nobody's guardian, and
-- s 10 ends the guardianship of the one it removes. REFUSAL IS THE
-- INTERPRETIVE LIMB, and the Act itself supplies the reading. s 6(1)-(2)
-- treat an appointee who "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act" as
-- absent for exactly this purpose -- the purpose of deciding whether the
-- court should step in and supply somebody -- and s 6(3) is a gate of the
-- same character, opening where nobody is there to look after the infant.
-- Recorded in the ambiguity register.
--
-- SO THIS ASKS THE MODULE'S OWN QUESTION: is there anybody who, on these
-- facts, acts as guardian of the person of this infant? That is
-- `the standing of the guardian under this Act`, which already answers "no"
-- for a guardian who is dead, who refuses to act and whom the court has
-- removed -- and also for two cases an ability test alone would miss, both of
-- which are genuine on their own terms even though neither can arise inside
-- s 6(3)'s own gate (each needs a living parent, and this gate needs none):
-- a testamentary appointee whose appointing parent is still alive, whose
-- appointment operates only "after his death" and who is therefore not yet a
-- guardian at all, and one whom the surviving parent has objected to under
-- s 7(3), who on this module's reading of that subsection does not act.
--
-- Using the same predicate on both sides is what keeps the answer here and
-- `the persons who are guardians of the infant` from contradicting each other
-- ON THE APPOINTEES, which is the defect this note began with.
--
-- AND IT WAS NOT ENOUGH, BECAUSE THE APPOINTEES ARE NOT THE ONLY GUARDIANS.
-- The second limb below is the repair for a contradiction of exactly the same
-- shape arriving by the other route. s 6(1)-(2) make the surviving parent
-- guardian BY FORCE OF THE SECTION -- "the mother, if surviving, shall,
-- subject to the provisions of this Act, be guardian of the infant" -- and
-- she needs no `Guardian` record to be one; the module says so in terms at
-- `the surviving parent who is guardian under section 6` below. A count that
-- reads only records therefore could not see her, and on the plainest facts
-- in the file -- the father dead, the mother alive, nobody appointed -- the
-- module answered "the guardians of this infant are: the mother" and "the
-- infant has no guardian of the person" in one breath. The tell was that the
-- SAME s 6 facts with a redundant record for the mother answered the other
-- way, so the answer turned on whether a case-file author had written down a
-- record the module itself says is unnecessary.
--
-- A PARENT WHO IS "GUARDIAN OF THE INFANT" IS A GUARDIAN OF HIS PERSON.
-- s 6(1)-(2) make the survivor guardian of the infant simpliciter, with no
-- division into person and property; s 6(3)'s "no guardian of the person" is
-- a limb about who is there to look after the infant, and the surviving
-- parent is the paradigm of somebody who is. The limb reads the "holds"
-- decision and not the bare s 6(1)/(2) test, so a parent the court has
-- removed under s 10 does not close it.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the infant has no guardian of the person` IF
        "no guardian of the person"
    ... `no parent is guardian of the infant by force of section 6`
    AND `no person recorded as a guardian of his person acts as one`
    WHERE
        `no parent is guardian of the infant by force of section 6` MEANS
            count (`the surviving parent who is guardian under section 6` infant) EQUALS 0
        `no person recorded as a guardian of his person acts as one` MEANS
            count (filter (GIVEN g YIELD     `a guardian of the person of the infant` g
                                         AND `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant`
                                               (`the standing of the guardian under this Act` g infant))
                          (infant's `the guardians of the infant`))
            EQUALS 0

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(3)
-- THE GATE ONLY. "the court, on the application of any person, may, if it
-- thinks fit, appoint the applicant to be the guardian of the infant" is a
-- discretion twice over -- "may" and "if it thinks fit" -- so who, if
-- anyone, is appointed is not computed here.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` IF
        "Where an infant has no parent,"
    ... `the infant has no parent` infant
    ... "no guardian of the person"
    ... `the infant has no guardian of the person` infant
    ... "and no other person having parental rights with respect to him,"
    AND NOT infant's `some other person has parental rights with respect to him`


§§ `7. Power of father and mother to appoint testamentary guardians`

-- THE HEART OF THE ACT. Six subsections that compose: an appointment (1)-(2)
-- that lies dormant until the appointing parent dies; a default of joint
-- action with the survivor (3) that the survivor can switch off by
-- objecting; a route to the court (4) whose three possible orders each have
-- a stated consequence; a rule for two sets of appointees meeting on the
-- second death (5); and a rule for a court's own appointee meeting the
-- survivor's appointee on that same death (6).
--
-- WHAT IS DETERMINATE AND WHAT IS NOT, subsection by subsection:
--   (1),(2)  determinate -- who may appoint, by what instrument, and from
--            when the appointment operates.
--   (3)      determinate -- a default plus one switch, both computable.
--   (4)      the APPLICATION is determinate (who may apply, on what facts);
--            the CHOICE of order is a discretion and is not computed. But
--            each of the three orders has a CONSEQUENCE the subsection
--            states expressly, and those are encoded, including the express
--            consequence of a refusal.
--   (5),(6)  determinate -- pure composition rules.

§§§ `7(1)-(2). Who may appoint, and from when the appointment operates`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1) -- "The father of an infant may by deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant after his death."; s 7(2) -- "The mother of an infant may by deed or will appoint any person to be guardian of the infant after her death."
-- ***** THE OTHER SIDE OF WILLS ACT 1838 s 27(3). *****
--
-- s 7(1) and s 7(2) let a parent appoint "by deed or will". Where the
-- instrument is a WILL, whether the appointment is any good is not this Act's
-- question: it is the Wills Act's. wills-act.l4 states the hand-off from its
-- own side, at its
-- `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the
-- testator is of full force and effect`, and asks this module to read, for a
-- guardian appointed by will, whether that decision is TRUE. This note is
-- that request answered.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE DOES: it takes a will-appointment as ALREADY CONSTRUED.
-- The members `appointed by the father by will` and
-- `appointed by the mother by will` of `How the guardian was appointed`
-- record that a good appointment was made by will; the decisions below impose
-- no condition on the will and never look at one. So the two routes are on
-- the same footing here -- a deed-appointed and a will-appointed guardian are
-- treated identically -- and that is deliberate, not an oversight.
--
-- WHY IT CANNOT READ THE FACT INSTEAD, which is the honest form of the
-- answer. This module can read every field the appointment turns on except
-- the one that decides it: `Guardian` carries how the guardian was appointed
-- but carries NOTHING about the instrument, and `Infant` carries no will. The
-- object that would carry it, `Will`'s
-- `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will`, hangs off a
-- `Deceased person` that never reaches any decision in this module. So the
-- hand-off is TYPED -- both sides share `Guardian` -- and cannot be TAKEN
-- from inside this file. Reported as a domain gap in
-- `Where the shared ontology stopped short` below.
--
-- WHAT A COMBINED CASE FILE MUST DO, so that the gap is closable rather than
-- merely named: run the Wills Act decision on the will FIRST, and set
-- `appointed by the father by will` or `appointed by the mother by will` only
-- where it answered TRUE. Where it answered FALSE the appointment is not of
-- full force and effect and the guardian is not appointed at all -- which in
-- this module's vocabulary is no `Guardian` record, not a `Guardian` who
-- fails a test.
--
-- ONE THING THE WILLS ACT SETTLES THAT THIS ACT WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE TO:
-- wills-act.l4 records at that site that a guardian since REMOVED is still an
-- appointment of full force and effect, because s 27(3) asks whether the
-- appointment contained in the will is good and removal ends the office
-- without unmaking the appointment. That is right, and it is this Act's
-- s 10 that ends the office -- see
-- `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` above,
-- which is read at every place a guardian is counted. The two modules
-- therefore divide the question cleanly and neither needs to trust the other
-- on it.
-- "after his death" / "after her death" is the operative limitation: the
-- appointment is made in the appointing parent's lifetime and takes effect
-- on his or her death, not before. Everything downstream in s 7 assumes it.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
DECIDE `the parent by whom the guardian was appointed` IS
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by deed` THEN infant's `the father of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by will` THEN infant's `the father of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by deed` THEN infant's `the mother of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by will` THEN infant's `the mother of the infant`
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(3) -- "shall act jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
DECIDE `the other parent, the mother or father as the case may be` IS
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by deed` THEN infant's `the mother of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the father by will` THEN infant's `the mother of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by deed` THEN infant's `the father of the infant`
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by will` THEN infant's `the father of the infant`
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1)-(2) -- "to be guardian of the infant after his death" / "after her death"
-- THE ROUTE CONJUNCT IS ENTAILED BY THE DEATH CONJUNCT and cannot be
-- falsified on its own: `the parent by whom the guardian was appointed`
-- answers NOTHING for every route that is not a parental appointment, so the
-- second conjunct is already FALSE wherever the first would be. It is written
-- out because s 7(1)-(2) are about WHO may appoint as much as about when the
-- appointment operates, and a reader holding those subsections should find
-- both; it is named here because no file can ever make it the reason for an
-- answer, and a coverage claim that did not say so would be false.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` IF
        `appointed by a parent by deed or will under section 7(1) or (2)` guardian
    AND `the parent is in evidence and has died` (`the parent by whom the guardian was appointed` guardian infant)

§§§ `7(3). Joint action with the surviving parent, unless that parent objects`

-- s 7(3): "Any guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or
--          father, as the case may be, of the infant so long as the mother
--          or father remains alive, unless the mother or father objects to
--          his so acting."
--
-- A DEFAULT WITH A SWITCH, and the burden sits where the subsection puts it:
-- joint action is the rule and the objection is the exception, so the
-- encoding tests for the objection rather than asking whether the parent
-- consented. A guardian whose appointing parent has died and whose surviving
-- co-parent has said nothing acts jointly.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(3) -- "unless the mother or father objects to his so acting"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` IF
        `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` guardian infant
    ... "so long as the mother or father remains alive,"
    AND `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (`the other parent, the mother or father as the case may be` guardian infant)
    ... "unless the mother or father objects to his so acting."
    AND NOT guardian's `the mother or father objects to his so acting`

§§§ `7(4). Application to the court, and what follows from each order`

-- s 7(4): "If the mother or father so objects, or if the guardian so
--          appointed as aforesaid considers that the mother or father is
--          unfit to have the custody of the infant, the guardian may apply
--          to the court, and the court may either refuse to make any order
--          (in which case the mother or father shall remain sole guardian)
--          or make an order that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly
--          with the mother or father, or that he shall be sole guardian of
--          the infant, and in the later case may make such order regarding
--          the custody of the infant and the right of access thereto of the
--          mother or father as, having regard to the welfare of the infant,
--          the court may think fit, and may further order that the mother or
--          father shall pay to the guardian towards the maintenance of the
--          infant such weekly or other periodical sum as, having regard to
--          the means of the mother or father, the court may consider
--          reasonable."
--
-- ("in the later case" is the source's spelling and is reproduced as it
-- appears; it plainly refers to the sole-guardianship order, which is the
-- last of the three the sentence names.)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(4) -- "If the mother or father so objects, or if the guardian so appointed as aforesaid considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant, the guardian may apply to the court"
-- WHOSE RIGHT THIS IS. Both limbs are written in the vocabulary of s 7(3)
-- and are confined by it. "so objects" can only mean an objection of the
-- kind s 7(3) provides for, and s 7(3) governs "Any guardian so appointed",
-- i.e. one appointed by a parent under s 7(1) or (2); the second limb says
-- "the guardian so appointed as aforesaid" in terms. An earlier draft took a
-- bare `Guardian` and read two flags off it, so it answered TRUE for a
-- guardian the court had appointed, for the surviving parent herself, and
-- for a testamentary appointee whose appointing parent was still alive --
-- for whom this module's own `the standing of the guardian under this Act`
-- says "the appointment has not taken effect".
--
-- SO TWO CONJUNCTS ARE ADDED, and the second goes further than the defect
-- reported. The first is that the appointment has taken effect, which
-- carries the route test with it. The second is that the other parent
-- remains alive: s 7(3) operates only "so long as the mother or father
-- remains alive", every one of the three orders s 7(4) offers speaks of the
-- mother or father as a living party ("the mother or father shall remain
-- sole guardian"; "act jointly with the mother or father"; an order about
-- "the right of access thereto of the mother or father"), and where both
-- parents are dead it is s 7(5) and s 7(6) that compose the guardianship,
-- not s 7(4).
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` IF
        "Any guardian so appointed"
    ... `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` guardian infant
    ... "so long as the mother or father remains alive,"
    ... `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (`the other parent, the mother or father as the case may be` guardian infant)
    AND (    "If the mother or father so objects,"
         ..  guardian's `the mother or father objects to his so acting`
         ..  "or if the guardian so appointed as aforesaid considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant,"
         OR  guardian's `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(4)
DECLARE `The order the court may make under section 7(4)` IS ONE OF
    `the court refuses to make any order`
    `the court orders that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father`
    `the court orders that he shall be sole guardian of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(4)
DECLARE `Who is guardian after an order under section 7(4)` IS ONE OF
    `the mother or father remains sole guardian`
    `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father`
    `the guardian so appointed is sole guardian of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(4) -- "the court may either refuse to make any order (in which case the mother or father shall remain sole guardian) or make an order that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father, or that he shall be sole guardian of the infant"
-- WHICH order the court makes is a DISCRETION and is not computed. What
-- follows FROM each order is not: the subsection states the consequence of a
-- refusal in a parenthesis of its own, and the other two orders say their
-- own consequence. So the discretion is an INPUT here and the mapping is the
-- rule.
GIVEN order IS A `The order the court may make under section 7(4)`
GIVETH A `Who is guardian after an order under section 7(4)`
DECIDE `who is guardian after the order under section 7(4)` IS
    CONSIDER order
    WHEN `the court refuses to make any order`
        THEN `the mother or father remains sole guardian`
    WHEN `the court orders that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father`
        THEN `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father`
    WHEN `the court orders that he shall be sole guardian of the infant`
        THEN `the guardian so appointed is sole guardian of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(4) -- "and in the later case may make such order regarding the custody of the infant and the right of access thereto of the mother or father ... and may further order that the mother or father shall pay to the guardian towards the maintenance of the infant such weekly or other periodical sum"
-- THE ANCILLARY POWERS ARE GATED, and the gate is determinate even though
-- the powers are not: they arise only "in the later case", i.e. only where
-- the court has made the appointee sole guardian. What order it then makes
-- as to custody, access and maintenance is "as ... the court may think fit"
-- and "as ... the court may consider reasonable", and is not encoded.
GIVEN order IS A `The order the court may make under section 7(4)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make the further orders under section 7(4) as to custody, access and maintenance` IF
    CONSIDER order
    WHEN `the court orders that he shall be sole guardian of the infant` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§§ `7(5). Guardians appointed by both parents act jointly after the second death`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(5) -- "Where guardians are appointed by both parents, the guardians so appointed shall after the death of the surviving parent act jointly."
-- COUNTED ON THE SAME FOOTING AS s 6(1)'s "any guardian appointed by the
-- father": only appointees able and willing to act, and not removed. An
-- earlier draft counted the appointments here and the appointees in the
-- answer, so where the father's only appointee was dead the module reported
-- that "the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly" and then named
-- one person -- joint action by a single guardian.
--
-- The reason is the reason register entry 3 gives for s 6(1), and it is
-- stronger here: what s 7(5) does with the appointments is make them act
-- JOINTLY, and a dead or refusing appointee cannot act jointly with anybody.
-- Where one side's appointees have all dropped out there is nothing for the
-- other side's to act jointly with, and the survivor's appointee simply acts
-- -- which is the answer `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent
-- by deed or will` then gives him.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guardians are appointed by both parents` IF
        count (`the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` infant) AT LEAST 1
    AND count (`the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` infant) AT LEAST 1

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(5)
-- "after the death of the surviving parent" is the same fact as s 6(3)'s
-- "an infant has no parent" -- both parents in evidence and both dead -- so
-- the predicate written for s 6(3) is reused rather than restated. It is not
-- the same RULE: s 6(3) adds two further limbs that s 7(5) does not have.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 7(5) is engaged` IF
        "Where guardians are appointed by both parents,"
    ... `guardians are appointed by both parents` infant
    ... "the guardians so appointed shall after the death of the surviving parent act jointly."
    AND `the infant has no parent` infant

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(5) -- "the guardians so appointed shall ... act jointly"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` IS
    IF NOT `section 7(5) is engaged` infant
    THEN EMPTY
    ELSE map `the reference for the guardian`
             (append (`the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` infant)
                     (`the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` infant))

§§§ `7(6). The court's appointee after the death of the parent he was appointed to act with`

-- s 7(6): "If a guardian has been appointed by the court to act jointly with
--          a surviving parent, he shall continue to act as guardian after
--          the death of the surviving parent; but if the surviving parent
--          has appointed a guardian, the guardian appointed by the court
--          shall act jointly with the guardian appointed by the surviving
--          parent."
--
-- WHO THE SURVIVING PARENT IS has to be computed, and it is the only place
-- in the Act where the ORDER of two deaths matters. Where both parents are
-- dead and both dates are known, the survivor is the one who died later.
-- Where a date of death is not known, or both died on the same day, the
-- question cannot be answered on the recorded facts and the standing says
-- so rather than guessing.

GIVEN person IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `the date of death of the person, if known` IS
    CONSIDER person's `vital status`
    WHEN `living` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `died on` `the date of death` THEN JUST `the date of death`
    WHEN `dead, the date of death not known` THEN NOTHING

GIVEN `the first person`  IS A Person
      `the second person` IS A Person
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
DECIDE `the later of the two deaths` IS
    CONSIDER `the date of death of the person, if known` `the first person`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the first date` THEN
        CONSIDER `the date of death of the person, if known` `the second person`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST `the second date` THEN
            BRANCH IF Day `the first date` GREATER THAN Day `the second date`
                   THEN JUST `the first person`
                   IF Day `the second date` GREATER THAN Day `the first date`
                   THEN JUST `the second person`
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(6) -- "a surviving parent"; s 6(1)-(2) -- "if surviving"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE Person
DECIDE `the parent who survived the other` IS
    CONSIDER infant's `the father of the infant`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the father` THEN
        CONSIDER infant's `the mother of the infant`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST `the mother` THEN
            BRANCH IF `the person remains alive` `the father` AND `the person is dead` `the mother`
                   THEN JUST `the father`
                   IF `the person remains alive` `the mother` AND `the person is dead` `the father`
                   THEN JUST `the mother`
                   -- This guard is belt and braces and cannot be falsified:
                   -- `the later of the two deaths` already answers NOTHING
                   -- unless both dates are known, so removing the guard would
                   -- change no answer anywhere. It is kept because it states
                   -- the case the branch is FOR.
                   IF `the person is dead` `the father` AND `the person is dead` `the mother`
                   THEN `the later of the two deaths` `the father` `the mother`
                   OTHERWISE NOTHING

GIVEN person IS A Person
      infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is the father of the infant` IF
    CONSIDER infant's `the father of the infant`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST f THEN f's `the reference for this person` EQUALS person's `the reference for this person`

GIVEN person IS A Person
      infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is the mother of the infant` IF
    CONSIDER infant's `the mother of the infant`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST m THEN m's `the reference for this person` EQUALS person's `the reference for this person`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(6) -- "if the surviving parent has appointed a guardian"
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF Guardian
DECIDE `the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other, who are able and willing to act` IS
    CONSIDER `the parent who survived the other` infant
    WHEN NOTHING THEN EMPTY
    WHEN JUST `the surviving parent` THEN
        IF `the person is the father of the infant` `the surviving parent` infant
        THEN `the guardians appointed by the father who are able and willing to act` infant
        ELSE `the guardians appointed by the mother who are able and willing to act` infant

-- Two readable projections, for the #EVALs alone: a `Person` and a
-- `Guardian` print as whole records, which buries the answer.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the reference for the parent who survived the other` IS
    `the reference for the parent, if in evidence` (`the parent who survived the other` infant)

GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the references for the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` IS
    map `the reference for the guardian`
        (`the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other, who are able and willing to act` infant)

§§§ `The composition: what standing each person has, and who the guardians are`

-- ss 6 AND 7 TOGETHER. Every member below is a case the Act states, and the
-- comment beside it names the provision that states it. The classification
-- is TOTAL over `How the guardian was appointed` -- which is what makes it
-- checkable against the Act rather than merely plausible.
@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(3), s 7(1)-(6), s 10
DECLARE `The standing of a guardian under this Act` IS ONE OF
    -- s 10: removal ends the guardianship, whatever created it.
    `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
    -- s 6(1)-(2): "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act".
    `he is dead or refuses to act`
    -- Not a case in the Act: a case in the FILE. It fires where a parent of
    -- the infant is not in evidence, or where neither parent has died so
    -- there is no surviving parent at all.
    --
    -- IT USED TO FIRE ON A THIRD CASE AND SHOULD NOT HAVE. Where BOTH parents
    -- are dead and the order of the two deaths cannot be established -- a
    -- date not known, or the same day -- s 7(6) still answers the first
    -- question: "he shall continue to act as guardian after the death of the
    -- surviving parent", and whichever parent survived, that parent is dead.
    -- Only the SECOND question is open, because "if the surviving parent has
    -- appointed a guardian" cannot be answered without knowing which parent
    -- survived. Saying that even "whether he acts" was unknown put the module
    -- in contradiction with itself: it dropped him from
    -- `the persons who are guardians of the infant` while
    -- `the infant has no guardian of the person` went on counting him, so the
    -- file said the infant had no guardian and had one. The member below
    -- carries the narrower answer.
    `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
    -- s 7(6), first limb, where the second limb has no answer on the facts.
    `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
    -- s 7(1)-(2): "to be guardian of the infant after his death".
    `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive`
    -- s 7(3), the default limb.
    `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`
    -- s 7(3), the "unless" limb; s 7(4) is then open to him.
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`
    -- s 7(5).
    `after the death of the surviving parent the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly`
    -- s 7(1)-(2) where the second parent has died and s 7(5) is not engaged
    -- because only one parent appointed anybody.
    `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`
    -- s 6(1)-(2), second sentence: the court's appointee, while the parent
    -- he was appointed to act with is still alive.
    `he acts jointly with the surviving parent, the court having appointed him to do so`
    -- s 7(6), second limb.
    `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, jointly with the guardian appointed by that parent`
    -- s 7(6), first limb.
    `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent`
    -- s 6(3).
    `he acts as guardian of the infant, the infant having no parent`
    -- s 10, second limb.
    `he acts as guardian in place of a guardian removed by the court`
    -- s 6(1)-(2), first sentence.
    `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
    `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 7(1)-(5)
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
DECIDE `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent by deed or will` IS
    BRANCH IF     `the parent is not in evidence` (`the parent by whom the guardian was appointed` guardian infant)
              OR  `the parent is not in evidence` (`the other parent, the mother or father as the case may be` guardian infant)
           THEN `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
           IF NOT `the parent is in evidence and has died` (`the parent by whom the guardian was appointed` guardian infant)
           THEN `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive`
           IF `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (`the other parent, the mother or father as the case may be` guardian infant)
           THEN IF guardian's `the mother or father objects to his so acting`
                THEN `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`
                ELSE `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`
           IF `section 7(5) is engaged` infant
           THEN `after the death of the surviving parent the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly`
           OTHERWISE `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2) second sentence; s 7(6) -- "he shall continue to act as guardian after the death of the surviving parent; but if the surviving parent has appointed a guardian, the guardian appointed by the court shall act jointly with the guardian appointed by the surviving parent"
-- s 7(6) ASKS TWO QUESTIONS AND THEY FAIL SEPARATELY. The first -- does he go
-- on acting? -- is answered by the death of the surviving parent, whoever
-- that was. The second -- does he act jointly with somebody? -- needs the
-- survivor to be identified, because it turns on whether THAT parent
-- appointed a guardian.
--
-- So where both parents are dead and the order of the deaths cannot be
-- established, the first question still has an answer and only the second
-- does not. An earlier draft collapsed the two and reported that nothing
-- could be determined, which took a guardian the Act says "shall continue to
-- act" out of `the persons who are guardians of the infant` while
-- `the infant has no guardian of the person` went on counting him -- the
-- module contradicting itself, in the same shape as the s 6(3) defect above.
-- `the infant has no parent` is the test for "both in evidence and both
-- dead", which is exactly the case in which the first question is answerable.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
DECIDE `the standing of a guardian appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` IS
    CONSIDER `the parent who survived the other` infant
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF   `the infant has no parent` infant
        THEN `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
        ELSE `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
    WHEN JUST `the surviving parent` THEN
        BRANCH IF `the person remains alive` `the surviving parent`
               THEN `he acts jointly with the surviving parent, the court having appointed him to do so`
               IF count (`the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other, who are able and willing to act` infant) AT LEAST 1
               THEN `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, jointly with the guardian appointed by that parent`
               OTHERWISE `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2) first sentence
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
DECIDE `the standing of a parent who holds by virtue of surviving the other parent` IS
    BRANCH IF     `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` infant
              AND `the person is the mother of the infant` (guardian's `the guardian`) infant
           THEN `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
           IF     `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` infant
              AND `the person is the father of the infant` (guardian's `the guardian`) infant
           THEN `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
           OTHERWISE `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(3), s 7(1)-(6), s 10
-- s 10 and the death-or-refusal limb are tested FIRST, before anything about
-- appointment, because they defeat every route into guardianship the Act
-- provides. "subject to the provisions of this Act" in s 6(1)-(2) is what
-- lets them do so.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
DECIDE `the standing of the guardian under this Act` IS
    BRANCH IF `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` guardian
           THEN `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
           IF `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` guardian
           THEN `he is dead or refuses to act`
           OTHERWISE `the standing according to how the guardian was appointed` guardian infant

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
DECIDE `the standing according to how the guardian was appointed` IS
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        THEN `the standing of a parent who holds by virtue of surviving the other parent` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the father by deed`
        THEN `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent by deed or will` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the father by will`
        THEN `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent by deed or will` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by deed`
        THEN `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent by deed or will` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the mother by will`
        THEN `the standing of a guardian appointed by a parent by deed or will` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`
        THEN `the standing of a guardian appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` guardian infant
    WHEN `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        THEN `he acts as guardian of the infant, the infant having no parent`
    WHEN `appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian`
        THEN `he acts as guardian in place of a guardian removed by the court`

GIVEN standing IS A `The standing of a guardian under this Act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` IF
    CONSIDER standing
    WHEN `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`                      THEN FALSE
    WHEN `he is dead or refuses to act`                                                THEN FALSE
    WHEN `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`  THEN FALSE
    -- s 7(6)'s first limb IS answered here, so he acts; it is only the person
    -- he acts jointly with that the facts do not fix.
    WHEN `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`  THEN FALSE
    WHEN `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `he holds by virtue of surviving the other parent` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `how the guardian was appointed`
    WHEN `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN parent IS A MAYBE Person
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the reference for the parent, if in evidence` IS
    CONSIDER parent
    WHEN NOTHING THEN EMPTY
    WHEN JUST p  THEN LIST (p's `the reference for this person`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(2); s 10
-- s 6 makes the survivor guardian BY FORCE OF THE SECTION, whether or not a
-- `Guardian` record has been made for him. So the survivor is added here,
-- and any `Guardian` record whose appointment route is
-- `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` is excluded from the
-- traversal below, or the same parent would be counted twice.
--
-- AND s 10 IS READ HERE, which is the whole reason the "holds" decisions
-- exist: the excluded record is where a removal of the parent is written
-- down, so if this route did not read it, a removed parent would be counted
-- and nothing anywhere would say otherwise.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the surviving parent who is guardian under section 6` IS
    BRANCH IF `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` infant
           THEN `the reference for the parent, if in evidence` (infant's `the mother of the infant`)
           IF `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` infant
           THEN `the reference for the parent, if in evidence` (infant's `the father of the infant`)
           OTHERWISE EMPTY

GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
GIVETH A MAYBE STRING
DECIDE `the reference for the guardian if he acts as guardian of the infant` IS
    IF `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant`
         (`the standing of the guardian under this Act` guardian infant)
    THEN JUST (`the reference for the guardian` guardian)
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(3), s 7(1)-(6), s 10
-- THE ANSWER THE ACT IS FOR: given who died and who was appointed, who are
-- the guardians of this infant.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
DECIDE `the persons who are guardians of the infant` IS
    append (`the surviving parent who is guardian under section 6` infant)
           (mapMaybe (GIVEN g YIELD `the reference for the guardian if he acts as guardian of the infant` g infant)
                     (filter (GIVEN g YIELD NOT `he holds by virtue of surviving the other parent` g)
                             (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)))


§§ `8. Dispute between joint guardians`

-- s 8: "Where 2 or more persons act as joint guardians of an infant and they
--       are unable to agree on any question affecting the welfare of the
--       infant, any of them may apply to the court for its direction, and
--       the court may make such order regarding the matters in difference as
--       it may think proper including power — (a) ... (b) ... (c) ..."
--
-- THE GATE IS ENCODED; THE ORDER IS NOT. "such order regarding the matters
-- in difference as it may think proper" is a discretion, and paragraphs (a)
-- and (b) are discretions inside it -- "as, having regard to the welfare of
-- the infant, the court may think fit" and "as, having regard to the means
-- of the mother or father, the court may consider reasonable". Paragraph (c)
-- ("to vary or discharge any order previously made under this section") adds
-- no computable output either.
--
-- ACTING AS JOINT GUARDIANS IS NOT MEMBERSHIP OF A LIST. Two guardians of one
-- infant may act separately -- a guardian of the person and a guardian of
-- the property do exactly that -- so the fact is read from each guardian's
-- own `alone or jointly`, which is what `family-domain` declares it for.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 8 -- "Where 2 or more persons act as joint guardians of an infant"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian acts jointly with one or more other persons` IF
    CONSIDER guardian's `alone or jointly`
    WHEN `alone` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `jointly with` `the references` THEN count `the references` AT LEAST 1

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 8
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` IF
    any (GIVEN g YIELD     `the guardian acts jointly with one or more other persons` g
                       AND `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant`
                             (`the standing of the guardian under this Act` g infant))
        (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 8 -- "and they are unable to agree on any question affecting the welfare of the infant, any of them may apply to the court for its direction"
-- Disagreement is a GIVEN and not a field on any record: it is a fact about
-- an occasion, not about a person, and the shared ontology records no
-- occasions.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
      `they are unable to agree on a question affecting the welfare of the infant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `any of them may apply to the court for its direction under section 8` IF
        `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` infant
    AND `they are unable to agree on a question affecting the welfare of the infant`


§§ `9. Enforcement of orders for payment of money`

-- s 9(1) in full:
--   "When the court has made any order under this Act for payment of money,
--    the court shall, in addition to any other powers for enforcing
--    compliance with the order, have power, in any case where there is any
--    pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made
--    and capable of being attached, after giving the person by whom the
--    pension or income is payable an opportunity of being heard, to order
--    that such part as the court may think fit of any such pension or income
--    be attached and paid to the persons named by the court and such further
--    order shall be an authority to the person by whom such pension or income
--    is payable to make the payment so ordered, and the receipt of the person
--    to whom the payment is ordered to be made shall be a good discharge to
--    the person by whom the pension or income is payable."
--
-- HALF DISCRETION, HALF RULE -- and an earlier draft of this module wrote
-- the whole subsection off as "nothing follows from facts", which was wrong
-- twice over. What is discretionary is the SIZE of the attachment ("such
-- part as the court may think fit") and the PAYEES ("the persons named by
-- the court"); neither is encoded. What is determinate is the GATE on the
-- power and the two things the further order, once made, "shall be".
--
--   THE GATE. Four plain facts, and the last is a step the court must have
--   taken: an order under this Act for payment of money; a pension or income
--   payable to the person against whom the order was made; that pension or
--   income being "capable of being attached"; and the payer having been given
--   "an opportunity of being heard". They are conjoined below in the
--   subsection's own order. (An earlier draft of this note counted three,
--   running "payable ... and capable of being attached" together as one; the
--   record has always split them, because they fail separately, and the count
--   here was simply wrong.)
--
--   THE TWO CONSEQUENCES. Both open with "shall": the further order "shall
--   be an authority to the person by whom such pension or income is payable
--   to make the payment so ordered", and "the receipt of the person to whom
--   the payment is ordered to be made shall be a good discharge to the
--   person by whom the pension or income is payable". Each follows from the
--   order having been made, which is the same shape as s 7(4) -- the
--   discretion is an INPUT and the mapping from it is the rule.
--
-- WHAT THIS DISCHARGE IS NOT. It is a good discharge to the PENSION PAYER,
-- not to a personal representative and not to anybody paying an infant. It
-- is therefore NOT a third route past s 17, which is about a guardian's
-- power to receipt capital moneys of the infant; it is the same SHAPE as the
-- s 19(1) rule noted under s 19 below, and is noted here for the same
-- reason -- so that a reader can see every place in the Act where a receipt
-- is made good by statute.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 9(1)
DECLARE `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` HAS
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached`                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 9(1) -- "the court shall ... have power, in any case where there is any pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made and capable of being attached, after giving the person by whom the pension or income is payable an opportunity of being heard, to order that such part as the court may think fit of any such pension or income be attached"
GIVEN application IS AN `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` IF
        "When the court has made any order under this Act for payment of money,"
    ... application's `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money`
    ... "in any case where there is any pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made"
    ... application's `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made`
    ... "and capable of being attached,"
    ... application's `the pension or income is capable of being attached`
    ... "after giving the person by whom the pension or income is payable an opportunity of being heard,"
    AND application's `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 9(1) -- "such further order shall be an authority to the person by whom such pension or income is payable to make the payment so ordered"
-- WHICH part is attached and to WHOM is the discretion, and is not computed;
-- that the order authorises the payer to pay is not a discretion at all.
GIVEN `the court has made the further order attaching the pension or income` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the further order is an authority to the person by whom the pension or income is payable to make the payment so ordered` IF
    `the court has made the further order attaching the pension or income`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 9(1) -- "the receipt of the person to whom the payment is ordered to be made shall be a good discharge to the person by whom the pension or income is payable"
GIVEN `the court has made the further order attaching the pension or income` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person to whom the payment is ordered to be made has given a receipt for the payment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the receipt is a good discharge to the person by whom the pension or income is payable` IF
        `the court has made the further order attaching the pension or income`
    AND `the person to whom the payment is ordered to be made has given a receipt for the payment`

-- NOT ENCODED, s 9(1), the SIZE and the PAYEES: "such part as the court may
-- think fit of any such pension or income be attached and paid to the
-- persons named by the court". Neither follows from facts.
-- NOT ENCODED, s 9(2) -- "This section shall be binding on the Government."
-- -- a statement about whom s 9 binds. It has no antecedent and no output,
-- so there is nothing to decide; encoding it as a constant TRUE would be a
-- decision that never decides anything.


§§ `10. Removal of guardian`

-- THE POWER IS NOT ENCODED; THE EFFECT IS. s 10 -- "The court may remove
-- from his guardianship any guardian, and may appoint another guardian in
-- his place." -- is a discretion twice over, and nothing in the section says
-- when it is exercised. What IS determinate is what a removal does, and that
-- is read everywhere above: `the guardian has been removed from his
-- guardianship by the court` is the first test in
-- `the standing of the guardian under this Act`, and a removed guardian is
-- excluded from every count of appointees in ss 6 and 7. The replacement
-- appointee has his own standing member.
--
-- INCLUDING A REMOVED PARENT. s 10 says "any guardian", and s 6(1)-(2) make
-- the surviving parent's guardianship "subject to the provisions of this
-- Act", so the parent is removable like anybody else. An earlier draft read
-- s 10 on appointees only, and the claim in the paragraph above was false as
-- to the one guardian the Act creates without an appointment. That is what
-- `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under
-- section 10` and the two "holds the guardianship" decisions under s 6 are
-- for.


§§ `11. Matters to be considered`

-- s 11: "The court, in exercising the powers conferred by this Act, must
--        have regard primarily to the welfare of the infant, and must
--        consider —
--          (a) where the infant has a parent or parents — the wishes of the
--              parent or both of them, as the case may be; and
--          (b) where the infant is of an age to express an independent
--              opinion — the wishes of the infant."   [Act 18 of 2023 wef 31/01/2024]
--
-- WHAT IS DETERMINATE IS *WHICH* MATTERS, NOT WHAT WEIGHT. "must have regard
-- primarily to the welfare of the infant" is the s 3 direction again and is
-- no more decidable here than there. But (a) and (b) are each governed by a
-- "where" clause that is a plain question of fact, so the CONTENTS of the
-- list the court must consider are computable even though what the court
-- makes of them is not.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 11(a)-(b)
DECLARE `A matter the court must consider under section 11` IS ONE OF
    `the wishes of the parent or both of them, as the case may be`
    `the wishes of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 11(a) -- "where the infant has a parent or parents"
-- A parent NOT IN EVIDENCE is not a parent the infant "has" for this
-- purpose. That is the same discipline s 6(3) uses in the other direction:
-- a missing record never manufactures a fact, whether the fact would help
-- or hinder. It is recorded in the ambiguity register because the two
-- readings differ on a real case -- a father whose whereabouts are unknown.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the infant has a parent or parents` IF
        `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (infant's `the father of the infant`)
    OR  `the parent is in evidence and remains alive` (infant's `the mother of the infant`)

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 11
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A LIST OF `A matter the court must consider under section 11`
DECIDE `the matters the court must consider under section 11` IS
    append `paragraph (a), if the infant has a parent or parents`
           `paragraph (b), if the infant is of an age to express an independent opinion`
    WHERE
        `paragraph (a), if the infant has a parent or parents` MEANS
            IF   `the infant has a parent or parents` infant
            THEN LIST `the wishes of the parent or both of them, as the case may be`
            ELSE EMPTY
        `paragraph (b), if the infant is of an age to express an independent opinion` MEANS
            IF   infant's `of an age to express an independent opinion`
            THEN LIST `the wishes of the infant`
            ELSE EMPTY


§§ `11A. Court to have regard to advice of welfare officers, etc.`

-- NOT ENCODED. s 11A -- "the court shall, whenever it is practicable, have
-- regard to the advice of a person, whether or not a public officer, who is
-- trained or experienced in child welfare but shall not be bound to follow
-- such advice." -- has no determinate output on either half. The duty is
-- conditioned on practicability, which is a judgement, and its content is to
-- "have regard to", which is weight. The closing words state a
-- NON-obligation: they take away a duty that was never imposed, so there is
-- nothing to decide there either.


§§ `12. Variation of trusts for maintenance of infant`

-- NOT ENCODED. "the court may order the whole or any part of the sum so
-- payable under the trust to be paid to the person to whose care the infant
-- is committed, to be applied by that person for the benefit of the infant
-- in such manner as, having regard to the terms of the trust, the court may
-- direct." A discretion as to whether, as to how much ("the whole or any
-- part"), and as to the manner. Its antecedent also reaches outside this Act
-- entirely, to the terms of a trust this cleanroom does not model.


§§ `13. Production of infant`
§§ `14. Placing infant in custody of guardian`

-- NOT ENCODED. Both are procedural discretions. s 13: the court "may ...
-- direct any person appearing to have the custody of an infant to produce
-- the infant" and "make such order for the temporary custody and protection
-- of the infant as the court thinks fit". s 14: where an infant "leaves, or
-- is removed from, the custody of his lawful guardian, the court may order
-- that he be returned to such custody". Who a "lawful guardian" is IS
-- computed above, by `the persons who are guardians of the infant`; what the
-- court does about it is not.


§§ `15. Security to be given`

-- s 15(1): "Where a person appointed by the court is the guardian of an
--           infant’s property, he shall unless the court otherwise orders,
--           give security in such sum as may be appointed for the due
--           performance of his duties as such guardian."   [27/2014]
--
-- A DUTY WITH A DISPENSATION, and the same shape as ss 16-18: the burden is
-- on the guardian, and the court's order is the exception. The AMOUNT ("in
-- such sum as may be appointed") is not computable and is not computed; what
-- is computable is whether security is owed at all, and whether it was
-- given.
--
-- WHY ss 15-18 DO NOT READ THE STANDING, though ss 6, 7, 8 and 20 do, and
-- though s 6(3)'s "no guardian of the person" was repaired by making it do
-- so. The question was asked of each of them and the answer is the same: it
-- would change what the module says about a guardian the court has since
-- removed, and change it for the worse. s 16(2) exposes to avoidance "Any
-- disposal of an infant’s property in contravention of this section"; if a
-- removed guardian ceased to be "A guardian of the property of an infant" for
-- the purposes of s 16(1), the disposal he made while serving would cease to
-- be a contravention and s 16(2) would have nothing to bite on. The records
-- these four sections read are records of things DONE -- security given, a
-- lease granted, a discharge purported, a sum applied -- and none of them
-- carries a date, so there is no way to ask whether the thing was done while
-- he was still a guardian. ss 6, 7 and 20 ask instead who IS guardian now,
-- which is a question about the present and is what the standing answers.
-- The consequence, stated so nobody has to rediscover it: these four
-- decisions answer about the guardianship as the record describes it, and a
-- case file that records a removal must not be read as unwinding what was
-- done before it.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 15(1)
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` IF
        "Where a person appointed by the court is the guardian of an infant’s property,"
    ... `a person appointed by the court` guardian
    ... `a guardian of the property of the infant` guardian
    ... "he shall unless the court otherwise orders, give security"
    AND NOT guardian's `the court has otherwise ordered as to security`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 15(1)
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has not given the security section 15(1) requires` IF
        `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` guardian
    AND NOT guardian's `has given security`

-- NOT ENCODED, s 15(2): "Such security shall be given in the manner
-- prescribed for the time being in the case of receivers appointed by the
-- court; and the guardian so appointed shall pass his accounts at such
-- periods as may be ordered, and shall pay any balance certified to be due
-- from him into court in the manner prescribed in the case of receivers."
-- Three duties whose whole content is fixed elsewhere -- by the practice as
-- to receivers, and by orders of the court ("at such periods as may be
-- ordered"). Nothing in the Act says what the manner or the periods are, so
-- there is nothing here to compute.


§§ `16. Limitation of guardian's powers`

-- s 16(1): "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not, without the
--           permission of the court —
--             (a) sell, mortgage, exchange, or otherwise part with the
--                 possession of any of the movable or immovable property of
--                 the infant; or
--             (b) lease any land belonging to the infant for a term
--                 exceeding one year."   [27/2014] [Act 25 of 2021 wef 15/10/2024]
--
-- A PROHIBITION WITH A PERMISSION EXCEPTION AND A NUMERIC THRESHOLD, and the
-- shape matters. The subsection does NOT say that a guardian may deal with
-- the property if the court permits: it says he SHALL NOT, unless permitted.
-- So
-- the decision below is named for, and returns TRUE on, the CONTRAVENTION --
-- the burden sits on the guardian to produce the permission, and a guardian
-- about whom nothing is recorded is in breach, not in the clear.
--
-- THE THRESHOLD IS ON LIMB (b) ONLY. Limb (a) bites on any parting with
-- possession whatever; limb (b) bites only on a lease "for a term exceeding
-- one year", so a one-year lease is outside the prohibition altogether and
-- needs no permission. "exceeding" is strict: a term of exactly one year
-- does not exceed one year.
--
-- WHERE THE TERM IS NOT IN EVIDENCE the prohibition is not made out. That is
-- the conservative reading and it follows the shape of the limb: what s 16
-- forbids is a lease of more than a year, and a lease of unknown length has
-- not been shown to be one. It is in the ambiguity register.
--
-- THE AMENDMENT MARKERS. The source prints two under s 16(1), "[27/2014]"
-- and "[Act 25 of 2021 wef 15/10/2024]". The second is the more recent and,
-- on the source's own legislative history, Act 25 of 2021 is the "Courts
-- (Civil and Criminal Justice) Reform Act 2021" whose "Commencement" is
-- given as "15 October 2024". So the words above -- including "one year" --
-- are the text in force as amended, notwithstanding that the volume is the
-- "2020 REVISED EDITION" which "incorporates all amendments up to and
-- including 1 December 2021". The markers do not say WHICH words each
-- amendment touched, and the source carries no earlier text, so this module
-- does not claim to know what the limb said before.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(1)(b) -- "lease any land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year"
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` IF
        guardian's `has leased land belonging to the infant`
    AND `the term of the lease exceeds one year`
    WHERE
        `the term of the lease exceeds one year` MEANS
            CONSIDER guardian's `the term of the lease, in years`
            WHEN NOTHING              THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST `the term` THEN `the term` GREATER THAN 1

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(1)(a)-(b)
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has done a thing section 16(1) forbids` IF
        "(a) sell, mortgage, exchange, or otherwise part with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant; or"
    ..  guardian's `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant`
    ..  "(b) lease any land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year."
    OR  `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` guardian

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(1) -- "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not, without the permission of the court"
-- ONE PERMISSION FLAG COVERS BOTH LIMBS, AND THAT IS A SIMPLIFICATION.
-- `family-domain`'s `Guardian` carries a single
-- `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant`,
-- so a guardian permitted to grant a long lease under limb (b) reads as
-- permitted to sell under limb (a) as well. s 16(3) tells against that --
-- the court "shall not make any order under subsection (1) unless it is
-- necessary or advisable in the interests of the infant", and necessity is
-- judged of the dealing proposed, not at large -- so the true shape is one
-- permission per dealing. Splitting it would mean adding a field to the
-- shared ontology that three other Act modules already build against, so it
-- is recorded here and in the ambiguity register rather than done here.
-- The direction of the error is worth naming: it is permissive, so a case
-- file must not record a limb (b) permission unless the court's permission
-- in fact covered the dealing in question.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` IF
        "A guardian of the property of an infant"
    ... `a guardian of the property of the infant` guardian
    ... `the guardian has done a thing section 16(1) forbids` guardian
    ... "shall not, without the permission of the court"
    AND NOT guardian's `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(2) -- "Any disposal of an infant’s property in contravention of this section may be declared void"
-- THE ANTECEDENT ONLY. Whether the disposal IS declared void is a discretion
-- ("may be declared void"), and what follows a declaration is another one
-- ("the court may make such order as appears requisite for restoring to the
-- infant’s estate the property so disposed of"). What is determinate is
-- which disposals are exposed to it.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the disposal is one that may be declared void under section 16(2)` IF
    `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` guardian

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(3) -- "The court shall not make any order under subsection (1) unless it is necessary or advisable in the interests of the infant."
-- A NEGATIVE CONSTRAINT ON THE COURT, not a power. It does not tell the
-- court when to permit; it tells it when it may not, and that IS
-- determinate: absent necessity or advisability in the infant's interests,
-- the permission may not be given. The converse does not follow -- necessity
-- does not oblige the court to permit -- so nothing here says a permission
-- must issue.
--
-- "any order under subsection (1)": subsection (1) speaks of "the permission
-- of the court" and mentions no order. The reading taken is that the
-- permission is the order s 16(3) means, since it is the only thing
-- subsection (1) lets the court do. See the ambiguity register.
GIVEN `it is necessary or advisable in the interests of the infant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 16(3) forbids the court to permit the dealing` IF
    NOT `it is necessary or advisable in the interests of the infant`


§§ `17. Guardian may not give discharge for capital property`

-- s 17: "A guardian of the property of an infant shall not, unless in any
--        case the court otherwise orders, be empowered to give a good
--        discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys payable to or
--        receivable by an infant."   [27/2014]
--
-- THIS IS THE ACT'S DIRECT BITE ON SUCCESSION. An infant's share of an
-- estate is "capital moneys payable to or receivable by an infant" in the
-- section's own words, and a legacy is named expressly. So s 17 is what
-- stands between an infant being ENTITLED to a share and the share being
-- PAID OVER: the personal representative who pays an infant's legacy to the
-- infant's guardian does not get a good discharge for it, and remains
-- accountable, unless the court has ordered otherwise in that case.
--
-- The words "unless in any case the court otherwise orders" are
-- case-by-case on their
-- face -- "in any case" -- so a standing dispensation is not contemplated;
-- the recorded fact is read as an order in this case.
--
-- THE DEFAULT IS NO POWER. The subsection withholds a power rather than
-- imposing a duty, so the encoding says what is NOT so, and a guardian about
-- whom nothing is recorded has no power.
--
-- WHERE THE LEGACY AND THE CAPITAL MONEYS COME FROM, and who says so. s 17
-- presupposes an entitlement it does not create. Two of the other three Acts
-- create it and neither of them says anything about paying it over:
--   THE INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967 s 5 distributes the property "among the
--     persons entitled to succeed beneficially to that property or the
--     proceeds thereof", and intestate-succession-act.l4 computes exactly
--     that in `the persons entitled to succeed beneficially, and their
--     shares`. Entitlement, and no more: nothing in that Act is about
--     discharging the personal representative who pays.
--   THE WILLS ACT 1838 makes the dispositions, and wills-act.l4 computes
--     them. Its own infants are elsewhere -- s 4's testator, and s 27(3)'s
--     appointment of a guardian -- and neither is about payment.
--   THE PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT 1934 is where payment would live, and
--     probate-administration-act.l4 does not reach distribution: its ss 57-58
--     and both Schedules are out of scope on its own statement.
-- So among the four modules the pointer runs in ONE direction, out of this
-- file: s 17 is the only provision any of them encodes that stands between
-- being entitled to a share and being validly paid it. Named again in
-- `Where this Act joins the other three` below. (The Intestate Succession Act
-- sentence is quoted from source/ISA1967.txt, not from this Act.)
--
-- WHAT s 17 DOES NOT DECIDE, so that the direction of the join is not
-- overstated: it does not stop the infant being entitled, and it does not
-- stop the money being paid. It decides that the payer does not get a good
-- discharge for paying it to the guardian -- so the personal representative
-- remains accountable -- unless the court has ordered otherwise in the case.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` IF
        "A guardian of the property of an infant"
    ... `a guardian of the property of the infant` guardian
    ... "shall not, unless in any case the court otherwise orders, be empowered to give a good discharge for any legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by an infant."
    AND NOT guardian's `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 17
-- The ontology's field is named `has given a good discharge ...`; what it
-- records is that the guardian PURPORTED to discharge, which is the fact a
-- witness can depose to. Whether the discharge is good is what s 17 decides,
-- and this is where it is decided.
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` IF
        `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` guardian
    AND guardian's `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant`


§§ `18. Guardian may support infant out of income`

-- s 18(1): "A guardian of the property of an infant may make reasonable
--           provision out of the income of such property for his maintenance
--           and education, having regard to his station in life; but no sum
--           exceeding $100 per month may be so applied without the
--           permission of the court."   [27/2014] [Act 25 of 2021 wef 15/10/2024]
--
-- ONE HALF IS A DISCRETION AND THE OTHER IS ARITHMETIC. What counts as
-- "reasonable provision ... having regard to his station in life" is not
-- computable and is not computed. The cap after the semicolon is: "no sum
-- exceeding $100 per month may be so applied without the permission of the
-- court" is a rate, a comparison and an exception.
--
-- THE FIGURE, AND ITS MARKERS. $100 a month is a strikingly small sum for a
-- child's maintenance and education, and the reader's first instinct will be
-- that it is a 1934 figure never updated. The source does not support that
-- instinct and does not refute it. The subsection carries the SAME two
-- markers as s 16(1) -- "[27/2014]" and "[Act 25 of 2021 wef 15/10/2024]" --
-- so the words above are the text in force after an amendment commencing 15
-- October 2024, ninety years after the Act. What the markers do NOT say is
-- which words each amendment touched: the source gives no earlier text, so
-- whether "$100" is the 1934 figure carried forward or a figure the 2021 Act
-- set is not answerable from this document. The encoding uses the figure the
-- in-force text states and claims nothing more.
--
-- A RATE IS NOT AN AMOUNT, which is why the ontology gives the provision a
-- period as well as a sum. $100 is not the cap; $100 PER MONTH is.
--
-- AND THE ACT SUPPLIES NO CONVERSION. A weekly provision cannot be compared
-- with a monthly cap without a rule about how many weeks are in a month, and
-- s 18(1) has none. The reading taken here is the calendar one -- twelve
-- months to fifty-two weeks, so a weekly sum is multiplied by 52/12 -- and
-- it is flagged, because a four-weeks-to-the-month reading gives a
-- different answer between $23.08 and $25.00 a week. An "other periodical"
-- provision is not converted at all; the verdict says so instead of
-- guessing.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 18(1) -- "no sum exceeding $100 per month may be so applied without the permission of the court"
DECLARE `Whether the provision exceeds the cap in section 18(1)` IS ONE OF
    `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
    `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
    `the period of the sum applied is neither weekly nor monthly, and the Act supplies no conversion to a monthly rate`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 18(1)
GIVEN provision IS AN `A periodical sum`
GIVETH A `Whether the provision exceeds the cap in section 18(1)`
DECIDE `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` IS
    CONSIDER provision's `the period`
    WHEN `monthly` THEN
        IF   provision's `the sum` GREATER THAN 100
        THEN `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
        ELSE `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
    WHEN `weekly` THEN
        IF   provision's `the sum` TIMES (52 DIVIDED BY 12) GREATER THAN 100
        THEN `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
        ELSE `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
    WHEN `other periodical` THEN
        `the period of the sum applied is neither weekly nor monthly, and the Act supplies no conversion to a monthly rate`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 18(1)
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian has applied a sum exceeding $100 per month without the permission of the court` IF
        `a guardian of the property of the infant` guardian
    AND `the sum applied exceeds the cap`
    AND NOT guardian's `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month`
    WHERE
        `the sum applied exceeds the cap` MEANS
            CONSIDER `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court`
                       (guardian's `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education`)
            WHEN `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 18(2): "Where the income of the infant’s property in the hands of the
--           guardian is insufficient for such purpose, or money is required
--           for the infant’s advancement, the court may order that provision
--           for such purpose be made out of the capital of the infant’s
--           property, and for such purpose may authorise the sale or
--           mortgage of any part of the infant’s property, and give such
--           directions in regard thereto as may be necessary in the
--           interests of the infant."   [27/2014]
--
-- TWO DETERMINATE THINGS, AND ONE DISCRETION. Determinate: the gate, which
-- is a plain disjunction of two facts; and the NEGATIVE that the subsection
-- implies and s 18(1) confirms -- the guardian's own power runs to income
-- only, so capital is reached by ORDER OF THE COURT and not otherwise.
-- Discretionary: whether the order is made, and the authorisation of a sale
-- or mortgage that goes with it.
--
-- INSUFFICIENCY IS A GIVEN, NOT A COMPUTATION, and that is a gap worth
-- naming. `Infant`'s `the income of the property of the infant` is a bare
-- `Money` with no period attached, while the provision it would have to be
-- compared against is a rate. Dollars cannot be compared with dollars per
-- month, so insufficiency is taken as construed rather than computed from a
-- figure that does not carry its own period.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 18(2) -- "Where the income of the infant’s property in the hands of the guardian is insufficient for such purpose, or money is required for the infant’s advancement"
GIVEN `the income of the infant's property in the hands of the guardian is insufficient for such purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `money is required for the infant's advancement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may order provision to be made out of the capital of the infant's property under section 18(2)` IF
        `the income of the infant's property in the hands of the guardian is insufficient for such purpose`
    OR  `money is required for the infant's advancement`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 16(1), s 18(1)-(2)
-- A CONCLUSION DRAWN FROM THREE PROVISIONS, NOT A RULE ANY ONE OF THEM
-- STATES, and it is stated as a constant because the Act gives the guardian
-- no route to capital in any circumstances, not because the question never
-- arises. The inference: s 18(1) empowers the guardian over INCOME and says
-- so expressly ("out of the income of such property"); s 18(2) supplies the
-- capital route and puts it in the hands of the COURT ("the court may order
-- that provision for such purpose be made out of the capital"); and s 16(1)
-- forbids him, without the permission of the court, to "sell, mortgage,
-- exchange, or otherwise part with the possession of any of the movable or
-- immovable property of the infant", which is how capital would have to be
-- realised. Whatever permission he holds under s 18(1) is about the SIZE of
-- a payment out of income, not about its source.
--
-- IT IS AN EXPRESSIO UNIUS INFERENCE and the competing reading is real:
-- that s 18(2) adds a court route without withdrawing any power of the
-- guardian's own, leaving s 16(1)'s permission requirement to do all the
-- work -- on which reading a guardian WITH the court's permission to sell
-- could apply the proceeds himself. Entered in the ambiguity register.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the guardian may of his own motion make provision out of the capital of the infant's property` MEANS FALSE


§§ `19. Special order in case of small estate`

-- NOT ENCODED, and it is the most thoroughly discretionary section in the
-- Act. s 19(1) fires only "If it appears that, having regard to the station
-- in life of an infant and to the value of his property and to all the
-- circumstances of the case, it would be expedient" -- three open-ended
-- considerations and an expediency test -- and the person then appointed
-- deals with the property "in his sole and uncontrolled discretion". s 19(2)
-- ("Any person so appointed may be ordered by the court to render an
-- account") and s 19(3) ("The court may for any sufficient reason discharge
-- any order, or revoke any appointment") are discretions on top of it.
--
-- The section's own words also put its appointee OUTSIDE the guardianship
-- this module computes: the court acts "instead of appointing a guardian of
-- the property of the infant". He is therefore not a `Guardian`, has no
-- member of `How the guardian was appointed`, and does not appear in
-- `the persons who are guardians of the infant`. `family-domain.l4` reaches
-- the same conclusion for the same reason, and says so.
--
-- One thing in s 19(1) is determinate and is still not encoded, because it
-- is the consequence of an order that cannot be predicted: "the receipt of
-- the person so appointed shall be a good discharge to any person making any
-- payment or transfer of any property to him on behalf of the infant". It is
-- noted here so that a reader comparing s 19 with s 17 can see that the Act
-- does provide one route by which a payment on an infant's behalf gets a
-- good discharge without a s 17 order -- but only where a s 19(1) order has
-- been made.


§§ `20. Application for opinion, etc.`

-- s 20: "Any guardian may apply to the court for the court’s opinion, advice
--        or direction on any question respecting the management or
--        administration of the infant’s property."   [27/2014]
--
-- DETERMINATE AS TO WHO AND AS TO WHAT. "Any guardian" -- not, as in ss 16,
-- 17 and 18(1), "A guardian of the property of an infant" -- so a guardian
-- of the person may apply too. The SUBJECT-MATTER is confined instead: the
-- question must respect "the management or administration of the infant’s
-- property". What opinion the court gives is not encoded and could not be.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 20
GIVEN guardian IS A Guardian
      infant   IS AN Infant
      `the question respects the management or administration of the property of the infant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the guardian may apply to the court for its opinion, advice or direction under section 20` IF
        "Any guardian"
    ... `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant`
          (`the standing of the guardian under this Act` guardian infant)
    ... "on any question respecting the management or administration of the infant’s property."
    AND `the question respects the management or administration of the property of the infant`


§§ `21. Exception for money paid into a District Court`

-- s 21: "Unless the court otherwise orders, nothing in this Act shall apply
--        to any money paid into a District Court to the credit of an infant
--        in any action or proceeding in a District Court or to any money in a
--        District Court ordered to be transferred to the credit of an
--        infant, but the same shall be dealt with according to the Rules of
--        Court."   [16/93; 5/2014; 27/2014]
--
-- AN APPLICATION GATE ON THE WHOLE ACT, and the only provision in it that
-- can switch every other provision off. Note the direction of the exception:
-- the Act does NOT apply to such money, and the court's order brings it back
-- in -- so a court order here is an INCLUSION, the mirror image of ss 15-18
-- where the court's order is a dispensation.
--
-- "THE COURT" IN s 21 IS s 2's COURT, and s 21 is not about it: the money
-- sits in a District Court, which s 2 excludes from the defined term (see
-- s 2 above and the `Forum` type). So the money is in one forum and the
-- order that reaches it comes from another.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 21
DECLARE `Money to which this Act might apply` IS ONE OF
    `money paid into a District Court to the credit of an infant in any action or proceeding in a District Court`
    `money in a District Court ordered to be transferred to the credit of an infant`
    `money of neither kind described in section 21`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 21
DECLARE `Whether this Act applies to the money` IS ONE OF
    `nothing in this Act applies to the money, and the same shall be dealt with according to the Rules of Court`
    `this Act applies to the money`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 21
GIVEN money IS A `Money to which this Act might apply`
      `the court otherwise orders` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Whether this Act applies to the money`
DECIDE `whether this Act applies to the money` IS
    IF   `the court otherwise orders`
    THEN `this Act applies to the money`
    ELSE CONSIDER money
         WHEN `money of neither kind described in section 21` THEN `this Act applies to the money`
         OTHERWISE `nothing in this Act applies to the money, and the same shall be dealt with according to the Rules of Court`


§§ `The Act as a whole -- the infant it presupposes, and the record it reads`

-- TWO THINGS THAT SIT ACROSS THE SECTIONS RATHER THAN IN ANY ONE OF THEM.
-- Neither is a rule of this Act, and both say so.
--
-- 1. THE INFANT THE ACT PRESUPPOSES. Every operative section above that names
--    its subject at all names "an infant" -- ss 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 11 and 12 to
--    21 -- and not one of the decisions above re-tests that its subject is
--    one: they take an `Infant`, and the record IS the construal. (ss 5A and
--    11A say "a child" instead, and this module encodes s 5A over its own
--    `A breach of an access order` and never over `Infant`, precisely because
--    the Act does not say whether the two words denote the same class. See
--    the s 5A note above and ambiguity register entry 11.)
--    `family-domain.l4` says in terms that
--    `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` is an
--    already-construed input, and this Act supplies no age with which a
--    re-test could be made (ambiguity register entry 1).
--
--    THAT IS A CHOICE AND IT HAS A COST, which is stated rather than left for
--    a reader to find. A case file that puts a person of full age into an
--    `Infant` gets s 6(1)'s surviving parent made his guardian and ss 15 to 18
--    applied to his property, and nothing in the sections above objects. So
--    the gate is written HERE, once, over the answer the Act is for, and a
--    consumer that cares about the date runs this decision rather than
--    `the persons who are guardians of the infant` directly.
--
--    WHY ONCE, AND NOT THREADED THROUGH EVERY SECTION -- set down so that a
--    second formalizer can differ knowingly. probate-administration-act.l4
--    does thread infancy through its provisions, and is right to: its s 21(1)
--    bars a grant "while he is an infant", its s 6(2) turns on whether "there
--    is a minority", and its s 63(1) lets a payment be made "during the
--    minority of any such person" -- phrases that fix infancy TO A MOMENT, so
--    each provision must ask about a different one. THIS Act contains no such
--    phrase. Searching source/GIA1934.txt: "while he is" does not occur;
--    "minority" does not occur; "majority" does not occur; "years of age"
--    does not occur. "An infant" is used throughout as a class term; and where
--    this Act does tie an outcome to the CONTINUANCE of a state, the state is
--    a parent's life and not the infant's age -- s 7(3)'s "so long as the
--    mother or father remains alive". One gate over the class is what this
--    Act's own grammar asks for.
--    (The three Probate and Administration Act phrases are quoted from
--    source/PAA1934.txt, not from this Act.)
--
-- 2. THE RECORD, AND WHETHER IT HANGS TOGETHER. `Guardian` carries
--    `the reference for the infant` and `Infant` carries
--    `the guardians of the infant`, and the two can disagree. Every traversal
--    above takes the LIST as authoritative and never reads the
--    back-reference -- see `Where the shared ontology stopped short` below for
--    why that is right -- so a file that put one infant's guardians into
--    another infant's list would have them counted there. The check is
--    written here, as a check on the file and not as a rule of the Act, so
--    that the field has a reader and a combined case file has something to
--    run. probate-administration-act.l4 uses the same shape for its
--    s 29(8)(a), on `Grantee`'s
--    `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is
--    made`, which is the third form this one link takes.

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 -- "infant", used in ss 3 to 21 and defined nowhere in the Act
-- THREE OUTCOMES AND NOT TWO. The third is the not-in-evidence case, and it
-- is a member of its own rather than a fold into either of the others, for
-- the reasons given at `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix` above.
DECLARE `Whether this Act has an infant to govern` IS ONE OF
    `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are`
        HAS `the references for the guardians` IS A LIST OF STRING
    `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`
    `whether the person is an infant on that date cannot be determined, the date on which he attains the age of majority not being in evidence`

@ref Guardianship of Infants Act 1934 s 6(1)-(3), s 7(1)-(6), s 10
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
      `the date in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Whether this Act has an infant to govern`
DECIDE `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` IS
    CONSIDER `the person is an infant on the date in question` (infant's `the infant`) `the date in question`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        `whether the person is an infant on that date cannot be determined, the date on which he attains the age of majority not being in evidence`
    WHEN JUST `he is an infant on that date` THEN
        IF   `he is an infant on that date`
        THEN `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are`
                 (`the persons who are guardians of the infant` infant)
        ELSE `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`

-- NOT A RULE OF THIS ACT. ss 6, 7 and 10 decide who the guardians of an
-- infant are; nothing in the Act says how a file is to record the link
-- between the two records, because the Act has no records. This is an
-- integrity check on a case file, and it is named as one so that nobody reads
-- it as a provision.
GIVEN infant IS AN Infant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` IF
    all (GIVEN g YIELD g's `the reference for the infant`
                       EQUALS infant's `the infant`'s `the reference for this person`)
        (infant's `the guardians of the infant`)

§§ `Worked facts`

-- CONSTRUCTORS, NOT LITERALS. L4 has no record-update operator -- a `WITH`
-- clause works only on a type name, never on an existing value -- so each
-- scenario below would otherwise have to spell nineteen `Guardian` fields
-- and thirteen `Person` fields in full. These constructors take the fields a
-- scenario actually varies and default the rest, which is what keeps the
-- #EVALs readable as facts rather than as forms.

GIVEN `the name of the person`      IS A STRING
      `the reference`               IS A STRING
      `the vital status`            IS A `Vital status`
      `the day of birth`            IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the day of majority`         IS A MAYBE DATE
GIVETH A Person
DECIDE `a GIA person` IS Person WITH
    `name`                                            IS `the name of the person`
    `the reference for this person`                   IS `the reference`
    `date of birth`                                   IS `the day of birth`
    `vital status`                                    IS `the vital status`
    `children`                                        IS EMPTY
    `at the date of the death only conceived in the womb but subsequently born alive` IS FALSE
    `money or other property given, paid or settled by the intestate during his life to or for the advancement of the person` IS 0
    `the date on which the person attains the age of majority` IS `the day of majority`
    `a soldier being in actual military service`      IS FALSE
    `a mariner or seaman being at sea`                IS FALSE
    `a member of any naval or marine forces so circumstanced that if he were a soldier he would be in actual military service` IS FALSE
    `so desires that letters of administration be granted to such person as the court thinks fit` IS FALSE
    `lacks mental capacity (within the meaning of the Mental Capacity Act 2008) to make decisions with respect to the management and administration of the person's property and affairs` IS FALSE
    `absent from Singapore`                           IS FALSE
    `willing to act`                                  IS TRUE

`no GIA provision` MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH
    `the sum`    IS 0
    `the period` IS `monthly`

GIVEN `the appointee`             IS A Person
      `the infant referred to`    IS A STRING
      `the route of appointment`  IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `the kind of guardianship`  IS A `Kind of guardianship`
      `acting alone or jointly`   IS AN `Alone or jointly`
      `refusing to act`           IS A BOOLEAN
      `objected to by the parent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `removed by the court`      IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Guardian
DECIDE `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` IS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the appointee`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the infant referred to`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `the route of appointment`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `the kind of guardianship`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `acting alone or jointly`
    `refuses to act`                     IS `refusing to act`
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS `objected to by the parent`
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS `removed by the court`
    `has given security`                 IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant`        IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`                IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education` IS `no GIA provision`
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

-- s 7(4) HAS TWO LIMBS AND THE CONSTRUCTOR ABOVE FIXES THE SECOND AT FALSE.
-- This one varies both, so that "if the guardian so appointed as aforesaid
-- considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the
-- infant" can be walked on its own rather than only in company with an
-- objection.
GIVEN `the appointee`                IS A Person
      `the infant referred to`       IS A STRING
      `the route of appointment`     IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `objected to by the parent`    IS A BOOLEAN
      `considering the parent unfit` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Guardian
DECIDE `a GIA guardian, on the two limbs of section 7(4)` IS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the appointee`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the infant referred to`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `the route of appointment`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS `objected to by the parent`
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS `considering the parent unfit`
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS FALSE
    `has given security`                 IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant`        IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`                IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS FALSE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education` IS `no GIA provision`
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the appointee`               IS A Person
      `the infant referred to`      IS A STRING
      `the route of appointment`    IS A `How the guardian was appointed`
      `security given`              IS A BOOLEAN
      `security dispensed with`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `parted with possession`      IS A BOOLEAN
      `land leased`                 IS A BOOLEAN
      `the term in years`           IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `permission to deal`          IS A BOOLEAN
      `discharge purportedly given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `discharge otherwise ordered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision applied`       IS AN `A periodical sum`
      `permission to exceed the cap` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Guardian
DECIDE `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case` IS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `the appointee`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS `the infant referred to`
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `the route of appointment`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `guardian of the property of the infant`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS FALSE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS FALSE
    `has given security`                 IS `security given`
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS `security dispensed with`
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS `parted with possession`
    `has leased land belonging to the infant`        IS `land leased`
    `the term of the lease, in years`                IS `the term in years`
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS `permission to deal`
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS `discharge purportedly given`
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS `discharge otherwise ordered`
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education` IS `the provision applied`
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS `permission to exceed the cap`

GIVEN `the child`                 IS A Person
      `the father, if in evidence` IS A MAYBE Person
      `the mother, if in evidence` IS A MAYBE Person
      `the guardians`             IS A LIST OF Guardian
      `another person has parental rights` IS A BOOLEAN
      `of an age to express an opinion`    IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Infant
DECIDE `a GIA infant` IS Infant WITH
    `the infant`                     IS `the child`
    `the father of the infant`       IS `the father, if in evidence`
    `the mother of the infant`       IS `the mother, if in evidence`
    `the guardians of the infant`    IS `the guardians`
    `some other person has parental rights with respect to him` IS `another person has parental rights`
    `the income of the property of the infant`  IS 12_000
    `the capital of the property of the infant` IS 250_000
    `of an age to express an independent opinion` IS `of an age to express an opinion`

§§§ `The people`

`Tan Boon Huat, the father`      MEANS `a GIA person` "Tan Boon Huat" "father"  (`died on` (YMD 2020 3 4)) NOTHING NOTHING
`Tan Boon Huat, still living`    MEANS `a GIA person` "Tan Boon Huat" "father"  `living` NOTHING NOTHING
`Tan Boon Huat, died undated`    MEANS `a GIA person` "Tan Boon Huat" "father"  `dead, the date of death not known` NOTHING NOTHING
`Tan Boon Huat, died first`      MEANS `a GIA person` "Tan Boon Huat" "father"  (`died on` (YMD 2018 2 2)) NOTHING NOTHING
`Lai Peng, the mother`           MEANS `a GIA person` "Lai Peng"      "mother"  `living` NOTHING NOTHING
`Lai Peng, died second`          MEANS `a GIA person` "Lai Peng"      "mother"  (`died on` (YMD 2024 11 11)) NOTHING NOTHING
`Wei Ming, the infant`           MEANS `a GIA person` "Wei Ming"      "infant"  `living` (JUST (YMD 2012 7 19)) (JUST (YMD 2033 7 19))
`Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence` MEANS `a GIA person` "Wei Ming" "infant" `living` (JUST (YMD 2012 7 19)) NOTHING
`Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father`    MEANS `a GIA person` "Lim Kok Wah" "lim"   `living` NOTHING NOTHING
`Lim Kok Wah, dead`                       MEANS `a GIA person` "Lim Kok Wah" "lim"   (`died on` (YMD 2021 5 5)) NOTHING NOTHING
`Siti, appointed by the mother`           MEANS `a GIA person` "Siti"        "siti"  `living` NOTHING NOTHING
`Rajan, appointed by the court`           MEANS `a GIA person` "Rajan"       "rajan" `living` NOTHING NOTHING
`Rajan, dead`                             MEANS `a GIA person` "Rajan"       "rajan" (`died on` (YMD 2023 6 6)) NOTHING NOTHING
-- A SECOND APPOINTEE OF THE FATHER'S. s 6(1)'s second sentence is written for
-- "the guardian or guardians", and until this person existed no file had two
-- appointees of one parent, so the plural was never run.
`Chandra, also appointed by the father`   MEANS `a GIA person` "Chandra"     "chandra" `living` NOTHING NOTHING
-- She predeceases `Tan Boon Huat, the father`, who died 4 March 2020. Every
-- other pair of deaths in this file has the father dying first, so until this
-- person existed `the later of the two deaths` was never once answered with
-- its FIRST argument and could have returned the wrong parent unnoticed.
`Lai Peng, died before the father`        MEANS `a GIA person` "Lai Peng"      "mother" (`died on` (YMD 2019 1 1)) NOTHING NOTHING
-- The same day as `Tan Boon Huat, died first`, so that s 7(6)'s "surviving
-- parent" has no answer for the second of the two reasons the standing type
-- names -- two deaths on one day, rather than a date not known.
`Lai Peng, died the same day as the father` MEANS `a GIA person` "Lai Peng"  "mother" (`died on` (YMD 2018 2 2)) NOTHING NOTHING

§§§ `The guardians`

`Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "mother")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE TRUE FALSE

`Lim, appointed by the father by will, refusing to act` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` TRUE FALSE FALSE

`Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

`Siti, appointed by the mother by deed, acting jointly with Lim` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Siti, appointed by the mother` "infant"
        `appointed by the mother by deed` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "lim")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Siti, appointed by the mother by will` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Siti, appointed by the mother` "infant"
        `appointed by the mother by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant"
        `appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "siti")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant"
        `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        `guardian of the person of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Lim, acting jointly with Siti` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "siti")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Chandra, appointed by the father by will` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Chandra, also appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "mother")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- s 7(1) names TWO instruments -- "by deed or will" -- and `family-domain`
-- splits them because they are proved differently. The deed route had no
-- fixture at all until now.
`Lim, appointed by the father by deed` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by deed` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "mother")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- s 7(4), second limb, with no objection: the appointee's own view of the
-- parent is enough to open the court to him.
`Lim, appointed by the father by deed, considering the mother unfit` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, on the two limbs of section 7(4)` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by deed` FALSE TRUE

-- BOTH s 7(4) LIMBS SET, so that the assertions about WHO may apply cannot
-- pass for the wrong reason: whatever answer these two produce, it is not
-- because the subsection's two triggers are absent.
`Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, on the two limbs of section 7(4)` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` TRUE TRUE

`Rajan, appointed by the court, objecting and considering the parent unfit` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, on the two limbs of section 7(4)` `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant"
        `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent` TRUE TRUE

`Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, dead` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- `jointly with` carrying an EMPTY list: a record that says the guardian
-- acts jointly and names nobody. s 8 asks whether "2 or more persons act as
-- joint guardians", so one is not enough and none is certainly not.
`Lim, appointed by the father by will, recorded as acting jointly with nobody` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` EMPTY) FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent, dead` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Rajan, dead` "infant"
        `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        `guardian of the person of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent, refusing to act` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant"
        `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        `guardian of the person of the infant`
        `alone` TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- s 10, second limb: "may appoint another guardian in his place".
`Siti, appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Siti, appointed by the mother` "infant"
        `appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- THE PARENT'S OWN RECORD. s 6 makes the survivor guardian without any
-- appointment, so a record is not needed to make her one; but there is
-- nowhere else to write down that the court has removed her under s 10, so
-- when it has, the record exists and has to be read.
`the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lai Peng, the mother` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`the mother, removed from her guardianship by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lai Peng, the mother` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

`the father, his guardianship under section 6(2) recorded` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Tan Boon Huat, still living` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

`the father, removed from his guardianship by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Tan Boon Huat, still living` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- A REMOVED PARENT-ROUTE RECORD FOR A PARENT WHO IS NOW DEAD. It exists so
-- that one file can carry a removal of the FATHER while the MOTHER is the
-- one s 6(1) now makes guardian -- which is what tests that
-- `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under
-- section 10` reads WHOSE removal it is and not merely that there was one.
`the father, removed from his guardianship, having since died` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Tan Boon Huat, the father` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- The mirror of `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court`
-- on the mother's side, so that s 6(2)'s own removal filter is exercised.
`Siti, appointed by the mother by will, removed by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Siti, appointed by the mother` "infant"
        `appointed by the mother by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- The mirror of `Lim, appointed by the father by will, refusing to act`, so
-- that s 6(2)'s "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act" limb has a file
-- of its own instead of borrowing the father's.
`Siti, appointed by the mother by will, refusing to act` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Siti, appointed by the mother` "infant"
        `appointed by the mother by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- A RECORD THAT CLAIMS THE PARENT ROUTE FOR SOMEBODY WHO IS NOT A PARENT.
-- `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` is a route on the
-- record, and a record is written by a person; s 6(1) and s 6(2) name the
-- MOTHER and the FATHER, so the route is a claim and s 6 is what decides
-- whether it holds -- of the person named, and not of whoever is on the file.
-- Without that check any record carrying this route would read as guardian
-- the moment s 6 made either parent one.
`Lim, wrongly recorded as guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant"
        `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
        `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- s 16(1)(b) has TWO facts in it -- that land was leased, and for how long --
-- and `family-domain` carries them in two fields, so a file can record a term
-- without a lease: a draft term noted and the lease never granted. The lease
-- fixtures below all set both together, which is why the "has leased" half
-- had nothing testing it.
`a guardian of the property with a three-year term noted but no lease granted` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE (JUST 3) FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

-- REMOVED AND ALSO DEAD, so that the ORDER of the first two branches of
-- `the standing of the guardian under this Act` is fixed by something rather
-- than left to whichever was written first.
`Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court and since dead` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, dead` "infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        `alone` FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- RECORDED AGAINST SOMEBODY ELSE. The same appointment as the first fixture
-- above, but `the reference for the infant` points at a different infant.
-- Nothing in ss 6, 7 or 10 reads that field, so this guardian is counted for
-- Wei Ming exactly as the first one is; the only thing that tells the two
-- apart is the integrity check in `The Act as a whole`.
`Lim, appointed by the father by will, recorded against a different infant` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case` `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "some other infant"
        `appointed by the father by will` `guardian of the person and property of the infant`
        (`jointly with` (LIST "mother")) FALSE FALSE FALSE

§§§ `The infants`

-- A. The father has died, the mother survives, and the father appointed one
--    guardian by his will who is able and willing to act.
`Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`) FALSE TRUE

-- B. The same, but the mother objects to his so acting (s 7(3) "unless").
`Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting`) FALSE TRUE

-- C. The same, but the appointee refuses to act (s 6(1), second sentence).
`Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian refusing to act` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, refusing to act`) FALSE TRUE

-- D. The father appointed nobody at all (s 6(1), second sentence, first limb).
`Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        EMPTY FALSE TRUE

-- E. Both parents dead, each having appointed a guardian (s 7(5)).
`Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Lim, acting jointly with Siti`, `Siti, appointed by the mother by deed, acting jointly with Lim`) FALSE TRUE

-- F. Both parents dead, only the father having appointed anybody.
`Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`) FALSE TRUE

-- G. s 7(6): the court appointed Rajan to act jointly with the surviving
--    mother; the mother has since died, having appointed Siti by her will.
`Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`,
              `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- G2. The same, but the surviving mother appointed nobody.
`Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed nobody` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- G3. The same, but the mother is still alive.
`Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living mother` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- H. s 6(3): both parents dead, no guardian of the person, nobody else with
--    parental rights.
`Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        EMPTY FALSE TRUE

-- H2. The same, except that the father is NOT IN EVIDENCE. The gate must
--     NOT open: a missing record is not a dead parent.
`Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` NOTHING (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        EMPTY FALSE TRUE

-- H3. The same as H, except that somebody else has parental rights.
`Wei Ming, an orphan but another person has parental rights` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        EMPTY TRUE TRUE

-- H4. The same as H, except that a guardian of the person is already acting.
`Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- I. s 10: the testamentary guardian has been removed by the court.
`Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian having been removed` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court`) FALSE TRUE

-- W. TWO APPOINTEES OF THE FATHER'S, ONE DEAD AND ONE ABLE AND WILLING. This
--    is the file that separates the two readings of s 6(1)'s second sentence
--    (register entry 2): on the ALL reading the court's supplementary power
--    has not arisen, because Chandra is still there to act jointly with the
--    mother; on the ANY reading it has, because Lim is dead. Every other file
--    has at most one appointee per parent, where the two readings coincide.
`Wei Ming, the father having appointed two guardians, one of them dead` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead`,
              `Chandra, appointed by the father by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- J. Both parents alive, so a testamentary appointment is dormant.
`Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`) FALSE TRUE

-- K. Both parents dead but the father's date of death is not known, so which
--    of them was "the surviving parent" cannot be established.
`Wei Ming, both parents dead but one death undated` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died undated`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- L. An infant not of an age to express an independent opinion, with a
--    living mother -- for s 11.
`Wei Ming, too young to express an independent opinion` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        EMPTY FALSE FALSE

-- M. An orphan not of an age to express an independent opinion -- for s 11.
`Wei Ming, an orphan too young to express an independent opinion` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        EMPTY FALSE FALSE

-- N. s 6(2), the mirror of A, which nothing walked before: the MOTHER has
--    died, the father survives, and the mother appointed a guardian by will.
`Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- N2. The same, but the mother appointed nobody -- s 6(2), second sentence.
`Wei Ming, the mother having died having appointed no guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        EMPTY FALSE TRUE

-- O. s 6(3): the infant's only guardian of the person is DEAD. The gate must
--    open -- a dead man is nobody's guardian -- and until this fixture
--    existed nothing showed whether it did.
`Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person has died` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent, dead`) FALSE TRUE

-- O2. The same, but he refuses to act -- s 6(1)-(2)'s own formula for a
--     guardian who is there in name only.
`Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person refuses to act` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent, refusing to act`) FALSE TRUE

-- P. s 10 ON A PARENT: the court has removed the mother from the
--    guardianship s 6(1) gave her, and appointed Siti in her place.
`Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `the mother, removed from her guardianship by the court`,
              `Siti, appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian`) FALSE TRUE

-- P2. The same facts WITHOUT the removal, so that the count can be seen not
--     to double: the mother is guardian by force of s 6 and also has a
--     record, and she must appear exactly once.
`Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`) FALSE TRUE

-- P3. The same record with BOTH parents alive, so s 6 makes nobody guardian.
`Wei Ming, both parents alive with the mother's guardianship recorded` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`) FALSE TRUE

-- P4. The mirror of P2 under s 6(2): the father survives and his own
--     guardianship is recorded.
`Wei Ming, the father's guardianship under section 6(2) recorded` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `the father, his guardianship under section 6(2) recorded`) FALSE TRUE

-- P5. The mirror of P: the father removed under s 10, with no replacement.
`Wei Ming, the father removed from his guardianship by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `the father, removed from his guardianship by the court`) FALSE TRUE

-- G3b. THE MIRROR OF G3, and the only file in which the FATHER is the
--     surviving parent of a court-appointed guardian. G, G2, G3, K and Q all
--     have the father dying first, so `the parent who survived the other`
--     never had to answer "the father" from its own first branch.
`Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living father` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- N3. THE MIRROR OF I UNDER s 6(2): the mother's testamentary appointee has
--     been removed by the court. `the guardians appointed by the mother`
--     drops him for the same reason `the guardians appointed by the father`
--     drops Lim in I, and until this file existed only the father's copy of
--     that filter was ever run.
`Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian having been removed` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Siti, appointed by the mother by will, removed by the court`) FALSE TRUE

-- P6. TWO PARENT-ROUTE FACTS IN ONE FILE, AND THE REMOVAL BELONGS TO ONE OF
--     THEM. The court removed the father from the guardianship s 6(2) once
--     gave him; he has since died, and s 6(1) now makes the surviving mother
--     guardian. A test that asked only "is there a removed parent-route
--     record on this file?" would answer that the MOTHER had been removed,
--     which is why the test compares references.
`Wei Ming, the father removed and since dead, the mother now holding` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `the father, removed from his guardianship, having since died`) FALSE TRUE

-- G4. THE MIRROR OF G, WITH THE FATHER AS THE SURVIVOR OF TWO DEATHS. G, G2,
--     K, Q and T all have the father dying first, so `the later of the two
--     deaths` had never had to prefer its first argument, and
--     `the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` had
--     never had to look at the FATHER's appointees. Here the mother died in
--     2019, the father in 2020, and the father had appointed Lim by will.
`Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the father who appointed a guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died before the father`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`,
              `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`) FALSE TRUE

-- H5. THE MIRROR OF H2: the FATHER is dead and the MOTHER is not in evidence.
--     H2 walks the missing-parent discipline in one direction only, and three
--     decisions ask about the mother where H2 can only ask about the father.
`Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) NOTHING
        EMPTY FALSE TRUE

-- N4. THE MIRROR OF C UNDER s 6(2): the mother's testamentary appointee
--     refuses to act. The father survives, so s 6(2) makes him guardian, and
--     the second sentence's SECOND limb -- "is or are dead or refuses or
--     refuse to act" -- is what opens the court's supplementary power. N2 has
--     the mother appointing nobody, which opens the FIRST limb instead.
`Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian refusing to act` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Siti, appointed by the mother by will, refusing to act`) FALSE TRUE

-- X1 and X2. THE PARENT ROUTE CLAIMED BY SOMEBODY WHO IS NOT THAT PARENT, in
--     a file where s 6 does make a parent guardian -- the mother in X1, the
--     father in X2. Both are needed because the decision tests the two
--     parents in two branches, and a file that closes one leaves the other
--     unwalked.
`Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the mother surviving` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, wrongly recorded as guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`) FALSE TRUE

`Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the father surviving` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Lim, wrongly recorded as guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- I2. REMOVED AND ALSO DEAD. Both of the first two branches of the standing
--     answer on this file, and only the order between them decides which.
`Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian removed and since dead` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court and since dead`) FALSE TRUE

-- Q. Both parents dead ON THE SAME DAY: the second of the two ways the order
--    of the deaths can fail, the first being K's unknown date.
`Wei Ming, both parents dead on the same day` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died the same day as the father`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- Q2. A DIFFERENT KIND OF GAP, and it has to answer differently. Here it is
--     not the ORDER of the deaths that is missing but a whole parent: the
--     mother is dead and the father is not in evidence. s 7(6) cannot say
--     that the surviving parent has died, because it cannot be said that the
--     father is dead -- and a living father who objected under s 7(3) would
--     stop the appointee acting. So even "does he act?" is open here, where
--     in K and Q it is not.
`Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence, with a court-appointed guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` NOTHING (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`) FALSE TRUE

-- Q3. THE TESTAMENTARY COUNTERPART OF Q2. The mother is dead and appointed a
--    guardian by her will; the father is not in evidence at all. s 7(3) makes
--    the appointee act jointly with the other parent "so long as the mother or
--    father remains alive", and s 7(3) also lets that parent object -- so
--    whether this man acts, and with whom, cannot be said without knowing
--    whether there is a father and what he has done. Q2 walks this case on the
--    COURT route; nothing walked it on the route s 7(1)-(2) create.
`Wei Ming, the father not in evidence, the mother dead having appointed a guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` NOTHING (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- R. s 7(1)'s other instrument: the father appointed by DEED, not by will.
`Wei Ming, the father having appointed a guardian by deed` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by deed`) FALSE TRUE

-- R2. The same appointee, the mother NOT objecting but the appointee
--     considering her unfit -- s 7(4)'s second limb alone.
`Wei Ming, the guardian considering the mother unfit` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by deed, considering the mother unfit`) FALSE TRUE

-- S. A guardian recorded as acting "jointly with" nobody at all.
`Wei Ming, the guardian recorded as acting jointly with nobody` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, recorded as acting jointly with nobody`) FALSE TRUE

-- T. s 7(5) where one side has dropped out: both parents appointed and both
--    are dead, but the father's only appointee is dead too, so there is
--    nobody for the mother's appointee to act jointly with.
`Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead`, `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- V. s 7(5) with its FIRST limb open and its SECOND closed: both parents have
--    appointed a guardian, and the mother is still alive, so "after the death
--    of the surviving parent" has not happened. (Her appointee's own
--    appointment is dormant meanwhile, s 7(2) operating only "after her
--    death"; s 7(5) asks about the appointments, not about their operation.)
`Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`,
              `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`) FALSE TRUE

-- U, U2, U3. THREE FILES IN WHICH BOTH s 7(4) TRIGGERS ARE PRESENT and the
--    right of application still has not arisen -- because the appointment has
--    not taken effect (U), because there is no living parent for s 7(4) to be
--    about (U2), or because the applicant is not "the guardian so appointed
--    as aforesaid" at all (U3).
`Wei Ming, both parents alive and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, still living`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit`) FALSE TRUE

`Wei Ming, both parents dead and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit`) FALSE TRUE

`Wei Ming, an orphan whose court-appointed guardian presses both limbs of section 7(4)` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, died first`) (JUST `Lai Peng, died second`)
        (LIST `Rajan, appointed by the court, objecting and considering the parent unfit`) FALSE TRUE

-- FOR THE GATE IN `The Act as a whole`. Fixture A's facts exactly, with one
-- substitution each.
--
--   The infant's majority date is not in evidence, so the gate closes neither
--   way and reports the third outcome.
`Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`) FALSE TRUE

--   The guardian is recorded against a different infant, so the list and the
--   back-reference disagree.
`Wei Ming, a guardian recorded against a different infant` MEANS
    `a GIA infant` `Wei Ming, the infant` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`) (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
        (LIST `Lim, appointed by the father by will, recorded against a different infant`) FALSE TRUE

§§§ `Guardians of the property, for sections 15 to 18`

`a GIA monthly provision of $80`   MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 80  , `the period` IS `monthly`
`a GIA monthly provision of $100`  MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 100 , `the period` IS `monthly`
`a GIA monthly provision of $101`  MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 101 , `the period` IS `monthly`
`a GIA monthly provision of $150`  MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 150 , `the period` IS `monthly`
`a GIA weekly provision of $20`    MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 20  , `the period` IS `weekly`
-- $23 a week is $99.67 a month and $24 a week is $104.00, so these two
-- straddle the $23.08 line the 52/12 conversion puts the cap at.
`a GIA weekly provision of $23`    MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 23  , `the period` IS `weekly`
`a GIA weekly provision of $24`    MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 24  , `the period` IS `weekly`
`a GIA weekly provision of $30`    MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 30  , `the period` IS `weekly`
`a GIA provision of $90 on some other period` MEANS `A periodical sum` WITH `the sum` IS 90, `the period` IS `other periodical`

-- The bare case: appointed by the court, guardian of the property, nothing
-- done and nothing dispensed with.
`a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a court-appointed guardian of the property who has given security` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a court-appointed guardian of the property excused security by the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

-- A guardian of the property appointed by the father's will, so NOT a person
-- appointed by the court: s 15(1) does not reach him.
`a testamentary guardian of the property` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Lim Kok Wah, appointed by the father` "infant" `appointed by the father by will`
        FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who sold without permission` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who sold with permission` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE NOTHING TRUE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who granted a one-year lease` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE (JUST 1) FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who granted a three-year lease` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE (JUST 3) FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who granted a lease of unrecorded term` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who gave a discharge with no order of the court` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE TRUE FALSE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property who gave a discharge the court had authorised` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE TRUE TRUE `no GIA provision` FALSE

`a guardian of the property applying $80 a month` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `a GIA monthly provision of $80` FALSE

`a guardian of the property applying $150 a month without permission` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `a GIA monthly provision of $150` FALSE

`a guardian of the property applying $150 a month with permission` MEANS
    `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`
        `Rajan, appointed by the court` "infant" `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
        TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE NOTHING FALSE FALSE FALSE `a GIA monthly provision of $150` TRUE

-- A guardian of the PERSON only: ss 16, 17 and 18(1) all open "A guardian of
-- the property of an infant", so none of them reaches him.
-- Spelled in full, because the constructor above fixes the kind of
-- guardianship at `guardian of the property of the infant` and the whole
-- point of this one is that the kind is different while everything else is
-- the same.
`a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession` MEANS Guardian WITH
    `the guardian`                       IS `Rajan, appointed by the court`
    `the reference for the infant`       IS "infant"
    `how the guardian was appointed`     IS `appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
    `of the person or of the property`   IS `guardian of the person of the infant`
    `alone or jointly`                   IS `alone`
    `refuses to act`                     IS FALSE
    `the mother or father objects to his so acting` IS FALSE
    `considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of the infant` IS FALSE
    `removed from his guardianship by the court` IS FALSE
    `has given security`                 IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to security` IS FALSE
    `has sold, mortgaged, exchanged, or otherwise parted with the possession of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant` IS TRUE
    `has leased land belonging to the infant`        IS FALSE
    `the term of the lease, in years`                IS NOTHING
    `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the infant` IS FALSE
    `has given a good discharge for a legacy or other capital moneys payable to or receivable by the infant` IS TRUE
    `the court has otherwise ordered as to giving a good discharge` IS FALSE
    `the provision made out of the income of the property of the infant for his maintenance and education` IS `a GIA monthly provision of $150`
    `has the permission of the court to apply a sum exceeding $100 per month` IS FALSE


§§ `Exercising the rules on the facts`

-- THESE ARE ASSERTIONS, NOT DEMONSTRATIONS. An earlier draft of this module
-- printed 166 values with `#EVAL` and checked none of them, which is a suite
-- that cannot fail. Every expected answer below was worked out from the
-- section named beside it and written down BEFORE it was run; where the
-- encoding then disagreed, the encoding was wrong and was repaired.
--
-- AND A GREEN ASSERTION IS NOT EVIDENCE ON ITS OWN. An assertion that would
-- hold whatever the rule under test did passes exactly as green as one that
-- would not, and the commonest way to write one is a negative over a fixture
-- that fails two things at once: the limb you meant to exercise never runs.
-- So the assertions below were audited by MUTATION -- one conjunct, branch
-- condition or `CONSIDER` arm of the rules above neutralised at a time, over
-- a scratch copy, the suite re-run each time -- and every mutation that no
-- assertion caught was either closed with a new file or named below as
-- uncloseable and why. That audit is the reason for a good many of the files
-- in this section: they exist because breaking a rule left the suite green.
--
-- WHAT IS COVERED, stated so it can be checked rather than believed.
--
--   EVERY MEMBER OF EVERY ENUM. All sixteen members of `The standing of a
--   guardian under this Act` are produced as the OUTPUT of
--   `the standing of the guardian under this Act` on some fixture below, and
--   the section headed "Which standings are standings in which he acts" then
--   walks all sixteen again as inputs, which is what makes that
--   classification checkable rather than merely plausible. The five members
--   of `A question in proceedings before a court`, the three of `Forum`, the
--   four of `What X has done in respect of the breach`, the three of
--   `The order the court may make under section 7(4)` and the three of
--   `Who is guardian after an order under section 7(4)`, the three of
--   `Whether the provision exceeds the cap in section 18(1)`, and the three
--   of `Money to which this Act might apply` are each exercised. From the
--   shared ontology: all eight members of `How the guardian was appointed`,
--   all three of `Kind of guardianship`, all three of `Vital status`, both of
--   `Court`, all three periods of `A periodical sum`, and `Alone or jointly`
--   in all three of its states -- `alone`, `jointly with` naming somebody,
--   and `jointly with` naming nobody.
--
--   BOTH SIDES OF EVERY BOUNDARY, and the boundary itself: the $20,000 fine
--   ($20,000 / $20,001), the 12 months' imprisonment (12 / 13), the one-year
--   lease (1 / 3, and a term not in evidence), the $100 a month ($100 / $101
--   monthly, and $23 / $24 weekly, which straddle the $23.08 the 52/12
--   conversion puts the line at), s 5A(4)'s access comparison (4/8, 8/8,
--   9/8), and the date of majority (the day before, the day itself).
--
--   EVERY LIMB OF EVERY MULTI-LIMB GATE closed on its own, with the other
--   limbs shown OPEN by a companion assertion, so that a FALSE cannot pass
--   for the wrong reason: s 5A(1)'s two paragraphs, s 6(3)'s three limbs,
--   s 7(3)'s three, s 7(4)'s two limbs, s 7(5)'s two limbs, s 8's two limbs,
--   s 9(1)'s four, s 15(1)'s three, s 16(1)(b)'s two, and s 18(2)'s two.
--
--   AND FIVE PLACES WHERE THAT IS NOT TRUE AND CANNOT BE MADE TRUE, named
--   because a coverage claim invites exactly the check that finds them. Each
--   is a conjunct that another conjunct already entails, or a tautology:
--     * s 6(1) and s 6(2), second sentence, FIRST limb ("no guardian has been
--       appointed"). On this module's reading of the plural -- register entry
--       2 -- `all` over an empty list is TRUE, so the second limb already
--       covers every case the first covers, and no file can distinguish them.
--     * the route conjunct of `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has
--       taken effect`: `the parent by whom the guardian was appointed` is
--       NOTHING for every route that is not a parental appointment, so the
--       death conjunct is already FALSE wherever the route conjunct would be.
--     * s 7(4)'s three restrictions on who may apply: two are isolated, the
--       third cannot be, because a court's appointee fails two of them at
--       once as a matter of the type. The note under s 7(4) sets this out.
--     * s 3's forum conjunct. s 3 reaches every forum, so the conjunct is a
--       tautology; what is asserted is its exhaustiveness, member by member.
--     * the both-parents-dead guard in `the parent who survived the other`,
--       which `the later of the two deaths` already enforces.
--   Everything else in the rules of this module was mutation-tested -- one
--   conjunct, branch condition or `CONSIDER` arm neutralised at a time, over
--   a scratch copy -- and every other mutation turned at least one assertion
--   red. Those five are the whole of the residue.
--
--   A HANDFUL OF NEGATIVE ASSERTIONS ARE OVER-DETERMINED AND ARE KEPT ANYWAY,
--   each labelled as such where it stands, because the state it describes is
--   the ordinary way the provision fails and a reader looking for it should
--   find it: no access order and no breach (s 5A(1)); no pension payable and
--   none attachable (s 9(1)); no guardians at all (s 8); neither s 7(4)
--   trigger present; a sentence of neither a fine nor imprisonment. In every
--   one of those five a MINIMAL companion sits beside it, closing one limb
--   with the others shown open. The over-determined assertion is the
--   illustration; the minimal one is the test.
--
-- WHAT WAS NOT COVERED BEFORE. Three of the eight members of `How the
-- guardian was appointed` had no fixture at all (`appointed by the father by
-- deed`, `appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian` and
-- `guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`); four standing members
-- were never produced; s 6(2)'s second sentence -- the mirror the Act writes
-- out in full -- was never run; and neither a same-day double death nor an
-- empty `jointly with` was ever walked. Two of the three defects this suite
-- was written to catch lived in exactly that unwalked region.
--
-- AND WHAT WAS NOT COVERED AFTER THAT, which is the more useful list, because
-- the fixtures above were written and the module was then declared covered.
-- Every one of these was found by mutation and not by reading, and each is
-- now closed by a file that differs from a passing one in exactly one fact:
--   * s 5A(1)(a) and s 9(1)'s "there is a pension or income payable". Both
--     had only an over-determined negative -- a file with two limbs false at
--     once -- so deleting either limb changed no answer.
--   * s 7(3)'s "so long as the mother or father remains alive". Deleting it
--     had the module report that a testamentary appointee acted jointly with
--     a parent who was dead, and nothing said so.
--   * the ALL reading of s 6(1)-(2)'s plural, which no file separated from
--     the ANY reading, every parent having had at most one appointee.
--   * s 15(1)'s property limb, s 16(1)(b)'s "has leased land" limb, and s 8's
--     standing limb -- three conjuncts whose only negatives were answered by
--     something else on the same file.
--   * the first branch of `the parent who survived the other` and the first
--     branch of `the later of the two deaths`: every pair of deaths in the
--     file had the father dying first, so the FATHER was never once the
--     answer, and both branches could have named the wrong parent.
--   * s 6(2)'s own removal filter and its own "refuses to act" limb, both of
--     which had been borrowing the father's file.
--   * the reference comparison in `the parent has been removed from his
--     guardianship by the court under section 10`, with no file carrying two
--     parent-route facts; and the identity test in
--     `the standing of a parent who holds by virtue of surviving the other
--     parent`, with no file where s 6 made a parent guardian and the record
--     belonged to somebody else.
--   * the not-in-evidence branch of the testamentary standing, which without
--     a file would have had the module report an absent parent as ALIVE.
--   * the absent-parent arms of `the person is the father of the infant`,
--     `the person is the mother of the infant` and `the parent has been
--     removed ...`, which no composition can reach and which are now pinned
--     by unit assertions.
--   * the branch ORDER between removal and death, which no file made both.

§§§ `The age of majority is an input, not a fact of this Act`

-- The Act supplies no age, so both routes take one from outside. The first
-- reads the construed majority date; the second builds it from a date of
-- birth and an age the CALLER names, which is where the number visibly
-- enters. Wei Ming was born 19 July 2012 and his construed majority date is
-- 19 July 2033.
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Wei Ming, the infant` (YMD 2026 8 26) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Wei Ming, the infant` (YMD 2035 1 1)  EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- THE BOUNDARY, both sides and on it: infancy is `LESS THAN` the date of
-- majority, so he is an infant on the day before and is not on the day
-- itself.
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Wei Ming, the infant` (YMD 2033 7 18) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Wei Ming, the infant` (YMD 2033 7 19) EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- NOTHING means the majority date is not in evidence, not that he is of age.
#ASSERT `the person is an infant on the date in question` `Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence` (YMD 2026 8 26) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the date on which the person attains the age of majority, computed from the date of birth` `Wei Ming, the infant` 21 EQUALS JUST (YMD 2033 7 19)
#ASSERT `the date on which the person attains the age of majority, computed from the date of birth` `Wei Ming, the infant` 18 EQUALS JUST (YMD 2030 7 19)
-- No date of birth recorded, so no majority date can be computed.
#ASSERT `the date on which the person attains the age of majority, computed from the date of birth` `Lai Peng, the mother` 21 EQUALS NOTHING

§§§ `Section 1 -- short title`

#ASSERT `the short title of this Act` EQUALS "Guardianship of Infants Act 1934"

§§§ `Section 2 -- "court" means the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court`

#ASSERT `the forum is a court within the meaning of section 2` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`)
#ASSERT `the forum is a court within the meaning of section 2` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `the General Division of the High Court`)
-- The two s 2 excludes, and the reason the `Forum` type exists at all.
#ASSERT NOT `the forum is a court within the meaning of section 2` `a District Court`
#ASSERT NOT `the forum is a court within the meaning of section 2` `a Magistrate's Court`

§§§ `Section 3 -- the gate, and "any court"`

-- s 3 reaches every forum, because it says "any court" and not "the court".
#ASSERT `section 3 reaches proceedings before the forum` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`)
#ASSERT `section 3 reaches proceedings before the forum` `a District Court`
#ASSERT `section 3 reaches proceedings before the forum` `a Magistrate's Court`

-- All four kinds of question s 3 names, and one it does not.
#ASSERT `the question is one of those section 3 names` `the custody of an infant`
#ASSERT `the question is one of those section 3 names` `the upbringing of an infant`
#ASSERT `the question is one of those section 3 names` `the administration of any property belonging to or held in trust for an infant`
#ASSERT `the question is one of those section 3 names` `the application of the income thereof`
#ASSERT NOT `the question is one of those section 3 names` `a question of some other kind`

#ASSERT `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`) `the custody of an infant`
#ASSERT NOT (`section 3 governs the deciding of the question` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`) `a question of some other kind`)
-- THE POINT OF THE FORUM LIMB: a District Court deciding an infant's custody
-- is within s 3, though it is not a "court" within s 2.
#ASSERT `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` `a District Court` `the custody of an infant`
#ASSERT NOT `section 3 governs the deciding of the question` `a Magistrate's Court` `a question of some other kind`

#ASSERT `the standing of the father and the mother in deciding the question` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`) `the upbringing of an infant` FALSE EQUALS JUST `neither the father nor the mother is deemed to have any right or claim superior to that of the other`
#ASSERT `the standing of the father and the mother in deciding the question` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`) `the upbringing of an infant` TRUE EQUALS JUST `the welfare of the infant otherwise requires, and to that extent the deeming rule in section 3 does not apply`
-- NOTHING: s 3 does not govern the question at all, so it says nothing about
-- the standing of the parents in deciding it.
#ASSERT `the standing of the father and the mother in deciding the question` (`the General Division of the High Court or a Family Court` `a Family Court`) `a question of some other kind` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the standing of the father and the mother in deciding the question` `a District Court` `the custody of an infant` FALSE EQUALS JUST `neither the father nor the mother is deemed to have any right or claim superior to that of the other`

§§§ `Section 4 -- the mother's parity of standing`

-- s 4 gives the mother "the like powers ... as are possessed by the father",
-- so her power is his, confined to matters affecting the infant. All four
-- combinations.
#ASSERT     `the mother has the like powers of applying to the court in respect of the matter` TRUE  TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the mother has the like powers of applying to the court in respect of the matter` TRUE  FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the mother has the like powers of applying to the court in respect of the matter` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the mother has the like powers of applying to the court in respect of the matter` FALSE FALSE

§§§ `Section 6 -- the surviving parent`

-- A. Father dead, mother surviving, one willing appointee of the father's.
--    s 6(1) makes the mother guardian jointly with him.
#ASSERT     `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS JUST (`jointly with` (LIST "lim"))
-- The court's supplementary power does NOT arise: an appointee is acting.
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother", "lim"

-- C. The appointee refuses to act: the mother is guardian ALONE and the
--    court's power under the second sentence of s 6(1) arises.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian refusing to act` EQUALS JUST `alone`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian refusing to act`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian refusing to act` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- D. No guardian appointed at all: the other limb of the same sentence.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian` EQUALS JUST `alone`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- W. THE READING OF THE PLURAL, and the only file that tests it. The father
--    appointed two guardians; one is dead and one is able and willing. On the
--    reading this module takes -- "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to
--    act" means ALL of them -- the second sentence is not satisfied and the
--    court's supplementary power has not arisen. Change `all` to `any` in the
--    rule and the third assertion below is the one that goes red; every other
--    file in this suite has at most one appointee per parent, where the two
--    readings give the same answer. Register entry 2.
#ASSERT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` `Chandra, appointed by the father by will`
#ASSERT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed two guardians, one of them dead`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed two guardians, one of them dead`
-- The dead appointee is dropped from the joint guardianship, so the mother
-- acts with the survivor of the two and not with both.
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed two guardians, one of them dead` EQUALS JUST (`jointly with` (LIST "chandra"))
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed two guardians, one of them dead` EQUALS LIST "mother", "chandra"

-- J. Both parents alive: s 6(1) is not engaged, so it yields nothing.
-- AND NEITHER IS s 6(2). The second sentence of each subsection has a
-- STANDING conjunct as well as its own disjunction, and it is the standing
-- conjunct that answers here: the mother has appointed nobody, so the
-- disjunction is satisfied on both sides, and the court's power still has not
-- arisen because neither parent has died. Delete the standing conjunct from
-- s 6(2) and the second assertion below goes red; the s 6(1) mirror of it is
-- answered by fixture P, where the mother is removed.
#ASSERT NOT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT NOT `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will` EQUALS EMPTY

-- THE MIRROR, s 6(2). The Act writes the rule out twice and so does this
-- module; until fixture N existed, the second copy was never run. Here the
-- MOTHER has died and the father survives, and she appointed Siti by will.
#ASSERT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS JUST (`jointly with` (LIST "siti"))
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST "father", "siti"

-- N2. The mother appointed nobody: s 6(2), second sentence, first limb.
#ASSERT `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` `Wei Ming, the mother having died having appointed no guardian` EQUALS JUST `alone`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the mother having died having appointed no guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother having died having appointed no guardian` EQUALS LIST "father"

-- N4. s 6(2), second sentence, SECOND limb -- and note what N2 could not do.
--     The two limbs are not independent: `all` over an empty list is TRUE, so
--     wherever the first limb is satisfied the second is satisfied with it,
--     and N2 therefore says nothing about the second. This file has an
--     appointee on the list -- so the first limb is CLOSED -- who refuses to
--     act, which is the second limb and nothing else. Delete the second limb
--     and this is the assertion that goes red; delete the first and nothing
--     moves anywhere, for the reason just given.
#ASSERT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will, refusing to act`
#ASSERT `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian refusing to act` EQUALS JUST `alone`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian refusing to act`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian refusing to act` EQUALS LIST "father"

-- N. And the appointment of a guardian by the MOTHER takes effect on her
--    death exactly as the father's does on his. `appointed by a parent by
--    deed or will under section 7(1) or (2)` is a disjunction of the two
--    routes, and until this pair existed only the father's half of it was
--    ever the reason the answer came out TRUE.
#ASSERT `appointed by a parent by deed or will under section 7(1) or (2)` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will`
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian`

-- G3 has the FATHER dying first, so s 6(2) is not engaged there; E has both
-- dead, so neither subsection is.
#ASSERT NOT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living mother`
#ASSERT `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living mother` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT NOT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead`

§§§ `Section 6(3) -- the three-limbed gate, each limb closed on its own`

-- H. All three limbs open: the gate is satisfied.
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights`

-- H2. The first limb closed, and closed for the right reason: a parent NOT
--     IN EVIDENCE is not a dead parent, so "an infant has no parent" fails
--     even though the only parent on the file is dead.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence`

-- H5. THE SAME LIMB CLOSED THE OTHER WAY ROUND, which nothing walked. The
--     father is dead and the MOTHER is not in evidence. Three decisions ask
--     "is this person the mother of the infant?" and the answer where there
--     is no mother on the file has to be no -- not "unknown, so yes".
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has a parent or parents` `Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence`
-- THE ABSENT-PARENT ARMS THEMSELVES, asserted directly because nothing in the
-- composition can reach them: every decision that consults these two has
-- already required the parent in question to be in evidence, so the arms are
-- total-function boilerplate over a MAYBE and would answer anything at all
-- without an assertion moving. They are pinned here.
#ASSERT NOT `the person is the mother of the infant` `Tan Boon Huat, the father` `Wei Ming, the father dead and the mother not in evidence`
#ASSERT NOT `the person is the father of the infant` `Lai Peng, died second` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence`
-- And the same discipline in `the parent has been removed from his
-- guardianship by the court under section 10`: a parent not on the file has
-- not been removed from anything. A missing record never manufactures a fact,
-- and s 10 is a fact about a court order.
#ASSERT NOT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence` NOTHING

-- H3. The third limb closed, the other two shown open.
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan but another person has parental rights`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, an orphan but another person has parental rights`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, an orphan but another person has parental rights`

-- H4. The second limb closed by a LIVING, WILLING guardian of the person,
--     the other two shown open.
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person`
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person`

-- O AND O2. THE DEFECT THIS PAIR WAS WRITTEN FOR. The only recorded guardian
--    of the person is dead (O) or refuses to act (O2). A dead man is nobody's
--    guardian, and s 6(1)-(2) treat one who "refuses ... to act" as absent
--    for exactly this purpose, so the limb is open and the gate is satisfied
--    -- and the module's own answer to "who are the guardians of this infant"
--    has to agree, which is what the third assertion in each pair checks. An
--    earlier draft filtered on removal alone and answered FALSE and EMPTY
--    together.
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person has died`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person has died`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person has died` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person refuses to act`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint an applicant to be the guardian of the infant under section 6(3)` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person refuses to act`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose only guardian of the person refuses to act` EQUALS EMPTY

-- AND THE RECORD LIMB IS ABOUT WHO ACTS, NOT WHO IS ON THE FILE. Each of the
-- three files below records somebody as a guardian of the person of this
-- infant, in none of them does that person act, and in none of them is any
-- parent guardian by force of s 6 -- so the record limb is the only one left
-- to answer, and the standing is what answers it. The reasons are: the
-- appointment has not taken effect (J), s 6 makes the recorded parent no
-- guardian because both parents are alive (P3), and a parent is not in
-- evidence so it cannot be said the appointee acts at all (Q2). The first
-- assertion of each pair shows the record IS a guardian of the person.
#ASSERT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, both parents alive with the mother's guardianship recorded`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence, with a court-appointed guardian`
-- And the contrast: in A the same appointee DOES act, so the infant has one.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`

-- B WAS IN THAT LIST AND HAS BEEN TAKEN OUT OF IT, BECAUSE IT ANSWERED THE
-- WRONG WAY AND NOTHING SAID SO. The mother has objected to the appointee
-- under s 7(3), so HE does not act -- which the standing assertion under s 7
-- below still shows -- but SHE is guardian of the infant under s 6(1), the
-- father having died and she surviving. So this infant has a guardian of his
-- person, and the assertion that he had none was an artefact of counting
-- records only. It is the one assertion in this file that was written down as
-- an expected answer, run green, and was wrong.
#ASSERT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting`
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- THE SECTION 6 LIMB, CLOSED ON ITS OWN AND ONLY BY ITSELF. D has NO guardian
-- records at all, so the record limb is open and cannot be what answers; the
-- mother is guardian by force of s 6(1) and nothing else. Delete the s 6 limb
-- and this assertion is the one that goes red.
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother"
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian`
-- P2 IS THE SAME s 6 FACTS PLUS A RECORD THE MODULE SAYS IS UNNECESSARY, and
-- the pair is here because it used to be the tell: D answered TRUE and P2
-- FALSE, so the answer to "has this infant a guardian of his person?" turned
-- on whether a case-file author had bothered to write the record down. They
-- now agree, which is the point of the pair.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`
-- AND THE LIMB READS s 10, not the bare s 6(2) test. P4 and P5 differ by
-- exactly one fact -- whether the court has removed the father -- and there
-- is no other guardian in either file, so the flip is the removal and nothing
-- else.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father's guardianship under section 6(2) recorded`
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father removed from his guardianship by the court`

§§§ `Section 10 read into section 6 -- a removed parent is not a guardian`

-- P. THE SECOND DEFECT THIS SUITE WAS WRITTEN FOR. s 10 removes "any
--    guardian", and s 6(1) makes the mother guardian only "subject to the
--    provisions of this Act". So s 6(1) is satisfied on its own terms and she
--    still does not hold, and the replacement the court appointed in her
--    place is the only guardian left.
#ASSERT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian of the infant under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place`
#ASSERT NOT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place`
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` EQUALS LIST "siti"
-- The second sentence of s 6(1) presupposes a mother to appoint somebody to
-- act jointly WITH; once she is removed there is none, and s 10's own power
-- to "appoint another guardian in his place" is the route the court used.
#ASSERT NOT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place`

-- P2. The same file WITHOUT the removal. The mother is guardian by force of
--     s 6 and also has a record of her own, and she must be counted once.
#ASSERT NOT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` `Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
#ASSERT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- P6. WHOSE REMOVAL IS IT? The court removed the father; he has since died
--     and s 6(1) now makes the surviving mother guardian. The removal record
--     is a parent-route record and it is the only one on the file, so a test
--     that asked "is there a removed parent-route record here?" would answer
--     that the MOTHER had been removed and strike her out. Delete the
--     reference comparison from `the parent has been removed from his
--     guardianship by the court under section 10` and the second, third and
--     fourth assertions below all go red together.
#ASSERT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` `Wei Ming, the father removed and since dead, the mother now holding` (JUST `Tan Boon Huat, the father`)
#ASSERT NOT `the parent has been removed from his guardianship by the court under section 10` `Wei Ming, the father removed and since dead, the mother now holding` (JUST `Lai Peng, the mother`)
#ASSERT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Wei Ming, the father removed and since dead, the mother now holding`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father removed and since dead, the mother now holding` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- P4 and P5. The mirror under s 6(2).
#ASSERT `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` `Wei Ming, the father's guardianship under section 6(2) recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father's guardianship under section 6(2) recorded` EQUALS LIST "father"
#ASSERT `the father is guardian of the infant under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the father removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT NOT `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` `Wei Ming, the father removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father removed from his guardianship by the court` EQUALS EMPTY

§§§ `Section 7 -- the composition`

-- A. s 7(3), the default limb: the appointment has taken effect, the mother
--    is alive, and she has not objected, so he acts jointly with her.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`

-- B. s 7(3), the "unless" limb. The appointment HAS taken effect -- so the
--    FALSE below is the objection doing the work and nothing else -- and
--    s 7(4) opens to him. On the reading this module takes (register entry 4)
--    he does not act at all, so the mother is left sole guardian.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian` EQUALS `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`
#ASSERT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, the mother objecting` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother objecting to the testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother"

-- R2. s 7(4)'s SECOND limb on its own: no objection, but the appointee
--     "considers that the mother or father is unfit to have the custody of
--     the infant". He may apply, and until the court orders otherwise he goes
--     on acting jointly under s 7(3).
#ASSERT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed, considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, the guardian considering the mother unfit`
#ASSERT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed, considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, the guardian considering the mother unfit`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed, considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, the guardian considering the mother unfit` EQUALS `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`

-- Neither limb: no application.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`

-- F. s 7(3)'s SURVIVORSHIP LIMB ON ITS OWN, which nothing closed before.
--    "Any guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father,
--    as the case may be, of the infant so long as the mother or father
--    remains alive" -- so when the other parent is dead too there is nobody
--    for him to act jointly with, and s 7(3) has stopped operating. The
--    appointment HAS taken effect and the mother has NOT objected, so the
--    two other conjuncts are open and the FALSE can only be the survivorship
--    limb. Without it the module would report that this appointee acts
--    jointly with a woman who died in 2024.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`
-- And what he does instead is s 7(1)-(2)'s own answer, not nothing: he acts.
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` (`the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`)

-- WHOSE RIGHT s 7(4) IS. Each of the three fixtures below has BOTH limbs of
-- s 7(4) recorded as satisfied, so the FALSE can only come from the limits
-- the subsection puts on who may apply -- which an earlier draft dropped.
--
-- TWO OF THE THREE ISOLATE ONE RESTRICTION EACH; THE THIRD CANNOT, and saying
-- otherwise would be the kind of coverage claim this section exists to make
-- checkable. U closes the taking-effect restriction with the living-parent
-- restriction open, and U2 closes the living-parent restriction with the
-- taking-effect restriction open. U3 closes BOTH at once and there is no file
-- that could do otherwise: a guardian the court appointed has no
-- `the parent by whom the guardian was appointed`, so his appointment cannot
-- have taken effect, AND no `the other parent, the mother or father as the
-- case may be`, so no parent of his remains alive either. The route
-- restriction is pinned instead by a unit assertion on the predicate itself,
-- the first of the two U3 assertions below.
--
-- U. The appointment has not taken effect: the father is still alive.
#ASSERT NOT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, both parents alive and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, both parents alive and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)`
-- U2. The appointment HAS taken effect, but both parents are dead, so there
--     is no "mother or father" for s 7(4) to be about; ss 7(5)-(6) compose
--     the guardianship instead.
#ASSERT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, objecting and considering the mother unfit` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the appointee pressing both limbs of section 7(4)`
-- U3. A court's appointee is not "the guardian so appointed as aforesaid".
#ASSERT NOT `appointed by a parent by deed or will under section 7(1) or (2)` `Rajan, appointed by the court, objecting and considering the parent unfit`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court under section 7(4)` `Rajan, appointed by the court, objecting and considering the parent unfit` `Wei Ming, an orphan whose court-appointed guardian presses both limbs of section 7(4)`

-- s 7(4): each of the three orders, and its stated consequence.
#ASSERT `who is guardian after the order under section 7(4)` `the court refuses to make any order` EQUALS `the mother or father remains sole guardian`
#ASSERT `who is guardian after the order under section 7(4)` `the court orders that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father` EQUALS `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father`
#ASSERT `who is guardian after the order under section 7(4)` `the court orders that he shall be sole guardian of the infant` EQUALS `the guardian so appointed is sole guardian of the infant`
-- The ancillary powers arise "in the later case" only.
#ASSERT NOT `the court may make the further orders under section 7(4) as to custody, access and maintenance` `the court refuses to make any order`
#ASSERT NOT `the court may make the further orders under section 7(4) as to custody, access and maintenance` `the court orders that the guardian so appointed shall act jointly with the mother or father`
#ASSERT     `the court may make the further orders under section 7(4) as to custody, access and maintenance` `the court orders that he shall be sole guardian of the infant`

-- J. s 7(1)-(2): the appointment is dormant while the appointing parent lives.
--    This is also s 7(3)'s FIRST conjunct closed on its own: the mother is
--    alive and has not objected, so the other two are open and the FALSE is
--    the appointment not having taken effect. With B (the objection) and F
--    (the survivorship) that is all three of s 7(3)'s conjuncts, one file
--    each.
#ASSERT NOT `the appointment under section 7(1) or (2) has taken effect` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian so appointed acts jointly with the mother or father under section 7(3)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will` EQUALS `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive`

-- R. s 7(1)'s other instrument. A DEED appointment operates exactly as a will
--    appointment does; it is split in the ontology because it is proved
--    differently, not because the Act treats it differently.
#ASSERT `appointed by the father by deed or will under section 7(1)` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed`
#ASSERT NOT `appointed by the mother by deed or will under section 7(2)` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed`
#ASSERT `the mother is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed a guardian by deed` EQUALS JUST (`jointly with` (LIST "lim"))
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by deed` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed a guardian by deed` EQUALS `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed a guardian by deed` EQUALS LIST "mother", "lim"

-- Q3. THE FIRST BRANCH OF THE TESTAMENTARY STANDING, which nothing walked.
--    A parent of this infant is not in evidence, so the file does not say
--    whether he is alive; s 7(3) turns on that and on whether he objects, and
--    neither is answerable. Replace the branch condition with FALSE and the
--    fall-through reports that the absent father is ALIVE -- the module
--    asserting a fact the file does not contain, which is the error
--    `family-domain` warns against running the other way. So this is the
--    assertion that goes red.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will` `Wei Ming, the father not in evidence, the mother dead having appointed a guardian` EQUALS `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father not in evidence, the mother dead having appointed a guardian` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, the father not in evidence, the mother dead having appointed a guardian`

-- C. A guardian who refuses to act, and one who is dead: s 6(1)-(2)'s own
--    pair, and the standing member that carries it.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, refusing to act` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian refusing to act` EQUALS `he is dead or refuses to act`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead` EQUALS `he is dead or refuses to act`

-- E. s 7(5): guardians appointed by both parents, both parents now dead, and
--    both appointees able to act -- so they act jointly.
#ASSERT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead`
#ASSERT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` EQUALS LIST "lim", "siti"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, acting jointly with Siti` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` EQUALS `after the death of the surviving parent the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` EQUALS LIST "lim", "siti"

-- F. Only one parent appointed anybody, so s 7(5) is not engaged. The second
--    limb of the gate ("after the death of the surviving parent") IS
--    satisfied, so the FALSE is the first limb doing the work.
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents dead, only the father having appointed a guardian` EQUALS `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`

-- V. The other way round: s 7(5)'s FIRST limb is open and its SECOND is
--    closed, the mother being alive. This is the only fixture that closes
--    that limb on its own.
#ASSERT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive`
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive` EQUALS EMPTY
-- The mother's own appointee is dormant while she lives, so the guardians are
-- the mother (s 6(1)) and the father's appointee (s 7(3)).
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive` EQUALS `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents having appointed a guardian, the mother still alive` EQUALS LIST "mother", "lim"

-- T. THE THIRD DEFECT THIS SUITE WAS WRITTEN FOR. Both parents appointed and
--    both are dead, but the father's only appointee is dead. s 7(5) makes the
--    appointees "act jointly", and a dead appointee cannot act jointly with
--    anybody, so the subsection is not engaged and the mother's appointee
--    simply acts. An earlier draft counted the APPOINTMENTS in the gate and
--    the APPOINTEES in the answer, and so reported joint action by one man.
#ASSERT `appointed by the father by deed or will under section 7(1)` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian is able and willing to act and has not been removed` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, dead`
#ASSERT `the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead`
#ASSERT NOT `guardians are appointed by both parents` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(5) is engaged` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead`
#ASSERT `the guardians who act jointly under section 7(5)` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead` EQUALS `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents dead and the father's only appointee dead` EQUALS LIST "siti"

-- G. s 7(6), second limb: the court's appointee outlives the parent he was
--    appointed to act with, and that parent had appointed a guardian too.
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "siti"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` EQUALS `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, jointly with the guardian appointed by that parent`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` EQUALS `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "rajan", "siti"

-- G2. The surviving parent appointed nobody: the first limb of s 7(6).
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed nobody` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the mother who appointed nobody` EQUALS `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent`

-- G3. The parent he was appointed to act with is still alive: s 6(1)-(2),
--     second sentence, and s 7(6) has not fired yet.
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living mother` EQUALS LIST "mother"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living mother` EQUALS `he acts jointly with the surviving parent, the court having appointed him to do so`

-- G3b. THE MIRROR, and the branch nothing walked. `the parent who survived
--     the other` decides three ways -- the father alive and the mother dead,
--     the mother alive and the father dead, and both dead -- and every file
--     with a court appointee had the father dying first, so the first branch
--     could have named the wrong parent and no assertion would have moved.
--     Here the mother died and the father survives.
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living father` EQUALS LIST "father"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living father` EQUALS `he acts jointly with the surviving parent, the court having appointed him to do so`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee acting with the living father` EQUALS LIST "father", "rajan"

-- G4. AND THE MIRROR OF G, WHERE BOTH PARENTS ARE DEAD AND THE FATHER IS THE
--     ONE WHO SURVIVED. Two branches turn on this and neither had a file:
--     `the later of the two deaths` had never had to prefer its FIRST
--     argument -- every other pair has the father dying first -- and
--     `the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` had
--     never had to reach for the FATHER's appointees. Make the first branch
--     of `the later of the two deaths` yield the second person, or make the
--     survivor's-appointees test choose the mother's list, and these
--     assertions go red.
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the father who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "father"
#ASSERT `the references for the guardians appointed by the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the father who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "lim"
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the father who appointed a guardian` EQUALS `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, jointly with the guardian appointed by that parent`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the court's appointee surviving the father who appointed a guardian` EQUALS LIST "rajan", "lim"

-- K and Q. The two ways the order of the deaths can fail: a date not known,
--    and two deaths on the same day. Register entry 13 names both; only the
--    first was ever walked before.
--
--    AND s 7(6) STILL ANSWERS HALF THE QUESTION. Both parents are dead, so
--    "the death of the surviving parent" has happened whoever the survivor
--    was, and the court's appointee "shall continue to act as guardian". What
--    the facts do not fix is whom he acts jointly with. So he is a guardian
--    of this infant, and the two assertions at the end of each group say the
--    same thing twice over -- which is the check that failed silently before
--    the standing type was split.
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, both parents dead but one death undated` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, both parents dead but one death undated` EQUALS `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents dead but one death undated` EQUALS LIST "rajan"
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, both parents dead but one death undated`
#ASSERT `the reference for the parent who survived the other` `Wei Ming, both parents dead on the same day` EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, both parents dead on the same day` EQUALS `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents dead on the same day` EQUALS LIST "rajan"
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has no guardian of the person` `Wei Ming, both parents dead on the same day`
-- The genuinely undetermined case is still there and still fires: a parent
-- NOT IN EVIDENCE leaves even "does he act?" open, because a living parent
-- who objected under s 7(3) would stop him.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence, with a court-appointed guardian` EQUALS `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence, with a court-appointed guardian` EQUALS EMPTY

-- H. s 6(3)'s appointee has a standing of his own.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person` EQUALS `he acts as guardian of the infant, the infant having no parent`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person` EQUALS LIST "rajan"

-- I. s 10: removal defeats every route into guardianship.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian having been removed` EQUALS `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian having been removed` EQUALS LIST "mother"
-- The removal half of the able-and-willing test, on its own: he is alive and
-- does not refuse, so it is the removal that answers.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian is able and willing to act and has not been removed` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court`

-- N3. THE SAME UNDER s 6(2), which is where it was untested. Both subsections
--     drop a removed appointee from the count of "any guardian appointed by
--     the mother", and the mother's copy of that filter had no file at all:
--     delete it and the second assertion below is the one that goes red,
--     because a removed appointee still on the list makes the second
--     sentence's "no guardian has been appointed" limb false and its "is or
--     are dead or refuses or refuse to act" limb false as well.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the mother by will, removed by the court` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian having been removed` EQUALS `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the father under section 6(2)` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian having been removed`
#ASSERT `the father is guardian either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the mother` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian having been removed` EQUALS JUST `alone`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the mother's testamentary guardian having been removed` EQUALS LIST "father"
-- The mother is now guardian alone, so the court's power under the second
-- sentence of s 6(1) arises. Note the tension recorded at register entry 3:
-- read literally, a guardian HAS been "appointed by the father" here, and it
-- is removal that is read into the count.
#ASSERT `the court may appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother under section 6(1)` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian having been removed`

-- P and P2 again, for the two standing members a removal of the PARENT
-- produces, and for s 10's own replacement appointee.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `the mother, removed from her guardianship by the court` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` EQUALS `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Siti, appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian` `Wei Ming, the mother removed and a guardian appointed in her place` EQUALS `he acts as guardian in place of a guardian removed by the court`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` `Wei Ming, the mother's guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` EQUALS `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `the father, his guardianship under section 6(2) recorded` `Wei Ming, the father's guardianship under section 6(2) recorded` EQUALS `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
-- P3. The same record where s 6 makes nobody guardian, because both parents
--     are alive.
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded` `Wei Ming, both parents alive with the mother's guardianship recorded` EQUALS `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents alive with the mother's guardianship recorded` EQUALS EMPTY

-- X1 AND X2. s 6 MAKES A NAMED PERSON GUARDIAN, NOT WHOEVER CLAIMS THE ROUTE.
--     In P3 the parent route fails because s 6 makes NOBODY guardian; here it
--     fails for the other reason, which nothing tested -- s 6 does make a
--     parent guardian, and this record is not that parent. The first
--     assertion of each pair shows the parent-route limb IS otherwise open,
--     so the answer is the identity test doing the work. Two files, because
--     the decision asks about the mother in one branch and the father in the
--     other and a single file walks only one of them.
#ASSERT `the mother holds the guardianship section 6(1) gives her` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the mother surviving`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, wrongly recorded as guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the mother surviving` EQUALS `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the mother surviving` EQUALS LIST "mother"
#ASSERT `the father holds the guardianship section 6(2) gives him` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the father surviving`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, wrongly recorded as guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the father surviving` EQUALS `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, a stranger recorded on the parent route, the father surviving` EQUALS LIST "father"

-- I2. WHICH OF THE FIRST TWO BRANCHES WINS when both answer. The court
--     removed this guardian and he has since died, so `he has been removed
--     from his guardianship by the court` and `he is dead or refuses to act`
--     are both true of him and only the branch order decides which the module
--     reports. Nothing turns on it legally -- he does not act either way, and
--     the assertion under `Which standings are standings in which he acts`
--     says so of both members -- but the choice is now pinned instead of
--     accidental, and the reason for it is that s 10 is what ENDED the
--     guardianship: he died a former guardian, not a guardian.
#ASSERT `the guardian has been removed from his guardianship by the court` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court and since dead`
#ASSERT `the guardian is dead or refuses to act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court and since dead`
#ASSERT `the standing of the guardian under this Act` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court and since dead` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian removed and since dead` EQUALS `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian removed and since dead` EQUALS LIST "mother"

§§§ `Which standings are standings in which he acts -- all sixteen members`

-- The classification is TOTAL over `The standing of a guardian under this
-- Act`, and this is where that is checked. Six of the sixteen members are not
-- standings in which he acts; the other ten are.
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he has been removed from his guardianship by the court`
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he is dead or refuses to act`
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `whether he acts, and with whom, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, but which parent survived, and so whom he acts jointly with, cannot be determined on the facts recorded`
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `the appointment has not taken effect, the parent who appointed him being alive`
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `the mother or father objects to his so acting, and he does not act jointly`
#ASSERT NOT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `section 6 does not make him guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he acts jointly with the mother or father, as the case may be`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `after the death of the surviving parent the guardians appointed by both parents act jointly`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `after the death of the surviving parent he acts as guardian of the infant`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he acts jointly with the surviving parent, the court having appointed him to do so`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent, jointly with the guardian appointed by that parent`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he continues to act after the death of the surviving parent`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he acts as guardian of the infant, the infant having no parent`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he acts as guardian in place of a guardian removed by the court`
#ASSERT `the standing is one in which he acts as guardian of the infant` `he is guardian by virtue of surviving the other parent`

§§§ `Section 8 -- joint guardians who cannot agree`

-- "act as joint guardians" is read off each guardian's own record, not off
-- membership of the infant's list.
#ASSERT `the guardian acts jointly with one or more other persons` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian acts jointly with one or more other persons` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, refusing to act`
-- `jointly with` naming NOBODY is not joint action either.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian acts jointly with one or more other persons` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, recorded as acting jointly with nobody`

#ASSERT `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead`
#ASSERT `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
-- The record limb closed: he says he acts jointly with nobody.
#ASSERT NOT `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the guardian recorded as acting jointly with nobody`
-- THE STANDING LIMB CLOSED ON ITS OWN, which nothing did before. In J the
-- record says in terms that Lim acts "jointly with" the mother -- the first
-- assertion below is the same record answering TRUE to the other conjunct --
-- but both parents are alive, so his appointment has not taken effect and the
-- Act does not make him act jointly with anybody yet. What a record says a
-- guardian is doing and what the Act says he may do are two questions, and
-- s 8's gate is about the second. Delete the standing conjunct and this is
-- the assertion that goes red. (Lim's record answering TRUE to the other
-- conjunct is the first assertion in this section, above.)
#ASSERT NOT `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will`
-- Both limbs closed at once: an empty list of guardians answers FALSE before
-- either conjunct is reached, so this one is over-determined and is kept only
-- because "nobody is a guardian" is the ordinary way s 8 fails.
#ASSERT NOT `2 or more persons act as joint guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian`
-- The two limbs of s 8's gate, each closed on its own.
#ASSERT     `any of them may apply to the court for its direction under section 8` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `any of them may apply to the court for its direction under section 8` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `any of them may apply to the court for its direction under section 8` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian` TRUE

§§§ `Section 9 -- attachment of a pension or income`

`an application to attach, everything in place` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS TRUE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS TRUE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS TRUE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS TRUE

`an application to attach, with no order for payment of money` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS FALSE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS TRUE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS TRUE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS TRUE

`an application to attach, with no pension or income` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS TRUE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS FALSE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS FALSE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS TRUE

-- THE SECOND LIMB ON ITS OWN. s 9(1) says "where there is any pension or
-- income payable to the person against whom the order was made and capable of
-- being attached", which is two facts and not one, and the record above
-- splits them because the subsection does. This file closes the FIRST of the
-- two and leaves the second open: nothing is payable to the judgment debtor,
-- though a pension of the kind in question would be attachable if there were
-- one -- a pension in payment to somebody else, or one this debtor has not
-- yet become entitled to. The file above closes both at once and so cannot
-- tell which limb answered.
`an application to attach, no pension payable but such pensions attachable` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS TRUE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS FALSE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS TRUE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS TRUE

`an application to attach a pension not capable of being attached` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS TRUE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS TRUE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS FALSE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS TRUE

`an application to attach, the payer not yet heard` MEANS `An application to attach a pension or income under section 9(1)` WITH
    `the court has made an order under this Act for payment of money` IS TRUE
    `there is a pension or income payable to the person against whom the order was made` IS TRUE
    `the pension or income is capable of being attached` IS TRUE
    `the person by whom the pension or income is payable has been given an opportunity of being heard` IS FALSE

#ASSERT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach, everything in place`
-- Each of the four limbs closed on its own, with the other three shown open
-- on the same file.
#ASSERT NOT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach, with no order for payment of money`
-- Limb 2 alone. Delete the "there is a pension or income payable" conjunct
-- and this is the assertion that goes red; the over-determined file below it
-- would stay green, which is what it was doing before this one existed.
#ASSERT NOT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach, no pension payable but such pensions attachable`
#ASSERT NOT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach a pension not capable of being attached`
-- Limbs 2 and 3 closed together.
#ASSERT NOT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach, with no pension or income`
-- "after giving the person by whom the pension or income is payable an
-- opportunity of being heard" is a condition on the power, not advice.
#ASSERT NOT `the court has power under section 9(1) to order the attachment` `an application to attach, the payer not yet heard`

-- The two things the further order "shall be", once it has been made.
#ASSERT     `the further order is an authority to the person by whom the pension or income is payable to make the payment so ordered` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the further order is an authority to the person by whom the pension or income is payable to make the payment so ordered` FALSE
#ASSERT     `the receipt is a good discharge to the person by whom the pension or income is payable` TRUE  TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the receipt is a good discharge to the person by whom the pension or income is payable` TRUE  FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the receipt is a good discharge to the person by whom the pension or income is payable` FALSE TRUE

§§§ `Section 11 -- which matters the court must consider`

-- s 11(a)'s "has a parent or parents" is read as in evidence AND alive; both
-- halves of that reading are exercised here. See register entry 10.
#ASSERT `the infant has a parent or parents` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
-- The other parent answers it too: the limb is a disjunction, and until this
-- assertion existed only the MOTHER had ever been the parent the infant "has".
#ASSERT `the infant has a parent or parents` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `the matters the court must consider under section 11` `Wei Ming, the mother having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST `the wishes of the parent or both of them, as the case may be`, `the wishes of the infant`
-- Both parents in evidence, both dead.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has a parent or parents` `Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights`
-- One parent dead, the other not in evidence.
#ASSERT NOT `the infant has a parent or parents` `Wei Ming, the mother dead and the father not in evidence`
#ASSERT `the matters the court must consider under section 11` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST `the wishes of the parent or both of them, as the case may be`, `the wishes of the infant`
#ASSERT `the matters the court must consider under section 11` `Wei Ming, too young to express an independent opinion` EQUALS LIST `the wishes of the parent or both of them, as the case may be`
#ASSERT `the matters the court must consider under section 11` `Wei Ming, an orphan with no guardian and nobody with parental rights` EQUALS LIST `the wishes of the infant`
#ASSERT `the matters the court must consider under section 11` `Wei Ming, an orphan too young to express an independent opinion` EQUALS EMPTY

§§§ `Section 15 -- security`

-- "Where a person appointed by the court is the guardian of an infant’s
-- property": all three of the court's appointment routes satisfy the first
-- half, and no other route does.
#ASSERT `a person appointed by the court` `Rajan, appointed by the court to act jointly with the surviving parent`
#ASSERT `a person appointed by the court` `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent`
#ASSERT `a person appointed by the court` `Siti, appointed by the court in place of a removed guardian`
#ASSERT NOT `a person appointed by the court` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`
#ASSERT NOT `a person appointed by the court` `the mother, her guardianship under section 6(1) recorded`

#ASSERT `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
#ASSERT `the guardian has not given the security section 15(1) requires` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has not given the security section 15(1) requires` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has given security`
-- The dispensation: the duty itself falls away, so a guardian excused
-- security is not in breach for want of it.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` `a court-appointed guardian of the property excused security by the court`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has not given the security section 15(1) requires` `a court-appointed guardian of the property excused security by the court`
-- Not a person appointed by the court, so s 15(1) does not reach him at all.
-- The FIRST limb closed, with the second shown open.
#ASSERT `a guardian of the property of the infant` `a testamentary guardian of the property`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` `a testamentary guardian of the property`
-- AND THE SECOND LIMB CLOSED ON ITS OWN, which nothing did before. s 15(1)
-- reaches "the guardian of an infant’s property"; this man was appointed by
-- the court -- so the first limb is open, and the assertion above it says so
-- -- but he is a guardian of the PERSON, and the court has not dispensed with
-- security, so the third conjunct is open too. Delete the property limb and
-- this is the assertion that goes red.
#ASSERT `a person appointed by the court` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian must give security under section 15(1)` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`

§§§ `Section 16 -- limitation of the guardian's powers`

-- Who is "A guardian of the property of an infant", in the section's own
-- opening words, and who is a guardian of
-- the person, over all three kinds of guardianship.
#ASSERT NOT `a guardian of the property of the infant` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`
#ASSERT `a guardian of the property of the infant` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
#ASSERT `a guardian of the property of the infant` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`
#ASSERT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`
#ASSERT NOT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
#ASSERT `a guardian of the person of the infant` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother`

#ASSERT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property who sold without permission`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property who sold with permission`
#ASSERT `the disposal is one that may be declared void under section 16(2)` `a guardian of the property who sold without permission`
#ASSERT NOT `the disposal is one that may be declared void under section 16(2)` `a guardian of the property who sold with permission`
-- THE THRESHOLD ON LIMB (b), both sides and on it: "exceeding one year" is
-- strict, so a lease OF one year is outside the prohibition and needs no
-- permission, and a lease of three years is inside it.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` `a guardian of the property who granted a one-year lease`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has done a thing section 16(1) forbids` `a guardian of the property who granted a one-year lease`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property who granted a one-year lease`
#ASSERT `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` `a guardian of the property who granted a three-year lease`
#ASSERT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property who granted a three-year lease`
-- The term not in evidence: the prohibition is not made out (register 6).
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` `a guardian of the property who granted a lease of unrecorded term`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property who granted a lease of unrecorded term`
-- AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME LIMB. This file and the three-year-lease
-- file above differ in exactly one fact -- whether land was leased at all --
-- and carry the same recorded term, so the FALSE here is the lease and not
-- the arithmetic. Without that conjunct a term noted on a file would make the
-- prohibition bite though no lease was ever granted.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has leased land belonging to the infant for a term exceeding one year` `a guardian of the property with a three-year term noted but no lease granted`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has done a thing section 16(1) forbids` `a guardian of the property with a three-year term noted but no lease granted`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the property with a three-year term noted but no lease granted`
-- s 16(1) opens "A guardian of the property of an infant", so a guardian of
-- the person is outside it however he deals with the property -- and the
-- fixture is built so that the forbidden act IS made out against him, which
-- is what the first of the pair shows.
#ASSERT `the guardian has done a thing section 16(1) forbids` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has contravened section 16(1)` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`
-- s 16(3): the court may not permit unless it is necessary or advisable.
#ASSERT NOT `section 16(3) forbids the court to permit the dealing` TRUE
#ASSERT     `section 16(3) forbids the court to permit the dealing` FALSE

§§§ `Section 17 -- the discharge for capital moneys`

#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `a guardian of the property who gave a discharge with no order of the court`
#ASSERT `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `a guardian of the property who gave a discharge with no order of the court`
-- "unless in any case the court otherwise orders".
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `a guardian of the property who gave a discharge the court had authorised`
#ASSERT NOT `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `a guardian of the property who gave a discharge the court had authorised`
-- No discharge purported at all: the want of power stands, but nothing has
-- gone wrong.
#ASSERT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
#ASSERT NOT `the discharge the guardian purported to give is not a good discharge` `a court-appointed guardian of the property who has done nothing`
-- And the guardian of the person is outside s 17 too, though his record says
-- he purported to give a discharge.
#ASSERT NOT `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a good discharge` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`

§§§ `Section 18 -- maintenance out of income, and the cap`

#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA monthly provision of $80` EQUALS `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
-- "exceeding $100 per month" is strict, so exactly $100 is within the
-- guardian's own power and $101 is not.
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA monthly provision of $100` EQUALS `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA monthly provision of $101` EQUALS `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA monthly provision of $150` EQUALS `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
-- THE WEEKLY BOUNDARY, on the 52/12 reading of register entry 8: the cap is
-- reached at $23.08 a week, so $23 is within ($99.67 a month) and $24 is not
-- ($104.00). $20 and $30 are the same question further from the line.
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA weekly provision of $20` EQUALS `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA weekly provision of $23` EQUALS `the sum applied does not exceed $100 per month`
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA weekly provision of $24` EQUALS `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA weekly provision of $30` EQUALS `the sum applied exceeds $100 per month`
-- An "other periodical" sum is not converted: the Act supplies no rule, and
-- the verdict says so rather than guessing one.
#ASSERT `whether the provision exceeds the sum applicable without the permission of the court` `a GIA provision of $90 on some other period` EQUALS `the period of the sum applied is neither weekly nor monthly, and the Act supplies no conversion to a monthly rate`

#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has applied a sum exceeding $100 per month without the permission of the court` `a guardian of the property applying $80 a month`
#ASSERT `the guardian has applied a sum exceeding $100 per month without the permission of the court` `a guardian of the property applying $150 a month without permission`
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has applied a sum exceeding $100 per month without the permission of the court` `a guardian of the property applying $150 a month with permission`
-- The guardian of the person is outside s 18(1), though his record carries a
-- $150 monthly provision.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian has applied a sum exceeding $100 per month without the permission of the court` `a guardian of the person only, who has parted with possession`

-- s 18(2): the gate on resort to capital, each limb on its own and neither.
#ASSERT     `the court may order provision to be made out of the capital of the infant's property under section 18(2)` TRUE  FALSE
#ASSERT     `the court may order provision to be made out of the capital of the infant's property under section 18(2)` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT     `the court may order provision to be made out of the capital of the infant's property under section 18(2)` TRUE  TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the court may order provision to be made out of the capital of the infant's property under section 18(2)` FALSE FALSE
-- The conclusion drawn from ss 16(1) and 18 together, entered as an
-- interpretation at register entry 15.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may of his own motion make provision out of the capital of the infant's property`

§§§ `Section 20 -- application for the court's opinion`

-- "Any guardian": a guardian of the PERSON may apply too, so long as the
-- question respects the management or administration of the property.
#ASSERT `the guardian may apply to the court for its opinion, advice or direction under section 20` `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court for its opinion, advice or direction under section 20` `Rajan, appointed by the court where the infant has no parent` `Wei Ming, an orphan with a court-appointed guardian of the person` FALSE
-- A person whose appointment has not taken effect is not yet a guardian.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court for its opinion, advice or direction under section 20` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, acting jointly with the mother` `Wei Ming, both parents alive, the father having appointed a guardian by will` TRUE
-- Nor is one the court has removed.
#ASSERT NOT `the guardian may apply to the court for its opinion, advice or direction under section 20` `Lim, appointed by the father by will, removed by the court` `Wei Ming, the testamentary guardian having been removed` TRUE

§§§ `Section 5A -- enforcement of a child access order`

`a breach of the access order` MEANS `A breach of an access order` WITH
    `the reference for X, the person given access to the child by the access order` IS "X"
    `the reference for Y, the person required by the order to give X access to the child` IS "Y"
    `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child` IS TRUE
    `the order is breached by Y` IS TRUE

`an access order that has not been breached` MEANS `A breach of an access order` WITH
    `the reference for X, the person given access to the child by the access order` IS "X"
    `the reference for Y, the person required by the order to give X access to the child` IS "Y"
    `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child` IS TRUE
    `the order is breached by Y` IS FALSE

`no access order at all` MEANS `A breach of an access order` WITH
    `the reference for X, the person given access to the child by the access order` IS "X"
    `the reference for Y, the person required by the order to give X access to the child` IS "Y"
    `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child` IS FALSE
    `the order is breached by Y` IS FALSE

-- PARAGRAPH (a) FALSE AND PARAGRAPH (b) TRUE, which is the only combination
-- that isolates (a). It is a case file that records a breach of an access
-- order the court never made, and it is not a fanciful one: the complaint
-- that "Y is not letting me see the child" arrives before anybody has checked
-- whether there is an order to breach, and s 5A(1)(a) is the check. A file
-- with BOTH paragraphs false answers FALSE whatever paragraph (a) says, which
-- is what `no access order at all` used to be doing on its own.
`a breach recorded of an access order that was never made` MEANS `A breach of an access order` WITH
    `the reference for X, the person given access to the child by the access order` IS "X"
    `the reference for Y, the person required by the order to give X access to the child` IS "Y"
    `the court makes an order under section 5 giving X access to a child` IS FALSE
    `the order is breached by Y` IS TRUE

-- s 5A(1)'s gate is a conjunction of its two paragraphs; each closed on its
-- own, with the other shown OPEN on the same file.
#ASSERT `section 5A applies` `a breach of the access order`
-- (b) closed, (a) open.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5A applies` `an access order that has not been breached`
-- (a) closed, (b) open. Delete paragraph (a) from the rule and this is the
-- assertion that goes red.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5A applies` `a breach recorded of an access order that was never made`
#ASSERT NOT `X may apply to the court to enforce the access order against Y` `a breach recorded of an access order that was never made`
-- Both closed together: over-determined, and kept only because a file with
-- neither is the ordinary case in which s 5A is not in play at all.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5A applies` `no access order at all`
#ASSERT `X may apply to the court to enforce the access order against Y` `a breach of the access order`
#ASSERT NOT `X may apply to the court to enforce the access order against Y` `no access order at all`

`a fine of $20,000`            MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS JUST 20_000, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS NOTHING
`a fine of $20,001`            MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS JUST 20_001, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS NOTHING
`a fine of $25,000`            MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS JUST 25_000, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS NOTHING
`imprisonment for 12 months`   MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS NOTHING, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS JUST 12
`imprisonment for 13 months`   MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS NOTHING, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS JUST 13
`imprisonment for 18 months`   MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS NOTHING, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS JUST 18
`a fine of $5,000 and 6 months` MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS JUST 5_000, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS JUST 6
`a fine of $25,000 and 6 months` MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS JUST 25_000, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS JUST 6
`neither a fine nor imprisonment` MEANS `A sentence under section 5A(3)(e)` WITH `the fine imposed, in dollars` IS NOTHING, `the term of imprisonment imposed, in months` IS NOTHING

-- BOTH CEILINGS, both sides and on the line: "not exceeding" is AT MOST, so
-- $20,000 and 12 months are themselves within the section and $20,001 and 13
-- months are not.
#ASSERT     `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `a fine of $20,000`
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `a fine of $20,001`
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `a fine of $25,000`
#ASSERT     `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `imprisonment for 12 months`
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `imprisonment for 13 months`
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `imprisonment for 18 months`
-- "or to both" -- the third sentence the paragraph offers.
#ASSERT     `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `a fine of $5,000 and 6 months`
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `a fine of $25,000 and 6 months`
-- A sentence of neither is not a sentence under (3)(e) at all.
#ASSERT NOT `the sentence is one the court may pass under section 5A(3)(e)` `neither a fine nor imprisonment`

-- s 5A(4): the additional access ordered may not exceed the entitlement.
-- "more access than" is strict, so equality is within.
#ASSERT     `the order under section 5A(3)(a) is within section 5A(4)` 4 8
#ASSERT     `the order under section 5A(3)(a) is within section 5A(4)` 8 8
#ASSERT NOT `the order under section 5A(3)(a) is within section 5A(4)` 9 8

-- s 5A(5)(b): the dispensation exists only where the question was reserved.
#ASSERT     `the court may return to the question subsequently without X having to make a fresh application` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the court may return to the question subsequently without X having to make a fresh application` FALSE

-- s 5A(6): either, but not both. Only the fourth state is forbidden.
#ASSERT     `X has kept within the election permitted by section 5A(6)` `neither`
#ASSERT     `X has kept within the election permitted by section 5A(6)` `made an application under subsection (2)`
#ASSERT     `X has kept within the election permitted by section 5A(6)` `brought proceedings to punish Y for contempt of court in respect of that breach`
#ASSERT NOT `X has kept within the election permitted by section 5A(6)` `both made an application under subsection (2) and brought proceedings for contempt`

-- s 5A(8): a Family Court may enforce but may not vary.
#ASSERT     `a Family Court has power to take the step` `enforcement in accordance with section 5A`
#ASSERT NOT `a Family Court has power to take the step` `variation of the order`

§§§ `Section 21 -- money paid into a District Court`

-- The exception runs the other way from ss 15-18: the Act does NOT apply to
-- this money, and the court's order brings it back in.
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money paid into a District Court to the credit of an infant in any action or proceeding in a District Court` FALSE EQUALS `nothing in this Act applies to the money, and the same shall be dealt with according to the Rules of Court`
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money paid into a District Court to the credit of an infant in any action or proceeding in a District Court` TRUE EQUALS `this Act applies to the money`
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money in a District Court ordered to be transferred to the credit of an infant` FALSE EQUALS `nothing in this Act applies to the money, and the same shall be dealt with according to the Rules of Court`
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money in a District Court ordered to be transferred to the credit of an infant` TRUE EQUALS `this Act applies to the money`
-- Money of neither kind is never taken out of the Act in the first place, so
-- an order changes nothing.
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money of neither kind described in section 21` FALSE EQUALS `this Act applies to the money`
#ASSERT `whether this Act applies to the money` `money of neither kind described in section 21` TRUE EQUALS `this Act applies to the money`


§§§ `The Act as a whole -- the gate, and the integrity of the record`

-- THE GATE, on one set of facts and three dates. Wei Ming was born
-- 19 July 2012 and his construed majority date is 19 July 2033; the guardians
-- his facts produce are the mother and Lim, which is what
-- `the persons who are guardians of the infant` answers unconditionally above.
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2026 8 26) EQUALS `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "mother", "lim")
-- THE BOUNDARY, from both sides, the same day either side of it as the
-- infancy predicate's own assertions above.
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2033 7 18) EQUALS `the person is an infant on that date, and the guardians of the infant are` (LIST "mother", "lim")
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2033 7 19) EQUALS `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`
-- THE CASE THE AUDIT NAMED: a person of full age in an `Infant` record. The
-- sections above still make the mother his guardian -- they never ask -- and
-- this is the decision that objects.
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` EQUALS LIST "mother", "lim"
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2035 1 1) EQUALS `the person has attained the age of majority on that date, so this Act has no infant to govern`
-- THE THIRD OUTCOME, which is where this Act parts from
-- probate-administration-act.l4: the same silence that Act folds to FALSE is
-- reported here as an answer of its own, on the same date at which the infant
-- with a majority date is plainly an infant.
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2026 8 26) EQUALS `whether the person is an infant on that date cannot be determined, the date on which he attains the age of majority not being in evidence`
#ASSERT `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to govern` `Wei Ming, majority date not in evidence, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian` (YMD 2035 1 1) EQUALS `whether the person is an infant on that date cannot be determined, the date on which he attains the age of majority not being in evidence`

-- THE INTEGRITY CHECK. Every guardian in the ordinary fixtures is recorded
-- against "infant", which is Wei Ming's own reference, so the check passes on
-- them.
#ASSERT `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` `Wei Ming, the father having died leaving a testamentary guardian`
#ASSERT `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` `Wei Ming, guardians appointed by both parents, both parents dead`
-- Vacuously true where the list is empty, which is the honest answer: there
-- is nothing recorded to disagree.
#ASSERT `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` `Wei Ming, the father having appointed no guardian`
-- AND THE CASE IT IS FOR. The same guardian, pointed at a different infant.
-- Note that ss 6 and 7 answer identically on both fixtures -- the mother and
-- Lim -- which is precisely why the check has to exist somewhere.
#ASSERT NOT `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded against this infant` `Wei Ming, a guardian recorded against a different infant`
#ASSERT `the persons who are guardians of the infant` `Wei Ming, a guardian recorded against a different infant` EQUALS LIST "mother", "lim"


§§ `Where this Act joins the other three`

-- FOUR JOINS: two that reach INTO this Act, one that reaches into the office
-- this Act creates, and one that reaches OUT of it. Until this section was
-- written this module named the Wills Act 1838 exactly once -- in the
-- ambiguity register, and only to disclaim its s 4's "21" -- and named the
-- Probate and Administration Act 1934 and the Intestate Succession Act 1967
-- not at all. Both of those modules named this one, and one of them wrote a
-- hand-off contract for a provision this file had never heard of. A module
-- that documents its own boundary well and its neighbours' not at all is
-- exactly how a typed hand-off goes untaken.
--
-- (1) WILLS ACT 1838 s 27(3) -> THIS ACT s 7(1)-(2). INTO this Act, and the
--     only one of the four that the other side states in terms. A privileged
--     will that is, or that would have been, rendered valid by Wills Act
--     s 27 makes good "any appointment contained in that will of any person
--     as guardian of the infant children of the testator" -- so the
--     appointment this Act's s 7(1)-(2) contemplates can be good even where
--     the will would otherwise fail. wills-act.l4's
--     `s 27(3) — the appointment of a guardian of the infant children of the
--     testator is of full force and effect` is the decision, and it asks this
--     module to read its answer for a guardian appointed by will.
--     WHAT THIS MODULE DOES ABOUT IT: it takes the appointment as ALREADY
--     CONSTRUED, and says so at s 7(1)-(2) above, where the reasoning and the
--     instruction to a combined case file are set out at length. In short:
--     `How the guardian was appointed`'s `appointed by the father by will`
--     and `appointed by the mother by will` are to be set only where the
--     Wills Act decision has answered TRUE; a will-appointed guardian is
--     otherwise treated here exactly as a deed-appointed one, and this Act
--     imposes no condition on the instrument.
--     WHY IT CANNOT READ THE ANSWER: no field the two modules share carries
--     it. See `Where the shared ontology stopped short` below.
--     WHAT EACH SIDE OWNS, once the appointment is good: the Wills Act's
--     s 27(3) and nothing after it; this Act's ss 7(3)-(6) (joint action, the
--     objection, the court's order, the meeting of two sets of appointees),
--     s 8 and s 10. Removal is s 10's and does not unmake the appointment,
--     which is the reading wills-act.l4 takes at its own site and this module
--     agrees with -- see s 7(1)-(2) above.
--
-- (2) PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT 1934 s 6(2), s 21, s 29(8)(a) AND
--     s 63(1) -> "AN INFANT". INTO this Act, but only in the sense that the
--     word is this Act's: none of the four Acts defines an infant, and the age
--     of majority comes from outside all of them. Both modules therefore read
--     `family-domain.l4`'s `the date on which the person attains the age of
--     majority`, and both compare it strictly, so wherever both give an
--     answer they give the same one. They differ in SHAPE and only in the
--     silent case: this module returns a `MAYBE BOOLEAN` and keeps
--     not-in-evidence as a third answer, and probate-administration-act.l4
--     folds it to FALSE on the strength of its own s 6(3). Both readings are
--     argued at their own sites, and this module's site now names the other
--     two -- see `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix` above and
--     ambiguity register entry 17 below. NOTHING IS
--     WRONG HERE, and it is worth saying so plainly, because two Acts giving
--     one question two shapes usually means one of them is: the Wills Act's
--     own "21" is a third treatment again and is right not to read the shared
--     field at all, because Wills Act s 4 states a number and s 27(1)
--     disapplies it for a soldier or a mariner.
--
-- (3) PROBATE AND ADMINISTRATION ACT 1934 s 21(2) -> THE OFFICE THIS ACT
--     CREATES. That subsection lets letters of administration be granted "to
--     the guardian of the person and property of the infant", and
--     probate-administration-act.l4 records that it can decide WHETHER such a
--     grant may be made and cannot decide WHO the guardian is. WHO is this
--     module's answer, and it is
--     `who are the guardians of the infant, if this Act has an infant to
--     govern` -- or, without the date gate,
--     `the persons who are guardians of the infant`. Both return REFERENCES,
--     which is the currency `Grantee`'s
--     `the reference for the person for whose use and benefit the grant is
--     made` is already in.
--     WHAT THE OTHER SIDE COULD DO WITH THAT, stated because the audit found
--     that nothing anywhere tests it: a grantee taking `as the guardian of the
--     person and property of the infant` for reference r ought to appear in
--     the `the guardians of the infant` list of the infant whose reference is
--     r. That is the shape probate-administration-act.l4 already uses for its
--     own s 29(8)(a). It is not written here because that is a rule about a
--     GRANT and this Act makes none.
--     WHAT THIS SIDE HAS DONE ABOUT IT: `Guardian`'s
--     `the reference for the infant` -- the third form the one link takes --
--     now has a reader, `every guardian recorded for this infant is recorded
--     against this infant` in `The Act as a whole` above, labelled as an
--     integrity check on a case file and not as a rule of this Act.
--
-- (4) THIS ACT s 17 -> WILLS ACT 1838 AND INTESTATE SUCCESSION ACT 1967
--     ENTITLEMENT. OUT of this Act, and the only join of the four whose
--     pointer runs in one direction. s 17 is this Act's direct bite on
--     succession -- what stands between an infant being ENTITLED to a share
--     and the share being validly PAID OVER. The entitlement is computed by
--     intestate-succession-act.l4's `the persons entitled to succeed
--     beneficially, and their shares` on an intestacy, and by wills-act.l4's
--     dispositions under a will; neither of those ACTS says anything about
--     paying an infant's share to anybody, and the Act that would,
--     the Probate and Administration Act 1934, is not encoded that far --
--     probate-administration-act.l4 puts its ss 57-58 and both Schedules out
--     of scope. Argued in full at s 17 above.
--     AND IT IS THE ONE JOIN OF THE FOUR THAT IS NOT EVEN TYPED. Joins (1) to
--     (3) run over objects the four modules share -- `Guardian`, `Person`,
--     `Infant` -- so a combined file could exercise them today. This one has
--     no object to run over: `family-domain.l4` declares `Entitlement`, and
--     intestate-succession-act.l4 RETURNS a `LIST OF Entitlement` from its
--     s 7 rules, but no field anywhere in the ontology has that type, so an
--     entitlement is computed and never stored and nothing carries it from
--     that module to this one. probate-administration-act.l4 reports the same
--     gap from its own side, at its s 29(5). Reported here as a domain gap;
--     not fixed here, because `family-domain.l4` must stay still while three
--     other Act modules build against it.
--
-- AND TWO PLACES WHERE A JOIN MIGHT BE EXPECTED AND THERE IS NONE.
--   s 5A, THE ACCESS ORDER, reaches out of this Act four times -- to the
--     Criminal Procedure Code 2010 and the Family Justice Rules (s 5A(7)), to
--     section 47(1) of the Family Justice Act 2014 (s 5A(8)) and to "a family
--     support programme as defined in section 139A of the Women’s Charter
--     1961" (s 5A(3)(c)(iv)) -- and to none of the other three Acts. Nothing
--     in succession touches it.
--   "ESTATE" IN THIS ACT IS NOT `Estate` IN THE ONTOLOGY. The word occurs in
--     two operative places -- s 16(2), "restoring to the infant’s estate the
--     property so disposed of", and s 19(2), "an account of his dealings with
--     the infant’s estate" -- and in both it is the INFANT'S own property, not
--     a deceased person's. `family-domain`'s `Estate` is a deceased person's
--     and belongs to the other three Acts. So this is a false join, and it is
--     named because the word invites the mistake.
--
-- NOTHING HERE IS YET EXERCISED ON A SHARED FILE, and that is the honest
-- limit of everything above. No assertion in this module tests any join: every
-- fixture here answers this Act's questions only. The file that would test
-- them, `family-cases.l4` -- named in the cleanroom brief's own file table --
-- does not exist, and could not have been written while the four Act modules
-- could not be imported together at all.
--
-- WHAT HAS BEEN DONE TOWARDS IT, from this side: this module is now
-- importable beside the other three. THE TAG CONVENTION, stated exactly,
-- because a convention nobody can check is not one. A name here carries the
-- tag `GIA`, placed immediately after its determiner, when its name describes
-- a SHARED TYPE generically and says nothing about this Act -- so that another
-- Act module could reasonably have chosen the same name for a different
-- thing. In this file that is fifteen names: the five record-building
-- constructors (`a GIA person`, `a GIA infant`,
-- `a GIA guardian, in the ordinary case`,
-- `a GIA guardian, on the two limbs of section 7(4)`,
-- `a GIA guardian of the property of the infant, in a property case`) and the
-- ten `A periodical sum` values (`no GIA provision`,
-- `a GIA monthly provision of $100` and their siblings). NOTHING ELSE IS
-- TAGGED, and in particular the statutory decisions are not: a reader holding
-- the Act must be able to find `section 17 denies the guardian power to give a
-- good discharge` by reading s 17. Fixture PEOPLE are not tagged either --
-- each is already named for a person and a fact of this Act's own scenarios,
-- and none of them collides with anything in the other three modules.
--
-- MEASURED, not assumed: with all four Act modules imported into one file,
-- this module's decisions resolve and evaluate. Renaming the fifteen changed
-- no answer -- the module reported 392 satisfied assertions before and 392
-- after, with the run output identical but for source column offsets.


§§ `Sections not encoded, and why`

-- A READER WHO CANNOT TELL WHAT THE ACT DOES NOT SAY FROM WHAT WE DID NOT
-- ENCODE HAS BEEN MISLED. Everything below is real statutory material in this
-- Act and none of it is encoded. Each entry names the provision and gives
-- the reason; where a section is split, the entry says which half was kept.
--
-- LEFT OUT BECAUSE THERE IS NO DETERMINATE OUTPUT -- a discretion, and a
-- boolean standing in for a discretion is a lie:
--
--   s 3, second limb -- "shall regard the welfare of the infant as the first
--     and paramount consideration". A direction about WEIGHT. The gate and
--     the neutrality rule are encoded; this is not, and cannot be.
--   s 5 in whole -- "make orders as it may think fit regarding the custody
--     of such infant, the right of access thereto and the payment of any sum
--     towards the maintenance of the infant".
--   s 5A(3)(a)-(d) -- "the court may do all or any of the following". Four
--     discretionary powers. (3)(e) is encoded because it alone carries
--     computable limits. (3)(c) also defers to "a family support programme
--     as defined in section 139A of the Women’s Charter 1961", which is
--     outside this Act.
--   s 5A(5)(a) -- "may ... reserve the question". (5)(b) IS encoded, because
--     it says what X need not do.
--   s 5A(7) -- the manner of an application to a Family Court. Pure
--     procedure, and expressly displaceable -- "Unless the Family Justice
--     Rules provide otherwise" -- by rules not in this Act.
--   s 6(1) and s 6(2), second sentence, CONSEQUENT -- "the court may if it
--     thinks fit appoint a guardian to act jointly with the mother". Only
--     the antecedent is encoded.
--   s 6(3), CONSEQUENT -- "the court, on the application of any person, may,
--     if it thinks fit, appoint the applicant". Only the three-limbed gate
--     is encoded.
--   s 7(4), the CHOICE of order -- the court "may either refuse ... or make
--     an order ...". Which of the three it makes is not computable; what
--     follows from each IS, and is encoded.
--   s 7(4), the ancillary orders -- custody and access "as ... the court may
--     think fit", and a periodical sum "as ... the court may consider
--     reasonable". The GATE on them ("in the later case") is encoded.
--   s 8, the order -- "such order regarding the matters in difference as it
--     may think proper", and paragraphs (a), (b) and (c) inside it. The gate
--     on the application is encoded.
--   s 9(1), the SIZE of the attachment and the PAYEES -- "such part as the
--     court may think fit ... be attached and paid to the persons named by
--     the court". The GATE on the power is encoded, and so are the two things
--     the further order "shall be" once made: an authority to the payer, and
--     a receipt that is a good discharge to him. An earlier draft of this
--     register declined the whole subsection on the footing that "nothing
--     follows from facts", which was wrong.
--   s 10, the POWER -- "The court may remove from his guardianship any
--     guardian, and may appoint another guardian in his place". The EFFECT
--     of a removal is encoded, and is read in every count above.
--   s 11, first limb -- "must have regard primarily to the welfare of the
--     infant". Weight again. WHICH matters must be considered is encoded.
--   s 11A in whole -- "shall, whenever it is practicable, have regard to the
--     advice of a person ... but shall not be bound to follow such advice".
--     Conditioned on a judgement, and its content is weight; the closing
--     words withhold a duty rather than imposing one.
--   s 12 in whole -- "the whole or any part of the sum ... to be applied by
--     that person for the benefit of the infant in such manner as ... the
--     court may direct". Its antecedent also reaches into the terms of a
--     trust, which this cleanroom does not model.
--   s 13 in whole -- "may ... direct any person appearing to have the
--     custody of an infant to produce the infant", and "such order for the
--     temporary custody and protection of the infant as the court thinks
--     fit".
--   s 14 in whole -- "the court may order that he be returned to such
--     custody". Who his "lawful guardian" is IS computed, by
--     `the persons who are guardians of the infant`.
--   s 16(2), the CONSEQUENT -- "may be declared void", and the restoring
--     order that follows a declaration. The antecedent is encoded.
--   s 18(1), first limb -- "reasonable provision ... having regard to his
--     station in life". The cap after the semicolon is encoded.
--   s 18(2), the ORDER -- "the court may order that provision ... be made
--     out of the capital", and the authorisation of a sale or mortgage. The
--     gate is encoded.
--   s 19 in whole -- "If it appears that, having regard to the station in
--     life of an infant and to the value of his property and to all the
--     circumstances of the case, it would be expedient", and then "in his
--     sole and uncontrolled discretion". Discretionary at both ends. Its
--     appointee is deliberately not a `Guardian`: s 19(1) has the court act
--     "instead of appointing a guardian of the property of the infant".
--   s 20, the OPINION -- what the court says on the question. Who may apply,
--     and on what questions, is encoded.
--
-- LEFT OUT BECAUSE THERE IS NOTHING TO DECIDE -- no antecedent, no output:
--
--   s 9(2) -- "This section shall be binding on the Government." A statement
--     about whom s 9 binds. A decision that returns TRUE on every input
--     decides nothing, and writing one would suggest the Act had been
--     exercised when it had not.
--
-- LEFT OUT BECAUSE THE CONTENT IS FIXED OUTSIDE THIS ACT:
--
--   s 15(2) -- security "in the manner prescribed for the time being in the
--     case of receivers appointed by the court", accounts passed "at such
--     periods as may be ordered", and a balance paid "in the manner
--     prescribed in the case of receivers". Three duties whose whole content
--     is elsewhere.
--   Every deferral to another instrument: the Criminal Procedure Code 2010
--     and the Family Justice Rules (s 5A(7)), section 47(1) of the Family
--     Justice Act 2014 (s 5A(8)), section 139A of the Women's Charter 1961
--     (s 5A(3)(c)(iv)), and the Rules of Court (s 21). Each is named where
--     it appears and none is modelled.
--
-- NOT MODELLED AT ALL:
--
--   THE AGE OF AN INFANT. See the header and the ambiguity register: the Act
--     defines "court" and nothing else, so the age enters as a parameter.
--   THE ORDER OF THE COURT AS A THING. An access order (s 5A), an order
--     under s 5, an order under s 7(4) and an order under s 19(1) share
--     nothing but the word. Each is modelled, where it is modelled at all,
--     by the facts the section reads off it.
--   MONEY ORDERED TO BE PAID BY A PARENT under s 7(4) or s 8(b). The amount
--     is the output of a discretion -- "as ... the court may consider
--     reasonable" -- so `family-domain`'s `A periodical sum` is used here
--     only for the s 18(1) cap, which measures a sum actually applied.


§§ `Ambiguities: where two readings of the text both survive`

-- Each entry names the provision, both readings, the one taken, and why.
-- These are the places a second formalizer could reasonably differ, and they
-- are the places to look first if this module and another disagree.
--
--  1. INFANT HAS NO AGE. s 2 defines only "court". Searching the extracted
--     source for the strings   years of age / majority / minor / attain /
--     21 years / 18 years   returns nothing, and the only periods
--     of time named anywhere in the Act are s 5A(3)(e)'s "12 months",
--     s 16(1)(b)'s "one year" and the "per month" of s 18(1)'s cap. The one
--     age the Act does name is not this one: s 11(b)'s "where the infant is
--     of an age to express an independent opinion", which is a capacity left
--     at large. (An earlier version of this entry also listed "age of" among
--     the strings that return nothing. It does not: "mortgage of" in s 18(2)
--     contains it. Corrected here and in the header, because a claim about a
--     grep is a claim a reader can run.) Readings: (a) the general age of majority in Singapore, which
--     is outside this Act; (b) 21, on the analogy of Wills Act 1838 s 4's
--     "under 21 years of age" (that phrase quoted from source/WA1838.txt,
--     not from this Act). TAKEN: neither. The age is a PARAMETER --
--     `the age of majority, in years` -- and the alternative route reads the
--     construed majority date `family-domain` already carries. Guessing a
--     number here would put a figure into the Act that is not in it.
--     WHAT THE NEIGHBOURING MODULES DO WITH THE SAME SILENCE, and what this
--     module's `NOTHING` becomes downstream, is entry 17 below.
--
--  2. s 6(1)-(2), "the guardian or guardians appointed by the father is or
--     are dead or refuses or refuse to act". Readings: (a) ALL of the
--     appointees are dead or refusing; (b) ANY one of them is. TAKEN: (a).
--     It makes the second sentence the exact complement of the first
--     ("either alone or jointly with any guardian appointed by the father"),
--     so the court's power arises precisely where the mother would otherwise
--     be guardian alone; on reading (b) the court could appoint a fourth
--     guardian alongside two who are still acting, which the words "to act
--     jointly with the mother" do not obviously contemplate.
--     THE FILE THAT SEPARATES THEM is `Wei Ming, the father having appointed
--     two guardians, one of them dead` -- two appointees of one parent, one
--     dead and one able and willing -- and it is the only one: everywhere
--     else a parent has at most one appointee, where ALL and ANY coincide.
--     Change `all` to `any` in the rule and exactly one assertion goes red.
--     A CONSEQUENCE OF READING (a) WORTH KNOWING: it makes the first limb of
--     the second sentence redundant, `all` over an empty list being TRUE. See
--     the note in place under s 6(1).
--
--  3. s 6(1)-(2), first sentence, "jointly with any guardian appointed by
--     the father". Readings: (a) literally every appointee, including one
--     who is dead or refuses; (b) only those able and willing to act.
--     TAKEN: (b). A dead guardian cannot act jointly with anybody, and the
--     second sentence exists to fill exactly that hole.
--
--     THE SAME READING IS NOW TAKEN FOR s 7(5), and it was not before. "Where
--     guardians are appointed by both parents, the guardians so appointed
--     shall after the death of the surviving parent act jointly" -- what the
--     subsection does with the appointments is make them act jointly, so an
--     appointee who cannot act cannot satisfy the gate either. An earlier
--     draft counted the appointments in s 7(5)'s gate and the appointees in
--     its answer, and so reported joint action by one man.
--
--     AND A THIRD PLACE, where the reading is more of a stretch and is worth
--     naming as such. `the guardians appointed by the father` drops a
--     guardian the court has REMOVED under s 10, and that list feeds the
--     second sentence's first limb, "When no guardian has been appointed by
--     the father". Read literally that limb is about the historical fact of
--     an appointment, and a guardian who was appointed and then removed does
--     not make it true that none was appointed. The reading taken is
--     purposive: after the removal the mother is guardian alone, which is
--     precisely the state the second sentence exists to relieve. Nothing much
--     turns on it, because s 10 gives the court its own power to "appoint
--     another guardian in his place"; but the two routes are different and a
--     reader should know which one this module reports.
--
--  4. s 7(3), the "unless" limb. Where the surviving parent objects, does
--     the appointee act ALONE, act SEPARATELY, or not act at all pending an
--     order? TAKEN: he does not act. s 7(4) says that if the court refuses
--     to make any order "the mother or father shall remain sole guardian",
--     and "remain" only makes sense if the parent was already sole. This is
--     the single most consequential reading in the module: it is what makes
--     `the persons who are guardians of the infant` return the mother alone
--     in the objection scenario.
--
--  4a. s 6(3), "no guardian of the person", where the only recorded guardian
--     of the person is DEAD, REFUSES TO ACT, or has been REMOVED. Readings:
--     (a) the limb asks who has been appointed, so any of the three still
--     counts and the gate stays shut; (b) the limb asks who is there, so none
--     of them counts and the gate opens. TAKEN: (b). Death and removal need
--     no argument -- a dead man is nobody's guardian, and s 10 ends the
--     guardianship of the one it removes. The refusal limb is the real
--     choice, and the Act supplies the reading: s 6(1)-(2) treat an appointee
--     who "is or are dead or refuses or refuse to act" as absent for exactly
--     this purpose, the purpose of deciding whether the court should step in
--     and supply somebody, and s 6(3) is a gate of the same character. On
--     reading (a) an infant whose only guardian had died would have no
--     guardian and no route to one, which cannot be right.
--
--     THE ENCODING GOES ONE STEP FURTHER and reads the limb through
--     `the standing of the guardian under this Act`, so that a testamentary
--     appointee whose appointing parent is still alive, and one the surviving
--     parent has objected to under s 7(3), are not counted either. Neither
--     case can arise inside s 6(3)'s own gate, which requires that the infant
--     have no parent; the reason for doing it anyway is that it makes this
--     answer and `the persons who are guardians of the infant` two readings
--     of one predicate over the guardian RECORDS.
--
--     THAT WAS ONCE STATED AS "AND SO INCAPABLE OF CONTRADICTING EACH OTHER",
--     AND IT WAS NOT TRUE, because the records are not the only guardians.
--     s 6 makes the surviving parent guardian by force of the section and
--     without any record, so a count that read records only could not see
--     her: on the plainest facts in the file -- the father dead, the mother
--     alive, nobody appointed -- the module said the guardians of this infant
--     were the mother, and that the infant had no guardian of his person. The
--     limb now has a second conjunct that reads the s 6 parent, so the two
--     answers agree on both routes and not merely on one; the claim is
--     recorded here in its corrected form because the false version was made
--     twice in this file and believed.
--
--     A SEPARATE READING RIDES ON THAT SECOND CONJUNCT and should be seen for
--     what it is: that a parent who is "guardian of the infant" under s 6 is
--     a guardian of his PERSON. s 6(1)-(2) make the survivor guardian of the
--     infant simpliciter and do not divide the office into person and
--     property, while s 6(3) asks after a guardian "of the person". Reading
--     the undivided office as including the person is the only reading that
--     makes s 6(3) work: on the other, an infant with a living mother would
--     have no guardian of his person and the court could appoint a stranger
--     over her head.
--
--  5. s 16(1)(a) versus (b). Limb (a) forbids parting with "the possession
--     of any of the movable or immovable property of the infant"; a lease
--     parts with possession. Readings: (a) a lease of a year or less is
--     still caught by limb (a), so limb (b) merely spells out the long-lease
--     case; (b) limb (b) is the specific provision for leases and displaces
--     limb (a) for them. TAKEN: (b) -- on reading (a) the words "for a term
--     exceeding one year" would do no work at all. Note that the encoding
--     leaves the choice visible: limb (a) is a recorded fact, so a case file
--     that records a short lease as a parting with possession will make the
--     prohibition bite.
--
--     A SIMPLIFICATION IN THE SAME SUBSECTION, recorded here because it sits
--     on the same (a)/(b) boundary. s 16(1) requires "the permission of the
--     court" for either limb, and the shared ontology carries ONE permission
--     flag for both, so a guardian permitted to grant a long lease under (b)
--     reads as permitted to sell under (a). s 16(3) tells against that: the
--     court "shall not make any order under subsection (1) unless it is
--     necessary or advisable in the interests of the infant", and necessity
--     is judged of the dealing proposed. Splitting the flag means adding a
--     field to `family-domain`, which three other Act modules build against,
--     so it is named rather than done. The error is permissive, so a case
--     file must not record the permission unless the court's permission in
--     fact covered the dealing in question.
--
--  6. s 16(1)(b), where the term of the lease is not in evidence. Readings:
--     (a) the prohibition bites, the guardian having to prove the term;
--     (b) it does not, the term being an element of the limb. TAKEN: (b).
--     What s 16 forbids is a lease of MORE than a year, and a lease of
--     unknown length has not been shown to be one.
--
--  7. s 16(3), "any order under subsection (1)". Subsection (1) mentions no
--     order -- only "the permission of the court". Readings: (a) the
--     permission is the order meant; (b) the reference is a slip for
--     subsection (2), whose order is the one to restore the property.
--     TAKEN: (a). It is the only thing subsection (1) lets the court do, and
--     reading (b) would leave subsection (1) with no constraint at all,
--     which is plainly not the point of a subsection headed by "shall not
--     make any order ... unless".
--
--  8. s 18(1), a weekly provision against a monthly cap. The Act supplies no
--     conversion. Readings: (a) the calendar year -- 52 weeks to 12 months,
--     so the multiplier is 52/12 and the cap is reached at $23.08 a week;
--     (b) four weeks to the month, so the cap is reached at $25.00 a week.
--     TAKEN: (a), as the reading that keeps a month a month. The difference
--     decides a weekly provision between $23.08 and $25.00, so it is not
--     academic. A provision on any OTHER period is not converted at all: the
--     verdict type has a third member that says so.
--
--  9. s 18(2), "insufficient for such purpose". Readings: (a) computable, by
--     comparing income with the provision; (b) construed, and supplied.
--     TAKEN: (b), and not by choice -- `family-domain`'s
--     `the income of the property of the infant` is a bare amount with no
--     period, while the provision is a rate, so the comparison cannot be
--     made. Recorded as a gap in the closing section.
--
-- 10. s 11(a), "where the infant has a parent or parents". Readings: (a) a
--     parent not in evidence is not counted; (b) the absence of a record is
--     not evidence of the absence of a parent, so the limb should be treated
--     as open. TAKEN: (a), following the discipline `family-domain` states
--     for s 6(3) -- a missing record never manufactures a fact, in either
--     direction. Note the two provisions cut opposite ways on the same
--     reading, which is why it is stated as a discipline and not as a
--     convenience: in s 6(3) it CLOSES a gate, and here it SHORTENS a list.
--
--     A SECOND, SEPARATE CHOICE IS TAKEN IN THE SAME LIMB and was not
--     recorded before: the parent must also be ALIVE. A dead parent's wishes
--     can be evidenced -- in a will, for instance -- so it is not obvious
--     that s 11(a) stops at the survivors. The reason for reading it that way
--     is internal consistency: s 6(3)'s "an infant has no parent" is answered
--     by both parents being dead, so within one Act "has a parent" would
--     otherwise be used in two different senses. Reading it the other way
--     would leave an orphan with a parent for s 11(a) and none for s 6(3).
--
-- 11. s 5A AND s 11A speak of "a child"; ss 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 11 and 12 to 21
--     speak of "infant" -- most of them as "an infant", but s 5 as "such
--     infant" and "the infant", s 11 as "the infant" throughout, and s 20 as
--     "the infant’s property"; ss 1, 2, 9 and 10 use neither word. (An earlier
--     version of this entry named s 5A as the only "child" section and gave
--     the "infant" range as "11-21", which both swallowed s 11A and claimed
--     ss 9 and 10 for a word neither of them uses.) Readings: (a) the
--     same class under two names; (b) two classes, "child" being undefined
--     here and possibly wider. TAKEN: neither. s 5A is encoded over its own
--     record and never over `Infant`, so the module does not assert an
--     identity the Act does not state.
--
--     s 11A IS THE DATAPOINT THAT BEARS ON THE CHOICE, which is why getting
--     it wrong mattered. It reads: "When considering any question relating to
--     the custody of any child, the court shall, whenever it is practicable,
--     have regard to the advice of a person, whether or not a public officer,
--     who is trained or experienced in child welfare but shall not be bound
--     to follow such advice." It sits immediately after s 11, whose own
--     subject is "the infant", and it governs the same custody question ss 3,
--     5 and 11 govern -- which is evidence for reading (a) that s 5A alone
--     could not supply, s 5A having a subject-matter of its own. Both "child"
--     sections are also the Act's only two lettered insertions: on the
--     source's own markers s 11A carries "[2/2011]", s 5A(6) carries
--     "[Act 3 of 2022 wef 02/01/2025]" and s 5A(7)-(8) carry
--     "[Act 18 of 2023 wef 02/01/2025]". So the shift is as likely to be the
--     drafting fashion of whoever wrote the insertions as a change of class.
--     It is still not enough to decide, and the module still does not.
--
-- 12. s 5A(4), "more access than what X is entitled to". No unit is given
--     for access. TAKEN: both quantities are in whatever unit the access
--     order itself uses, and only their comparison is encoded -- which is
--     all the subsection does.
--
-- 13. s 7(6), "a surviving parent". Where both parents are dead, which of
--     them survived is settled by comparing dates of death. Where a date is
--     not known, or both died on the same day, that comparison fails.
--     Readings: (a) fall back on some default; (b) say that it cannot be
--     determined. TAKEN: (b) -- the standing type has a member for exactly
--     this, and the alternative would be to invent a fact.
--
--     BUT WHAT CANNOT BE DETERMINED IS ONLY HALF THE SUBSECTION, and an
--     earlier draft said it was the whole. s 7(6) asks two questions: "he
--     shall continue to act as guardian after the death of the surviving
--     parent", and "but if the surviving parent has appointed a guardian, the
--     guardian appointed by the court shall act jointly with the guardian
--     appointed by the surviving parent". The first needs only that the
--     surviving parent be dead, and where BOTH parents are dead that is so
--     whichever of them survived. Only the second needs the survivor
--     identified. So the standing type now has a member that says he
--     continues to act and that whom he acts jointly with is open, and the
--     old member is kept for the case where a parent is not in evidence --
--     there, a living parent who objected under s 7(3) would stop him acting
--     at all, so "whether he acts" really is open.
--
-- 14. s 21, "Unless the court otherwise orders". The money sits in a
--     District Court, which s 2 excludes from "court". Readings: (a) "the
--     court" here is s 2's court, so the order comes from the General
--     Division or a Family Court; (b) it is the District Court holding the
--     money. TAKEN: (a) -- s 2 governs "In this Act, unless the context
--     otherwise requires", and s 21 gives no reason to depart. The encoding
--     does not turn on it: the fact recorded is that an order was made, not
--     which forum made it.
--
-- 15. ss 16(1) and 18, read together: may a guardian resort to CAPITAL of his
--     own motion? The Act nowhere says he may not. Readings: (a) he may not,
--     by expressio unius -- s 18(1) empowers him "out of the income of such
--     property" and s 18(2) puts capital in the hands of the court, so the
--     two subsections between them exhaust the routes; (b) s 18(2) merely
--     adds a court route without withdrawing anything, leaving s 16(1)'s
--     permission requirement to do all the work, on which reading a guardian
--     WITH the court's permission to sell could apply the proceeds himself.
--     TAKEN: (a), and the module states it as the constant
--     `the guardian may of his own motion make provision out of the capital
--     of the infant's property` MEANS FALSE. It is the only inference in this
--     module stated as a bare constant that the Act does not state in terms
--     -- the other constant, s 5A(5)(b)'s, is the subsection's own
--     parenthesis -- which is why it is entered here.
--
--     WHAT THIS IS NOT: it is not a claim that any answer depends on the
--     forum. s 3 reaches all three members, so the conjunct is a tautology in
--     the module as it stands. It earns its place by making the reading
--     checkable, not by discriminating.
--
-- 16. s 3, "Where in any proceedings before any court", against s 2's "In
--     this Act, unless the context otherwise requires, “court” means the
--     General Division of the High Court or a Family Court". Readings:
--     (a) s 2 governs, so s 3 binds only those two forums; (b) "any court" is
--     a context that otherwise requires, so s 3 binds every forum. TAKEN:
--     (b). Every other operative section says "the court"; s 3 is the one
--     that says "any court", and on reading (a) a District Court could decide
--     an infant's custody without regarding his welfare as paramount. This is
--     the only place in the module where the wider `Forum` type does
--     operative work -- s 5A(7) and s 21, the other two places the GIA names
--     a forum outside s 2, are both declined for other reasons. Note what
--     "operative" does and does not mean here: the reading is written into
--     the code and asserted over every member, but because s 3 reaches every
--     forum the ANSWER is the same for all three -- which is exactly the
--     point of reading (b), and is why the conjunct discriminates nothing.
--
-- 17. THE NOT-IN-EVIDENCE CASE: where the date on which a person attains the
--     age of majority is not recorded. Readings: (a) fold it to "not an
--     infant", so nothing in this Act operates on him until infancy is
--     proved; (b) fold it to "an infant", since the Act's provisions are
--     protective and the cost of a wrong answer is asymmetric; (c) refuse
--     both folds and report that the question cannot be determined. TAKEN:
--     (c), and `Whether this Act has an infant to govern` gives it a member
--     of its own so that a consumer cannot mistake it for either fold.
--     REASON: this Act supplies no age (entry 1), so there is no test here to
--     fail, and it contains no evidential provision -- nothing answering to
--     Probate and Administration Act 1934 s 6(3) -- to which the silence
--     could be routed. THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE FOUR MODULES GIVE ONE
--     QUESTION THREE SHAPES, and none of them said so before this round:
--     probate-administration-act.l4 folds to FALSE (its own ambiguity A2,
--     justified by s 6(3)); wills-act.l4 carries a distinct verdict for it
--     (its ambiguity A1) and computes from `date of birth` because Wills Act
--     1838 s 4 states a number; intestate-succession-act.l4 never asks. All
--     three are defensible and all three are now cross-referenced -- see
--     `The infant, and the age this Act does not fix` at the head of this
--     file, and join (2) in `Where this Act joins the other three`. Note what
--     is NOT in dispute: wherever this module and the Probate and
--     Administration Act module both give an answer they give the same one,
--     because both read the same shared field and compare it strictly.


§§ `Where the shared ontology stopped short`

-- Nouns and facts this Act needed that `family-domain.l4` does not carry.
-- MOST OF THESE ARE NOT DEFECTS in that module: they are GIA-only by its own
-- stated design, and they are recorded here so that a later editor asking why
-- something is declared twice gets an answer. TWO OF THEM ARE DIFFERENT, and
-- are marked as such where they appear -- the per-limb permission under
-- s 16(1), whose single flag is PERMISSIVE where it errs, and the want of any
-- field recording whether the instrument that appointed a guardian is good,
-- which is what stops this module taking a hand-off the Wills Act module
-- offers it. Neither is fixed here: three other Act modules build against
-- `family-domain.l4` and it has to stay still while they do. Both are
-- reported as domain gaps.
--
--   `Forum`. The GIA does operative work with a District Court (s 21,
--     s 5A(7)(a)) and names a Magistrate's Court (s 5A(7)(a)), and
--     `family-domain`'s `Court` deliberately excludes both, since s 2 does.
--     Its `The court that made the grant` widens the slot for GRANTS only,
--     which is a probate question. So the wider slot is declared here.
--     AND IT IS NOW THREADED THROUGH s 3: `section 3 governs the deciding of
--     the question` takes a `Forum`, because s 3 says "in any proceedings
--     before any court" and that is a context that "otherwise requires"
--     within s 2's own opening words. An earlier draft declared the type and
--     then consumed it nowhere but its own s 2 self-test, which left the
--     widest reading in the Act unstated.
--     BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THAT BUYS, because an earlier version of this
--     note said the type "does operative work" and a reader could take that
--     for more than it is. s 3's reach over forums is EXHAUSTIVE -- every
--     member answers TRUE -- so no answer anywhere turns on which forum it
--     is, and replacing the whole conjunct with TRUE would break nothing.
--     What was gained is that the exhaustiveness is written out member by
--     member and asserted member by member, so a reader can see the reading
--     was taken deliberately rather than by omission; make any member FALSE
--     and four assertions go red. That is the point, and it is the whole of
--     it. See ambiguity register entry 16.
--     WORTH SHARING: the PAA also needs a District Court (its s 35(1)), so
--     a general forum type is a candidate for the shared module if a second
--     Act ever needs one outside the grant context.
--
--   A PER-LIMB PERMISSION UNDER s 16(1). `Guardian` carries one
--     `has the permission of the court to deal with the property of the
--     infant`, but s 16(1) forbids two different things -- (a) parting with
--     possession and (b) a lease "for a term exceeding one year" -- and
--     requires "the permission of the court" for each. With one flag, a
--     guardian permitted to grant a long lease reads as permitted to sell.
--     s 16(3) is the reason that matters: the court "shall not make any order
--     under subsection (1) unless it is necessary or advisable in the
--     interests of the infant", and necessity is judged of the dealing
--     proposed. Not fixed here, because three other Act modules build against
--     that record; named here and in ambiguity register entry 5 instead. The
--     error is PERMISSIVE, which is the direction that matters for a case
--     file.
--
--   A PERIOD ON THE INCOME OF THE INFANT'S PROPERTY. `Infant`'s
--     `the income of the property of the infant` is a `Money` with no
--     period, but s 18(2) compares it with a provision that is a RATE. The
--     comparison cannot be made, so s 18(2)'s insufficiency limb is taken as
--     a construed input. `family-domain` argues at length, and rightly, that
--     an amount and a rate are different units; the same argument applies to
--     this field and it did not get the treatment.
--
--   THE ACCESS ORDER AND ITS BREACH (s 5A). Declared here as
--     `A breach of an access order`. Correctly GIA-only: no other Act in the
--     set has an access order, and `family-domain` says in terms that it
--     declares no order type because, as it puts it, the four Acts’ orders
--     share nothing except the word.
--
--   AN OCCASION ON WHICH JOINT GUARDIANS DISAGREE (s 8). Taken as a GIVEN.
--     It is a fact about an occasion, not about a person, and the shared
--     ontology records no occasions.
--
--   THE s 3 QUESTION, THE s 7(4) ORDER, THE s 9(1) APPLICATION TO ATTACH, THE
--     s 11 MATTERS, THE s 18(1) CAP VERDICT AND THE s 21 MONEY. All GIA-only
--     and all declared here.
--
--   A FIELD NO RULE OF THIS ACT READS. `Guardian` carries
--     `the reference for the infant`, and every fixture below sets it, but no
--     STATUTORY decision in this module tests it: every count and every
--     traversal above iterates `infant's `the guardians of the infant`` and
--     takes that list as authoritative. So a case file that put two infants'
--     guardians into one infant's list would have them all counted for that
--     infant, and no rule here would object. That is deliberate and is not a
--     defect in `family-domain`: the list is the ontology's own statement of
--     whose guardians these are, and the back-reference is provenance -- it
--     lets a reader of a `Guardian` record in isolation see which infant it
--     belongs to, which is what a record pulled out of a case file needs.
--     WHAT HAS CHANGED SINCE THIS NOTE WAS FIRST WRITTEN: the field is no
--     longer read NOWHERE. `every guardian recorded for this infant is
--     recorded against this infant`, in `The Act as a whole` above, reads it
--     as an INTEGRITY CHECK on a case file -- labelled as such, kept out of
--     the statutory sections, and asserted on a fixture built to fail it. The
--     statutory traversals are unchanged and still take the list as
--     authoritative, because that is the right reading of the ontology; what
--     the check adds is that a file whose two records disagree can now be
--     made to say so. `Grantee`'s `the reference for the person for whose use
--     and benefit the grant is made` is the third form this one link takes,
--     and probate-administration-act.l4's s 29(8)(a) uses the same shape on
--     it. See join (3) in `Where this Act joins the other three`.
--
--   NOTHING RECORDS WHETHER THE INSTRUMENT APPOINTING A GUARDIAN IS GOOD.
--     THE ONE GAP THIS ROUND FOUND THAT IS A REAL GAP. `How the guardian was
--     appointed` distinguishes an appointment by deed from one by will, and
--     Wills Act 1838 s 27(3) decides whether an appointment by will is "of
--     full force and effect" -- but no field on `Guardian`, and nothing on
--     `Infant`, carries that answer. The object that would, `Will`'s
--     `the guardians of infant children appointed by the will`, hangs off a
--     `Deceased person`, and no decision in this module takes one. So the
--     hand-off wills-act.l4 states at its s 27(3) is TYPED and cannot be
--     TAKEN from inside this file, and this module has to treat a
--     will-appointment as already construed. What would close it is a fact on
--     `Guardian` recording that the appointing instrument is good -- one
--     field, answered by Wills Act s 27(3) or s 6 for a will and by nothing
--     in this cleanroom for a deed. NOT ADDED HERE: three other Act modules
--     build against `family-domain.l4` and it must stay still while they do.
--     Reported as a domain gap, and named at s 7(1)-(2) above and in join (1)
--     of `Where this Act joins the other three`.
--
-- ONE POINT OF POSSIBLE CONFUSION, stated so that nobody resolves it the
-- wrong way. `Guardian`'s `alone or jointly` is an INPUT: it records what a
-- guardian is in fact doing, which is what s 8's "act as joint guardians"
-- asks. The decisions in ss 6 and 7 above COMPUTE what the Act requires. The
-- two are not the same question and neither is derived from the other -- a
-- guardian may be acting jointly when the Act says he should not be, and
-- that divergence is exactly what a s 7(4) or s 8 application is about.
